    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 7. SSH-Tunneled Remotes",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ53R011",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R012",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R013",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R014",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R015",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R016",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R017"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53G001",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R011/props/StringProp/HIWNVGSvT_9Q34O1",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R011/props/StringProp/uODagAFLjA26P-cT",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "TerminusDB remotes communicate over HTTP REST endpoints. Cross-machine communication between separate hosts utilizes SSH local port forwarding:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ53R012",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ53R012",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R011",
    "text": "**Transport Rationale**: Native remote commands (`push`/`pull`/`fetch`/`clone`) speak HTTP(S) only. TerminusDB itself listens on `0.0.0.0:6363` inside the container (confirmed via `/proc/net/tcp`) — the loopback-only restriction (`127.0.0.1:6363`) is this project's own `docker run -p 127.0.0.1:6363:6363` deployment choice, not a TerminusDB default. Either way, this project's containers are loopback-only, so cross-machine transport requires an SSH tunnel.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ53R013",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ53R013",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R011",
    "text": "**Tunnel Endpoint**: `ssh -N -f -L 0.0.0.0:6364:localhost:6363 <user>@<remote-host>`",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ53R014",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ53R014",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R011",
    "text": "**Firewall Access**: `sudo ufw allow from 172.17.0.0/16 to any port 6364 proto tcp`",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ53R015",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ53R015",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R011",
    "text": "**Bridge Addressing**: Remote URLs target the host's Docker bridge gateway (`http://172.17.0.1:6364/admin/aperas`).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ53R016",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ53R016",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R011",
    "text": "**Remote Tracking**: `fetch` updates local remote-tracking descriptors (`<org>/<db>/<remote>/branch/<branch>`). `fetch` must be executed before `push` or `pull` can resolve remote heads.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ53R017",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ53R017",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R011",
    "text": "---",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 8. Verification & Operational Utilities",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ53R018",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R019"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ53R019",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ53R019",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R01A",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R01B",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R01C"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R018",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R019/props/StringProp/7xRrOON-iqRdqRn7",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R019/props/StringProp/QCzH3tLEftqv9t59",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- **Verification Test Harness**: `npm run verify:phase0 -- --db` (`web/src/lib/verifyPhase0.ts`) — end-to-end, idempotent, self-cleaning live test: AST parsing, schema init, artifact track/ingest, GraphQL tree read, `FolderNode` ingestion, `Assertion` CRUD + WOQL impact propagation, and temporal commit management (branch + commit log). Demo-state cleanup has to happen in reference-reversed order — `FolderNode` before `ArtifactNode` before its `BlockNode`s — since TerminusDB enforces referential integrity and rejects deleting a still-referenced document.\n- **`Aperas/scripts/tdb-log.sh`**: Provides compact, local-time commit history (`--count 20` by default) with fast client-side message filtering (`--filter`). Avoids sluggish `tdb history` diffs.\n- **`Aperas/scripts/tdb-doc.sh`**: Wraps `tdb doc get` to convert ISO UTC timestamps to local time and unescape literal newlines (`\\n`) into readable multi-line prose.",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R01A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ53R01A",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ53R01A",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R019",
    "text": "**Verification Test Harness**: `npm run verify:phase0 -- --db` (`web/src/lib/verifyPhase0.ts`) — end-to-end, idempotent, self-cleaning live test: AST parsing, schema init, artifact track/ingest, GraphQL tree read, `FolderNode` ingestion, `Assertion` CRUD + WOQL impact propagation, and temporal commit management (branch + commit log). Demo-state cleanup has to happen in reference-reversed order — `FolderNode` before `ArtifactNode` before its `BlockNode`s — since TerminusDB enforces referential integrity and rejects deleting a still-referenced document.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R01B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ53R01B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ53R01B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R019",
    "text": "**`Aperas/scripts/tdb-log.sh`**: Provides compact, local-time commit history (`--count 20` by default) with fast client-side message filtering (`--filter`). Avoids sluggish `tdb history` diffs.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R01C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ53R01C",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ53R01C",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ53R019",
    "text": "**`Aperas/scripts/tdb-doc.sh`**: Wraps `tdb doc get` to convert ISO UTC timestamps to local time and unescape literal newlines (`\\n`) into readable multi-line prose.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Document Root",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G0000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0001"
    ],
    "parent": "ArtifactNode/00C7BJX360000",
    "type": "root",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Artifact Projection — Design",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G0001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0002",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0003",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0004",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0005",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000A",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000K"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0000",
    "text": "`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A names three projection modes that turn a stored\n`BlockNode` tree back into an output: Artifact Projection (serialize back to the physical\n`.md` file, always fully unfolded regardless of each block's `unfolded` state), the Human UI,\nand the Agentic BFS interface. This doc settles the design for the first of the three —\nArtifact Projection, the direct inverse of `astParser.ts`'s Markdown → tree transform. The\nother two modes are out of scope here. The design below is settled; nothing has been\nimplemented yet.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. Canonical regeneration, not byte-exact",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G0002",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0001",
    "text": "`heading`/`paragraph`/`code` blocks already round-trip exactly — their `title`/`text` are raw\nsource slices, not derived summaries. Container types (`list`, `listItem`, `blockquote`) are\nregenerated as clean, valid Markdown rather than reproducing the original file's exact\nwhitespace and spacing. This matches the bar reconciliation already uses elsewhere in this\nproject: \"parses back to the same tree,\" not \"identical bytes.\"",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. List items are always blank-line-separated in the output",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G0003",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0001",
    "text": "mdast's `spread` (tight vs. loose list) isn't captured. CommonMark requires blank lines\nbetween list items whenever any item is \"loose\" (multi-paragraph); always emitting them is\nvalid either way and has no effect on the re-parsed tree, since remark already wraps every\nlist item's content in a paragraph regardless of the original list's tightness (a pre-existing,\nseparately-confirmed quirk — not something this design introduces). Capturing `spread` would\nonly ever affect cosmetic rendering spacing, never structure, so it's left out.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. Blockquote line-prefixing is a projection-side concern",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G0004",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0001",
    "text": "CommonMark only requires the `> ` marker on a blockquote's first line (or after a blank line\nends and restarts the quote) — a continuation line needs no marker at all (\"lazy\ncontinuation\"). Whether `astParser.ts`'s raw-text slice for a multi-line paragraph inside a\n`blockquote` contains a stray `> ` on its continuation lines therefore depends on how the\nsource was actually written, not on anything the parser does: confirmed live, `\"> line\none\\n> line two\"` (marker repeated, common in practice) slices to `\"line one\\n> line two\"`\n(marker present in the leaf's own `text`), while `\"> line one\\nline two\"` (lazy continuation,\nalso valid) slices to `\"line one\\nline two\"` (nothing stray — matches mdast's own semantic\n`text.value` either way). Rather than depending on which form a given source used — or fixing\nthis at ingestion, which would perturb reconciliation's matching keys for existing blockquote\ncontent — the serializer normalizes at output time: strip any leading `> ` from each line of a\nblockquote's rendered content (a no-op when there isn't one), then re-apply `> ` uniformly.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 4. New fields: `ordered`, `start`, `checked` — captured, not derived",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G0005",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0006"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0001",
    "text": "A `list` node's numbered-vs-bulleted style and starting number, and a `listItem`'s task-list\ncheckbox state, aren't recoverable from anything currently stored — they have to be captured\nat parse time. mdast already carries all three directly on the relevant nodes (`list.ordered`,\n`list.start`, `listItem.checked`, confirmed live) — nothing lazy or derived about them, they\nsit on the same mdast node object `astParser.ts` already visits. They're absent from\n`ParsedBlockNode` today because `convertAstNode` was never asked to read them, not because\nthey were unavailable: it extracts `blockId`/`title`/`text`/`type`/`children` identically for\nevery node type and has never read a type-specific mdast property, since nothing downstream\nneeded one before projection made round-tripping a goal. So this is a scope gap in the\nexisting extraction logic, closed by capture — not inference of something the parser\npreviously discarded.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G0006",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G0006",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0007",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0008",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0009"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0005",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0006/props/StringProp/Ybk0Hg8EdEpzYZTf",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0006/props/StringProp/Yei_UAREPnIYjrXs",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "Schema (`BlockNode`, all `Optional` — unlike the `artifactId`/`type` migration, this needs no\nDB wipe, since existing documents remain valid against new optional fields):",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G0007",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G0007",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0006",
    "text": "`ordered: Optional xsd:boolean` — set on `list` nodes.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G0008",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G0008",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0006",
    "text": "`start: Optional xsd:integer` — set on `list` nodes (mdast always provides one, even for\n  unordered lists, where it's simply unused).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G0009",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G0009",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0006",
    "text": "`checked: Optional xsd:boolean` — set on `listItem` nodes only when mdast's own `checked` is\n  non-null; its absence (not `false`) is what distinguishes \"not a task item\" from \"unchecked.\"",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. The algorithm",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000A",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000B",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000C",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000F",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000G",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000H"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0001",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000A/props/StringProp/5ukvyouVjJDadskB",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000A/props/StringProp/jpOzQBgMO8ODm2Hg",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "Pure, recursive dispatch on `node.type`:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G000B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000A",
    "text": "**root** / any other container fallback: children rendered and joined with a blank line.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G000C",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000C",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000A",
    "text": "**heading**: `title` (already the full raw heading line, `#`s included) followed by its\n  rendered children, blank-line-joined.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G000D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000A",
    "text": "**paragraph**: `text`, dedented (every line but the first stripped of whatever common leading\n  whitespace they share — needed because a multi-line leaf's raw slice only has line 1's\n  container-required indentation stripped; every later line keeps its literal original-file\n  column, confirmed live on both a `blockquote` continuation line and a nested `code` fence's\n  interior — left alone, a container's own re-indentation (below) would compound on top of that\n  stale absolute value instead of establishing a clean baseline).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G000E",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000E",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000A",
    "text": "**code**: same dedenting, plus one more check — a *fenced* block's raw slice starts with its\n  own `` ``` ``/`~~~` marker, same as any other leaf, and is emitted as-is once dedented. An\n  *indented* (4-space) code block is also mdast type `code`, but its raw slice carries no fence\n  at all, and unlike every other leaf, line 1 isn't stripped of its marker either (confirmed\n  live — the indentation *is* the syntax marker, so it survives on every line including the\n  first). Left alone this would silently degrade into an ordinary paragraph on projection, so a\n  raw slice with no fence marker is dedented across all its lines (line 1 included) and\n  re-wrapped in a fresh generic fence — always converting indented-style input to fenced-style\n  output (§8).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G000F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000A",
    "text": "**blockquote**: render children, strip a leading `> ` from every line, re-prefix every line\n  with `> ` (§3).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G000G",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000G",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000A",
    "text": "**list**: render each child as a list item — marker is `${n}. ` if `ordered`, else `- `,\n  with `n` starting at `start ?? 1` — joined with a blank line (§2).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G000H",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000A",
    "text": "**list item**: prefix = marker + (`[x] ` / `[ ] ` if `checked` is defined), body = children\n  rendered and blank-line-joined, every line after the first indented to the prefix's width.\n  This is what makes a nested `list` under a `listItem` indent correctly — it falls out of the\n  recursion for free, no special-casing needed.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 6. Entry point",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000J",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0001",
    "text": "`projectArtifactToMarkdown(client, path)`: fetch the tree via the existing\n`getArtifactTreeViaGraphQL` (`graphql.ts` — its field selection needs `ordered`/`start`/\n`checked` added, the same fix already needed once this session for the `type` field), then run\nthe serializer over `tree.root`. Root itself is never emitted — it falls through the generic\ncontainer fallback, which is exactly \"join my children.\"",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 7. Verification strategy",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000K",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000M"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G0001",
    "text": "Round-trip equivalence — parse a sample exercising every branch (ordered list, unordered list\nwith a task item, nested list, blockquote, code, headings) → serialize → re-parse → compare —\nis checked by reusing `reconcile.ts`'s `reconcileTree` (the original parse as `oldRoot`, the\nre-parsed result as `newRoot`): equivalence is `stats.added === 0 && stats.removed === 0`,\ni.e. every block matches. This reuses already-verified matching logic as the equivalence check\ninstead of a bespoke tree comparator, and doubles as exercise of `reconcile.ts` against list/\ntask-list shapes it hasn't been run against yet.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G000M",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000M",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000N",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000P"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000K",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000M/props/StringProp/6vFiv3iRykzpCIbw",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000M/props/StringProp/pHuPTGe23v2ptzCP",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "Run against every real artifact in `AperasKG/artifacts/` (not just the synthetic sample): 9 of\n11 round-trip with zero added/removed. The other 2 each produce exactly one added+removed pair,\nboth explained by §1's canonical-not-byte-exact choice rather than a bug:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G000N",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000M",
    "text": "A nested `code` fence whose original *absolute* indentation was deeper than the minimal\n  amount its list item requires (source used 4 spaces where 2 would do) re-projects at the\n  canonical 2 — the code's own *relative* internal formatting is preserved, only the baseline\n  shifts, but that's still a different `text`, so exact-key Stage A correctly doesn't match it.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ6G000P",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ6G000P",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ6G000M",
    "text": "An indented-style code block converts to fenced-style on projection (§5) — a real, disclosed\n  change to the stored `text`, so it doesn't match either, for the same reason.\n  Both are accepted per the same \"precision over recall\" principle reconciliation already uses\n  elsewhere (§2 of the reconciliation design) — a wrong same-content guess is worse than a\n  visible remove+add.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Document Root",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR001"
    ],
    "parent": "ArtifactNode/00C7BJX3S0000",
    "type": "root",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Basic Assertion Authoring — Design",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR002",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR006",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR007",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00C",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00H",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00J"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR000",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 0. Why this moved out of Phase 2",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR002",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR003",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR004",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR005"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR001",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR002/props/StringProp/bubi9nXY1MOLkAj9",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR002/props/StringProp/j6aWFRq5wcqEgt6Z",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "The original roadmap (`Aperas-design.md`'s Development Roadmap) only ever named one assertion-\nrelated deliverable — Phase 2's \"graph search skill\" — and treated the Human UI (Phase 1) as\nthe thing that lets humans/agents see what's in the graph at all. Neither assumption survived\nPhase 0 finishing:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR003",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR003",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR002",
    "text": "`Assertion` (the concrete `BaseEdge`, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.C-D) has been a\n  fully working CRUD/WOQL primitive since Phase 0 — `insertAssertion`/`deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode`\n  (`crud.ts`), `queryNodeAssertions`/`traceImpactPropagation` (`woql.ts`) — but the *only* thing\n  that has ever called `insertAssertion` is a single hardcoded synthetic edge in\n  `verifyPhase0.ts`'s test harness. Nothing lets a human or agent write a real one.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR004",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR004",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR002",
    "text": "Artifact Projection (`Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md`) turned out to cover the\n  monitoring need the Human UI was assumed to be required for — `kg:project` already lets a\n  human read back exactly what's stored, without a SolidJS front end.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR005",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR005",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR002",
    "text": "So the basic ability to *write and inspect* a real `Assertion` — with no automated traversal,\nimpact-sweeping, or agent behavior attached — is built now, in Phase 0, so that Phase 1's\nskill-building work starts with it already available rather than blocked on it. Phase 2's\n\"graph search skill\" (`Aperas-design.md` §Development Roadmap, Phase 2) still owns everything\n*automated*: agents that traverse `affects`/`impacts` edges on their own initiative and flag\nstaleness. This doc only covers the manual primitive those agents would eventually build on.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. Scope: manual, not automated",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR006",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR001",
    "text": "This is a CLI-operated capability, not a skill that runs on its own. A human or an agent\nexplicitly invokes a command to assert one claim, or to look up what's already asserted about\na node. No code here decides *when* to assert something or walks the graph proactively — that\nremains Phase 2 territory, undesigned.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. Addressing is direct-or-query, not \"resolution\" or \"memory\"",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR007",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR008",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00B"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR008",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR008",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR009",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00A"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR007",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR008/props/StringProp/hly3FS_KxIl2l8ZR",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR008/props/StringProp/zsx1_4TlbcYenQVZ",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- **Direct addressing** — the normal case. You already hold a real node id, from wherever (a\n  prior command's own printed confirmation, a query result, code that still has the parsed\n  tree available). `AssertionInput.source`/`target` (`BlockNode/<id>`, `ArtifactNode/<id>`,\n  `FolderNode/<id>`) is passed through completely unchanged — no lookup, no search, nothing to\n  resolve.\n- **Query-based addressing** — applies when there's no id in hand at all. The simplest query\n  this project has is an exact match on a path, and both path-addressable node kinds get it\n  identically: a bare artifact path (e.g. `Aperas-design.md`) resolves via the existing\n  `getArtifactRecord` (`artifacts.ts`) to `ArtifactNode/<artifactId>`, and a bare folder path\n  (e.g. `.` for the artifacts root, or a subdirectory name) resolves via the structurally\n  identical `getFolderRecord` (`folders.ts`) to `FolderNode/<folderId>`. An artifact path and a\n  folder path never collide, so trying artifact-then-folder resolution in that order is\n  unambiguous. (Earlier revisions of this doc called this \"from memory\" — that framing\n  conflated an ordinary exact-match query with a distinct human-recall mechanism; see\n  `Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md` §1 for the fuller correction and the complete\n  inventory of what query tools this project actually has.)",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR009",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR009",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR008",
    "text": "**Direct addressing** — the normal case. You already hold a real node id, from wherever (a\n  prior command's own printed confirmation, a query result, code that still has the parsed\n  tree available). `AssertionInput.source`/`target` (`BlockNode/<id>`, `ArtifactNode/<id>`,\n  `FolderNode/<id>`) is passed through completely unchanged — no lookup, no search, nothing to\n  resolve.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR00A",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00A",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR008",
    "text": "**Query-based addressing** — applies when there's no id in hand at all. The simplest query\n  this project has is an exact match on a path, and both path-addressable node kinds get it\n  identically: a bare artifact path (e.g. `Aperas-design.md`) resolves via the existing\n  `getArtifactRecord` (`artifacts.ts`) to `ArtifactNode/<artifactId>`, and a bare folder path\n  (e.g. `.` for the artifacts root, or a subdirectory name) resolves via the structurally\n  identical `getFolderRecord` (`folders.ts`) to `FolderNode/<folderId>`. An artifact path and a\n  folder path never collide, so trying artifact-then-folder resolution in that order is\n  unambiguous. (Earlier revisions of this doc called this \"from memory\" — that framing\n  conflated an ordinary exact-match query with a distinct human-recall mechanism; see\n  `Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md` §1 for the fuller correction and the complete\n  inventory of what query tools this project actually has.)",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR00B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR007",
    "text": "`BlockNode` has no path-like shortcut: nobody carries an opaque id in their head or types block\ncontent as a handle. So a block-level assertion needs either an id already in hand from\nwhatever produced the claim (e.g. a skill that just parsed content and still holds the tree), or\na real query tool to find one — see `Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md` (`kg:tree`,\n`kg:expand`, `kg:search`), which closes this gap three ways: a structural map, a one-level\ncontent peek, and keyword/regex search. (Superseded reasoning, kept for context: this doc\noriginally argued no such command was needed in Phase 0, since nothing yet created a situation\nrequiring one — that held until the need was recognized as a set of general-purpose query\ntools in their own right, not just an assertion helper.)",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. Commands (`kgCli.ts`)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00C",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00D"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR00D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00D",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00F",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00G"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00C",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00D/props/StringProp/SPgAzOrYc7ZMpi6Q",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00D/props/StringProp/zoTbXZOljMf3pWMD",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- `kg:assert <source> <predicate> <target>` — `source`/`target` each accept a full node id\n  (direct addressing) or a bare artifact/folder path (query-based addressing, per §2); calls\n  `insertAssertion`.\n- `kg:assertions <node>` — same addressing as above, calls `queryNodeAssertions`, prints each\n  as `<direction>  <predicate>  <otherNodeId>`.\n- `kg:unassert <source> <predicate> <target>` — same addressing, deletes exactly the matching\n  assertion(s), not every assertion touching a node. `deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode` (existing,\n  `crud.ts`) is intentionally blunt — it exists for demo/reset cleanup, where wiping everything\n  touching one node is correct. A real \"undo this claim\" command needs a narrower\n  `deleteAssertion(client, source, predicate, target)`: filter `Assertion` docs on all three\n  fields (not just one), delete only those. New function, `crud.ts`.",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR00E",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00E",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00D",
    "text": "`kg:assert <source> <predicate> <target>` — `source`/`target` each accept a full node id\n  (direct addressing) or a bare artifact/folder path (query-based addressing, per §2); calls\n  `insertAssertion`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR00F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00D",
    "text": "`kg:assertions <node>` — same addressing as above, calls `queryNodeAssertions`, prints each\n  as `<direction>  <predicate>  <otherNodeId>`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR00G",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00G",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00D",
    "text": "`kg:unassert <source> <predicate> <target>` — same addressing, deletes exactly the matching\n  assertion(s), not every assertion touching a node. `deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode` (existing,\n  `crud.ts`) is intentionally blunt — it exists for demo/reset cleanup, where wiping everything\n  touching one node is correct. A real \"undo this claim\" command needs a narrower\n  `deleteAssertion(client, source, predicate, target)`: filter `Assertion` docs on all three\n  fields (not just one), delete only those. New function, `crud.ts`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 4. Real bug found by live testing (fixed)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR001",
    "text": "`kg:assertions` initially printed the predicate as `[object Object]`. WOQL binds a node-typed\nfield (`source`/`target` — `BaseNode`) as a plain id string, but a literal-typed field\n(`predicate` — `xsd:string`) as a wrapped `{\"@type\": \"xsd:string\", \"@value\": \"...\"}` object —\nconfirmed live by querying directly. `traceImpactPropagation`'s existing test coverage never\ncaught this because it only checks binding *presence*/*membership* (`AffectedNode`, itself\nnode-typed), never a literal field's actual value. Fixed in `woql.ts`'s `queryNodeAssertions`\nwith a small `unwrapLiteral` helper applied to `predicate` in both directions.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. Out of scope",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00J",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00K"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR00K",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00K",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00M",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00N",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00P"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00J",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00K/props/StringProp/90sRiAKvuqYy1ptm",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00K/props/StringProp/PzhgAHo-pR1o-ihz",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- Any automated creation or traversal (Phase 2's graph search skill).\n- Per-asserter branch placement — `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`'s \"Author-Based Placement\"\n  (§ Design Rationale) describes assertions eventually living in the asserting agent's own\n  branch/artifact so they don't require mutating someone else's. That's a multi-agent-branching\n  concern (Phase 3 territory); today, same as every other write in this codebase, an `Assertion`\n  simply commits to whatever branch the client is already pointed at (`main`). Not addressed\n  here.\n- Restricting `predicate` to a fixed vocabulary — stays free text, same as `insertAssertion`\n  today; the known examples (`impacts`/`verifies`/`derived_from`/`affects`) remain\n  conventions, not an enum.",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR00M",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00M",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00K",
    "text": "Any automated creation or traversal (Phase 2's graph search skill).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR00N",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00K",
    "text": "Per-asserter branch placement — `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`'s \"Author-Based Placement\"\n  (§ Design Rationale) describes assertions eventually living in the asserting agent's own\n  branch/artifact so they don't require mutating someone else's. That's a multi-agent-branching\n  concern (Phase 3 territory); today, same as every other write in this codebase, an `Assertion`\n  simply commits to whatever branch the client is already pointed at (`main`). Not addressed\n  here.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ7PR00P",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ7PR00P",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ7PR00K",
    "text": "Restricting `predicate` to a fixed vocabulary — stays free text, same as `insertAssertion`\n  today; the known examples (`impacts`/`verifies`/`derived_from`/`affects`) remain\n  conventions, not an enum.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Document Root",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8001"
    ],
    "parent": "ArtifactNode/00C7BJHG3R000",
    "type": "root",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Aperas Core Ontology Design",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8002",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8011",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801C",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8020",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802E"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8000",
    "text": "This document defines the core semantic graph ontology for Aperas. It establishes mathematically rigorous identity, strong polymorphic typing, and universal addressability for all graph components.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. Node Identity vs. Content Fingerprint",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8002",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8003",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800C"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8001",
    "text": "Addressable components (e.g., `BlockNode`) require stable identities that survive re-ingestion. Because identity must remain constant across edits while content-change detection must reflect every edit, these concerns are handled by two separate fields (see Appendix F for why a single-hash design failed).",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### A. Node Identity (Stable, Content-Independent)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8003",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8004",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8005",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8006",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8007",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800B"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8002",
    "text": "A `BlockNode`'s `@id` is a **Snowflake-style generated identifier**. It is assigned once at creation and is fully decoupled from the node's content and position, ensuring that `BaseLink` and `BaseEdge` assertions survive edits to their referenced nodes.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8004",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8004",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8003",
    "text": "**64-bit Layout**: Encoded as a 13-character Crockford Base32 string. It is compact and lexicographically sortable by creation time.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8005",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8005",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8003",
    "text": "| Field | Bits | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Sign** | 1 | Unused. Keeps value a positive signed int64 for cross-language compatibility. |\n| **Timestamp** | 45 | Milliseconds since custom epoch (e.g., 2025-01-01) — provides ~1,115 years of range. |\n| **Machine ID** | 10 | 1 anomaly-flag bit + 9 machine-number bits — supports 512 machines with personal failover identities. |\n| **Sequence** | 8 | Per-machine-identity counter, reset each millisecond — allows 256 ids/ms/machine. |",
    "type": "table",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8006",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8006",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8003",
    "text": "**Deterministic Collision Avoidance**: \nCollisions are prevented by construction rather than probability. Timestamps separate different times, the sequence counter disambiguates same-millisecond generation on one machine, and the machine ID separates different machines.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8007",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8007",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8008",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8009",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800A"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8003",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8007/props/StringProp/9QFFvQfhmPPjWBwG",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8007/props/StringProp/DAfhDs6GtCwRvFQb",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**Handling Local Clock Anomalies (NTP corrections)**:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8008",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8008",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8007",
    "text": "**Prevention**: NTP uses gradual \"slew\" adjustments instead of \"step\" (instant jumps).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8009",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8009",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8007",
    "text": "**Absorption**: The generator uses a high-water-mark (`max(now, last_emitted)`). Small backward jumps are absorbed by the sequence counter.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800A",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800A",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8007",
    "text": "**Failover**: If a backward jump is too large (risking counter overflow), the generator flips its anomaly bit to use a distinct failover machine ID, preventing collisions with its normal output.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8003",
    "text": "*(Note: While extreme, unbounded backward jumps under sustained load remain theoretically possible, they require deliberate clock tampering.)*",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### B. Content Fingerprint & Reconciliation",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800C",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800G",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800H"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8002",
    "text": "Once a `BlockNode` tree is ingested into the database, **the DB becomes the source of truth**. Edits are made directly in the DB, and artifact files are regenerated (projected) from it. Re-ingestion is a **fallback reconciliation path**, triggered only when an external hand-edit causes the file to drift from its projected state.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800D",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800F"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800C",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800D/props/StringProp/1MD_-Zeq2GiFiM_T",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800D/props/StringProp/zxtaABs2AEtt0s92",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**Minimal Fingerprinting on `ArtifactNode`**:\nOnly two flat, file-level hashes are maintained (no structural hashes):",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800E",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800E",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800D",
    "text": "**`fileHash`**: Hash of the raw file's current content, recomputed on every `track` event.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800D",
    "text": "**`ingestedHash`**: Hash of the raw file's content as of the last confirmed sync (ingestion or projection).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800G",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800G",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800C",
    "text": "**Reconciliation Trigger**: If `fileHash !== ingestedHash`, an unwitnessed edit occurred outside the DB, triggering reconciliation.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800H",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800C",
    "text": "**DB-to-File Changes**: No hash is needed for DB updates. Because DB writes are always witnessed, the system can mark the corresponding artifact as needing re-projection within the same transaction.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. The Universal Node-Link-Edge Lineage",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800J",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800K",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800N",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800S",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800X",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800Z"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8001",
    "text": "The core graph is built on a clean, fractal inheritance lineage. Every relationship is a fully addressable, first-class Document.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### A. BaseNode (The Root)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800K",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800M"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800J",
    "text": "The abstract root of all addressable content (inherited by `BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`, `FolderNode`). It explicitly owns an array of its intrinsic links.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800M",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800M",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800K",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"BaseNode\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@abstract\": [],\n  \"links\": { \"@type\": \"Set\", \"@class\": \"BaseLink\" }\n}\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### B. BaseLink (Intrinsic Connections)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800N",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800P",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800Q",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800R"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800J",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800N/props/StringProp/CzMtmV1780fgWrQP",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800N/props/StringProp/ICpfCcuT4bWjWtOM",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "An abstract class representing connections authored physically *inside* a source node (e.g., Markdown hyperlinks).",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800P",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800P",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800N",
    "text": "**Inheritance**: Inherits from `BaseNode`. (This means links themselves possess a `links` array, allowing arbitrary depth for provenance or comments).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800Q",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800Q",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800N",
    "text": "**Implicit Source**: Because a `BaseLink` resides within the `BaseNode.links` array, the node holding the array is implicitly the source.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800R",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800R",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800N",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"BaseLink\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@abstract\": [],\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseNode\"],\n  \"target\": \"BaseNode\",\n  \"predicate\": \"xsd:string\"\n}\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### C. BaseEdge (Extrinsic Assertions)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800S",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800T",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800V",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800W"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800J",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800S/props/StringProp/FLXbqHlaDZ6ZYduq",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800S/props/StringProp/Vdq_OCmFsqg5R5Ji",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "An abstract class representing independent semantic claims asserted from the outside (e.g., an AI Agent deducing a relationship).",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800T",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800T",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800S",
    "text": "**Inheritance**: Inherits from `BaseLink`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800V",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800V",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800S",
    "text": "**Explicit Source**: Because it floats independently of any node's `links` array, it adds a `source` pointer to explicitly declare its origin.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800W",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800W",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800S",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"BaseEdge\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@abstract\": [],\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseLink\"],\n  \"source\": \"BaseNode\"\n}\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### D. Assertion (The Concrete Extrinsic Edge)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800X",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800Y"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800J",
    "text": "`BaseEdge` is abstract, so it can never be instantiated directly — something concrete has to exist to actually write an extrinsic claim (`impacts`, `verifies`, `derived_from`, `affects`, ...). `Assertion` is that one generic leaf: it adds no fields of its own, just makes the `source`/`predicate`/`target` (+ inherited `links`) lineage instantiable.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y800Y",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800Y",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800X",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"Assertion\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseEdge\"]\n}\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### E. Link (The Concrete Intrinsic Edge)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y800Z",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8010"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800J",
    "text": "`BaseLink` is abstract too — the same gap as `BaseEdge`/`Assertion`, just noticed later, once `BlockNode.links` extraction (Aperas-markdown-fractal-mapping-design.md §4) actually needed to write one. `Link` is `BaseLink`'s one concrete leaf: no fields of its own, just makes the `target`/`predicate` (+ inherited `links`) lineage instantiable. Used for an inline Markdown link whose target resolves to an internal node (`[title]([[code]])`) — the `predicate` is always the fixed constant `\"references\"` for this use, distinguishing structural inline links from `Assertion`'s deliberately-chosen semantic predicates at query time.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8010",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8010",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y800Z",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"Link\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseLink\"]\n}\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. Universal Addressability & Ownership",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8011",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8012",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8015"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8012",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8012",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8013",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8014"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8011",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8012/props/StringProp/6A-7YCSo4iZxF26T",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8012/props/StringProp/SCMslq-qtSLG-QWD",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "*   **No Subdocuments**: `BaseLink` and `BaseEdge` are standard TerminusDB Classes, *not* `@subdocument`s. Every relationship possesses a global `@id`, enabling graph reification (assertions about assertions).\n*   **Author-Based Placement**: Extrinsic assertions (`BaseEdge`) are never embedded into the nodes they connect. They are stored in the branch or artifact owned by the **Asserter** (the author). This ensures that agents can assert claims about human-authored nodes without mutating the human's artifact.",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8013",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8013",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8012",
    "text": "**No Subdocuments**: `BaseLink` and `BaseEdge` are standard TerminusDB Classes, *not* `@subdocument`s. Every relationship possesses a global `@id`, enabling graph reification (assertions about assertions).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8014",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8014",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8012",
    "text": "**Author-Based Placement**: Extrinsic assertions (`BaseEdge`) are never embedded into the nodes they connect. They are stored in the branch or artifact owned by the **Asserter** (the author). This ensures that agents can assert claims about human-authored nodes without mutating the human's artifact.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### A. Native Backlinks & Bidirectional Traversal — only for `Set`-typed fields, not `List`",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8015",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8016"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8011",
    "text": "Because TerminusDB is built on RDF triples under the hood, `Set`-typed fields — `BaseNode.links:\nSet<BaseLink>`, `BaseEdge.source`/`target: BaseNode` — really do produce one direct triple per\nvalue, so a generic `t(X, Predicate, Target)` query genuinely finds anything pointing at a target\nthrough one of those, no custom backlink table needed. **Confirmed live**:\n`t(X, 'links', 'Link/y8LuGtix5YFbBn6Y')` correctly returned the owning `BlockNode` as `X`.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8016",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8016",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8017",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8019"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8015",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8016/props/StringProp/OaKbzO7v7XUKBtDN",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8016/props/StringProp/ZGXbdfZwcMs4-hIh",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "That does **not** extend to `List`-typed fields — and the tree's actual containment structure\n(`BlockNode.children`, `ArtifactNode.root`, `FolderNode.children`) is `List`, not `Set`,\ndeliberately: child order is semantically meaningful (document/section order; `project.ts`'s\nserializer depends on it) and `Set` is unordered. TerminusDB represents `List` membership through\nintermediate cons-cell/RDF-list structure at the triple level, not a direct `Parent predicate\nChild` triple. **Confirmed live**: `t(X, 'children', '<a known child's id>')` returned zero\nbindings, even though that id is genuinely present in its parent's `children` list. So \"upward\nbreadcrumbs\" and \"universal backlink tracing\" *do* work for `links` (item 1's own example happens\nto pick a `Set` field, so it was never actually wrong) but do **not** give you a node's structural\nparent, or an id→path conversion, for free — that would need either a schema change (a persisted\n`parentId`/back-reference field on `BlockNode`, the standard trade of write-time bookkeeping for\nO(1) reverse lookup) or an explicit downward search from a known root, not a generic triple query.\nNot built as of this writing — see `Aperas-deep-path-resolution-design.md`, which covers the\n*other* direction (path → id) only; id → path remains a real, separate, unbuilt gap.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8017",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8017",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8018"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8016",
    "text": "**Upward Breadcrumbs via a `Set` field (Context Retrieval)**:\n   Because `BaseLink` is a fully addressable Document, it does not need a physical `parent`\n   pointer for this specific case. If a user addresses a `Link`/`BaseLink` directly\n   (`Link/123`), they can follow the breadcrumbs \"upward\" to find the parent context, since\n   `links` is `Set`-typed:",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8018",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8018",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8017",
    "text": "```javascript\n   // What ParentNode has a 'links' property pointing to me?\n   WOQL.triple(\"v:ParentNode\", \"links\", \"Link/123\")\n   ```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8019",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8019",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801A",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801B"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8016",
    "text": "**Backlink Tracing, scoped to `Set`-typed edges (Logseq/Roam style)**:\n   You can instantly query the graph for anything pointing to a target *through a `Set`-typed\n   field* — `links`, or `Assertion.source`/`target` — regardless of the predicate:",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801A",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801A",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8019",
    "text": "```javascript\n   // What nodes point to this block via a Set-typed field, and via what relationship?\n   WOQL.triple(\"v:SourceNode\", \"v:Predicate\", \"BlockNode/MyTargetBlock\")\n   ```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8019",
    "text": "This returns every intrinsic `BaseLink`/`Link` or extrinsic `BaseEdge`/`Assertion` referencing\n   the target — but never a structural parent, since `children`/`root` aren't reachable this way.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 4. The Unbounded Block Tree (Node Typology)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801C",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801K",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801P"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8001",
    "text": "The graph topology models all content as an unbounded fractal tree spanning across folders, files, and blocks — \"unbounded\" in the same sense **Apeiron** (§Philosophy) names the unconditioned, boundless substrate: no fixed depth limit, not merely \"very large.\" The rigid boundaries between structural layers are eliminated to form a single continuous tree.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801C",
    "text": "> **Recurring pitfall when designing tools against this ontology**: reaching for \"file\" as a unit\n> of scope. A path like `foo.md` names one `ArtifactNode` — a single node in the same uniform\n> tree as every `FolderNode`/`BlockNode` around it, not a bounded container with its own\n> traversal rules. There is no \"whole file\" tier to recurse into or out of; a command that walks\n> \"everything under `<path>`\" is doing exactly the same walk whether `<path>` resolves to a\n> `BlockNode`, an `ArtifactNode`, or a `FolderNode` — see `kg:tree`'s `childRefs`/`printTree`\n> (`kgCli.ts:47-51,59`), which already recurses through all three with no kind-based branching.\n> Any new command's scoping flags should be designed against depth/traversal-cost concerns (e.g.\n> \"should this default to walking descendants at all,\" per\n> `Aperas-interactive-summarization-design.md` §3/§7), never against a file/folder/block\n> distinction — that distinction doesn't exist here, and designing as if it does is the mistake\n> to catch early.",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### A. FolderNode (The Structural Container)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801E",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801F",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801G",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801H",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801J"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801C",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801E/props/StringProp/4Oj3D2xwh80ss3_J",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801E/props/StringProp/pPUF9Ocvr0AQJjgW",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "Inherits from `BaseNode`. Represents a directory in the filesystem, acting as a seamless bridge in the unbounded tree.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801E",
    "text": "**`README.md` Ingestion**: A separate `README.md` file is a legacy \"dumb folder\" workaround. The `README.md` is fully absorbed into the `FolderNode`. Its content populates the folder's `text` (abstract) and initial `children` blocks. The `README.md` is not exposed as a separate `ArtifactNode`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801G",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801G",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801E",
    "text": "**`children`**: Contains other nested `FolderNode`s and `ArtifactNode`s, alongside the parsed block children from the `README.md`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801H",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801E",
    "text": "**Identity**: a Snowflake-style generated id, same scheme as `BlockNode` (§1.A); `path` is a plain, mutable field, not part of the key (Appendix G).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801J",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801J",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801E",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"FolderNode\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseNode\"],\n  \"title\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"path\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"text\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:string\" },\n  \"children\": { \"@type\": \"List\", \"@class\": \"BaseNode\" }\n}\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### B. ArtifactNode (The Physical Anchor)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801K",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801M",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801N"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801C",
    "text": "Represents the physical file on disk. It handles file metadata and holds a single pointer to the root of the block tree. Inherits from `BaseNode` — without this, no `BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` could target or originate from a whole file, contradicting §3's universal-addressability goal.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801M",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801M",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801K",
    "text": "**Identity**: Snowflake-generated, same as `FolderNode` above; `path` is a plain, mutable field, not the key (Appendix G). Gains `title`/`text` to match the folding philosophy (§5) uniformly across the whole fractal lineage: `title` is the filename, `text` is an abstract of the file's own content (naive fallback — first paragraph — until AI-driven summarization, §5 enhancement backlog, actually exists).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801N",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801K",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"ArtifactNode\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseNode\"],\n  \"title\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"path\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"text\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:string\" },\n  \"fileHash\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"lastTrackedAt\": \"xsd:dateTime\",\n  \"ingestedHash\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:string\" },\n  \"lastIngestedAt\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:dateTime\" },\n  \"root\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"BlockNode\" }\n}\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### C. BlockNode (The Fractal Atom)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801P",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801Q",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801T",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801V",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801W",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801Z"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801C",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801P/props/StringProp/ShpeP5w8TvEej823",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801P/props/StringProp/tIobSvH49a3X7bxk",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "Inherits from `BaseNode`. Every distinct piece of content is a `BlockNode`. The schema is driven by the philosophy of **Abstraction by Folding**: a block's children fold into its `text` (abstract), which folds into a `title`, which folds into a hidden `@id`.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801Q",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801Q",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801R",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801S"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801P",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801Q/props/StringProp/Su3BsK0jGW3u0h9U",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801Q/props/StringProp/Y-Tk43dVbmVMtHvU",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**`title` (The Semantic Label)**: Used for wikilinks and graph queries.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801R",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801R",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801Q",
    "text": "For direct UI input: The author is prompted to provide a title.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801S",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801S",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801Q",
    "text": "For Markdown ingestion: The parser uses an AI agent to summarize the block into a title. If AI is unavailable, it falls back to the block ID.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801T",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801T",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801P",
    "text": "**`text` (The Abstract/Body)**: The actual content shown in the current document.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801V",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801V",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801P",
    "text": "**`children`**: An ordered `List` of child `BlockNode`s.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801W",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801W",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801X",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801Y"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801P",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801W/props/StringProp/h9zlf4ZhpP_kjmPM",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801W/props/StringProp/wF4e4fn2gP8LkeDP",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**`unfolded`**: A boolean flag representing the persistent view-state for UIs and Agents.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801X",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801X",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801W",
    "text": "**Human UI & Agent Interface**: When rendering to a screen or an AI prompt, the graph defaults to *folded* (`false`) to manage cognitive/token overload. Only the current block's `text` and the children's `title`s are shown. If this flag is `true`, the subtree auto-expands on load.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801Y",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801Y",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801W",
    "text": "**Artifact Projection**: When syncing to a physical `.md` file, the tree is ALWAYS 100% unfolded (writing the full document to disk). This flag is ignored.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y801Z",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y801Z",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y801P",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"BlockNode\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseNode\"],\n  \"title\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"text\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:string\" },\n  \"children\": { \"@type\": \"List\", \"@class\": \"BlockNode\" },\n  \"unfolded\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:boolean\" }\n}\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8020",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. The Folding Philosophy & Graph Traversal",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8020",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8021",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8025"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8001",
    "text": "The entire architecture is governed by the philosophy of **Abstraction by Folding**: an unbounded subtree folds into an abstract (`text`), which folds into a semantic label (`title`), which folds into a hidden `@id`.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8021",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### A. The Three Projection Modes",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8021",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8022",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8023",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8024"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8020",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8021/props/StringProp/MPrMUIDNJfDGtZGa",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8021/props/StringProp/d_BuGn0mOI2ZaBbs",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      }
    ],
    "text": "How this folded state is handled depends strictly on the interaction interface:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8022",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8022",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8022",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8021",
    "text": "**Artifact Projection (File Syncing)**: When the system serializes a graph back to a physical `.md` file, the tree is ALWAYS 100% unfolded. The persistent `unfolded` state is ignored, as the physical file must contain the entire document body. For `FolderNode`s, the block children are serialized back out to the folder's `README.md`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8023",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8023",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8023",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8021",
    "text": "**Human UI (Phase 1 Web App)**: The UI is folded by default and traverses seamlessly across folder/file boundaries. Users see the `text` (abstract) of the current node (`FolderNode`, `ArtifactNode`, or `BlockNode`) and the abstracts of its direct children. Expanding a `FolderNode` reveals child files/folders and its own `README.md` blocks inline without context switching.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8024",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8024",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8024",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8021",
    "text": "**Agentic Interface (BFS Traversal)**: AI agents interact with the graph exactly like the Human UI to manage token limitations. They receive folded views and can navigate continuously from a workspace root `FolderNode` down to a deeply nested `BlockNode` using BFS tool calls to expand subtrees.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8025",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### B. Link Targeting & \"Hover Previews\"",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8025",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8026",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8027",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8028",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8029",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802C",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802D"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8020",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8025/props/StringProp/Oav8ClAnCK8ScNxI",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8025/props/StringProp/vwA2sfmWslmIpu03",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "We distinguish between **Intrinsic Links** (`BaseNode.links`) and **Extrinsic Edges** (`BaseEdge`):",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8026",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8026",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8026",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8025",
    "text": "**Intrinsic Links (`links`)**: Links and metadata (tags, properties) authored directly inside the node. Stored in `BaseNode.links`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8027",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8027",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8027",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8025",
    "text": "**Extrinsic Edges (`BaseEdge`)**: Independent assertions created by external authors/agents. They float independently (to avoid mutating the source node) and are discovered via WOQL queries.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8028",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8028",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8028",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8025",
    "text": "**UI Display**: The UI displays non-inline metadata/properties from `links` and queried extrinsic `BaseEdge`s in dedicated reference panels. Inline links remain rendered naturally inside the `text` body.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8029",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y8029",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y8029",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802A",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802B"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8025",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8029/props/StringProp/6ZqQfmEYJctj8ZYK",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8029/props/StringProp/dkp6XPWnRwEcD1sD",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**The Preview Mechanism**:",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y802A",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802A",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8029",
    "text": "**Human UI**: Humans receive the preview interactively by **hovering** over a link or wikilink in the UI, popping up the target block's abstract (`text`).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y802B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8029",
    "text": "**Agent Interface**: Agents receive the target block's abstract (`text`) embedded directly in the `links` list returned by the projection/tool call, providing instant preview context without requiring UI hover triggers.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y802C",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802C",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8025",
    "text": "**Manual Jump**: If the user or agent needs more context than the target's abstract, they manually \"jump\" to the target block to explore its children.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y802D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8025",
    "text": "---",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Appendix: Design History & Rationale",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802E",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802F",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802G",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802H",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802K",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802M",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802Q"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y8001",
    "text": "This section archives the original problems that necessitated this design.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### A. The Positional Identity Flaw",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802E",
    "text": "In Phase 0, `BlockNode` IDs were positional (`doc1_block_1`). If an author inserted a paragraph at the top of a document, all subsequent blocks shifted their IDs. This silently broke or orphaned any semantic edges pointing to those blocks upon re-ingestion. Moving to **Content-Addressed Identity** resolves this — though content-addressing itself was later found to have its own problems and was superseded; see Appendix F.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### B. The `xsd:string` Anti-Pattern",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802G",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802E",
    "text": "Initially, relationships defined `subjectId` and `objectId` as `xsd:string`. This treated relationships as flat text rather than true graph edges, destroying referential integrity. Introducing `BaseNode` as a polymorphic type target resolves this, forcing all edges to point to actual documents in the database.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### C. The Rejection of Subdocuments",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802E",
    "text": "TerminusDB's `@subdocument` feature was initially considered for `BaseNode.links` to tightly couple intrinsic links to their parent. However, subdocuments lose their global `@id`. In Aperas, every entity—even a single parsed link or punctuation mark—must be universally addressable so that external agents can comment on or refute it. Thus, subdocuments were rejected, and `BaseLink` was promoted to a full Document class.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### D. Resolving Provenance",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802J",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802E",
    "text": "Initially, `provenance` was modeled as a hardcoded string or dedicated context object on an edge. By making `BaseLink` inherit from `BaseNode`, every link gets its own `links` array. Provenance is now simply achieved by pushing a new `BaseLink` (e.g., `predicate: \"asserted_by\"`) into the parent link's array, unifying the ontology completely.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### E. The Unbounded Block Tree",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802K",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802E",
    "text": "Initially, the schema enforced a rigid structural hierarchy (`DocumentNode` -> `BlockNode`). However, this rigid \"solid\" paradigm was abandoned in favor of a fractal, Logseq-inspired block tree. `DocumentNode` was eliminated entirely, as a document is conceptually just a root `BlockNode`. Flattening the graph to Markdown is now handled cleanly via an `unfolded` flag in the projection layer.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### F. Separating Identity from Content Fingerprint",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802M",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802N",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802P"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802E",
    "text": "The content-addressed scheme from Appendix A (`@id = hash(parent context + content)`) conflated two requirements that pull in opposite directions: identity needs to stay constant across edits and moves so external references don't break; a content fingerprint needs to change whenever content changes, for dedup and change-detection. A single content-derived hash cannot satisfy both. Concretely, it broke two ways: (1) two independently-authored blocks with coincidentally identical text collapsed onto the same `@id`, silently merging distinct entities and their assertions; (2) because the hash was computed bottom-up through the tree, any edit to a leaf changed the hash of every ancestor up to the artifact root, orphaning assertions on whole sections for a single-word edit anywhere beneath them.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y802N",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802M",
    "text": "Resolved by decoupling the two entirely: `@id` is now a Snowflake-style generated identifier, assigned once and fully independent of content (§1.A) — collision-avoidance is deterministic (timestamp + machine identity + sequence counter), not derived from what the node contains. The content fingerprint (§1.B) turned out not to need a recursive/structural hash at all.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y802P",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802P",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802M",
    "text": "A first attempt at §1.B proposed reusing the bottom-up construction from this section — a recursive, per-`BlockNode` `treeHash` — for three things: detecting the DB-ahead-of-file case (by comparing it against `fileHash`), scoping reconciliation to only the diverged subtrees, and cross-checking the identity-matcher's decisions. The first use rested on a category error: `fileHash` hashes a flat string and a tree fingerprint hashes a nested structure — outputs of different functions over different domains, never meaningfully comparable regardless of whether real drift exists. Once that's gone, the DB-ahead-of-file case is better solved by direct on-write signaling instead (§1.B), and the remaining two uses (reconciliation scoping, cross-check) didn't justify a persisted, per-edit-maintained field on their own — reconciliation is rare and artifacts are bounded, human-document-sized trees, so a best-effort matcher can simply run over the whole tree each time without a hash-based pre-filter. Dropped entirely, not kept even as an on-demand technique — leaving two flat file-domain hashes (`fileHash`/`ingestedHash`) as the whole of the fingerprint design, and reconciliation itself resolved at the strategy level as best-effort content matching over the whole tree, with the specific matching algorithm left as an implementation detail.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### G. Separating Artifact/Folder Identity from Location",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802Q",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802R",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802S",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802T"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802E",
    "text": "`ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` were originally Lexical-keyed on `path` — not a deliberated design decision, just the obvious, simple choice made in an early, undesigned prototyping pass, before this document's identity/fingerprint rigor (§1, Appendix F) existed at all.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y802R",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802R",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802Q",
    "text": "On inspection, while designing reconciliation matching (see `Aperas-reconciliation-matching-design.md`), this turned out to be the same category error Appendix F already fixed once for `BlockNode`, just on a different axis: identity conflated with a mutable property. There, the mutable property was content; here, it's location. A file or folder rename is a routine edit, exactly like a paragraph edit, not an identity change — but `path`-as-`@id` makes every rename destructive by construction, since the `@id` is *recomputed* from the new path, orphaning the old document's identity and everything that ever referenced it directly. The only way to route around that self-inflicted damage is to detect the rename after the fact and manually transplant `root` onto a freshly-identified document — real, working, but entirely unnecessary machinery, built to compensate for the identity scheme rather than because the problem demanded it.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y802S",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802S",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802Q",
    "text": "Resolved the same way as Appendix F: `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` both get a Snowflake-generated identity (§1.A), and `path` becomes an ordinary mutable field. A detected rename (matched by content, same as everything else — see §4.A/B's `title`/`text` and the reconciliation doc's file/folder-matching design) becomes a field update on the same document, not a new identity; anything that referenced the artifact or folder by id keeps working with no transplant needed. `root` only ever needed transplanting because the container around it couldn't survive a move — fix the container, and the transplant machinery becomes unnecessary for this case (still needed for the case where a match genuinely fails and a new identity is warranted).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZ8Y802T",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZ8Y802T",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZ8Y802Q",
    "text": "Two costs were weighed before committing to this: losing the free, deterministic `id`-from-`path` lookup, and losing the database-enforced guarantee that no two documents can claim the same path. The first turned out not to be a cost at all — benchmarked, a `path`-filtered query came back faster than the direct id lookup it would replace (~7.5ms vs ~48ms at 50 documents). The second is real in shape (an application-level check-then-upsert race could in principle create duplicate `path`s) but not practically significant given how this project is actually deployed — one local TerminusDB per machine, reconciled via fetch/push, not a single server multiple machines write into concurrently; worth remembering if that deployment model ever changes.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Document Root",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX0000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0001"
    ],
    "parent": "ArtifactNode/00C7BJHG40000",
    "type": "root",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Deep Path Resolution & `kg:resolve` — Design",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX0001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0002",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0003",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8004",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800G",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Q",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8011",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8014",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8019",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802A"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0000",
    "text": "**Status**: both directions are **implemented and live-verified** — `kg:path` (id→path, §8) and\n`kg:resolve` (path→id, §1-7, including `--base`, `--create-holder`, and the `kg:tree`/`kg:search`\nbackport in §7.5) — via `nodeRef.ts`'s `resolveNodeRefDetail`/`resolveNodeRefOrNull`.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 0. What this is",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX0002",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0001",
    "text": "Path resolution is fundamentally one operation: converting a relative, human-typed path into an\nabsolute node code (a full `BlockNode/ArtifactNode/FolderNode` id) — the \"query-based addressing\"\ntier `nodeRef.ts` already provides (`Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md` §2). Today that\nconversion stops at the file/folder boundary: `getArtifactRecord`/`getFolderRecord` resolve a\npath to an `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`, and nothing carries a path further into that artifact's\nown block tree. This extends the same conversion one more tier — `folder/file.md/heading/title`\n— and gives it a command of its own, since nothing currently exposes resolution directly; every\nexisting caller only ever sees it as a side effect of some other command (`kg:assert`, `kg:tree`,\n...).",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. Command",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX0003",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8000",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8001",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8002",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8003"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8000",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8000",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0003",
    "text": "```\nkg:resolve [--base <path>] <path> [<path>...]\nkg:resolve [--base <path>] --create-holder <path> --titles <title> [<title>...]\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8001",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8001",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0003",
    "text": "The first form resolves each `<path>` through every tier (`Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`\n§2, extended by §3-4 below) and prints one line per input, in order — id, kind, and title, same\none-line shape `kg:tree --depth 0` already uses. Exits non-zero with a clear reason on a miss\n(unresolvable prefix, or an ambiguous segment — §4).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8002",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8002",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0003",
    "text": "Multiple `<path>` arguments are accepted specifically so `--base` (§2.1) pulls its weight:\nresolving many paths against the same base in one call, rather than re-resolving the base from\nscratch — and re-typing it — for each one.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8003",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8003",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0003",
    "text": "The second form is `--create-holder` (§7): single-path only, and shaped differently on purpose —\nsee §7.1 for why it needs its own `--titles` list rather than reusing `<path>`'s own segments.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. Path grammar",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8004",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8005",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8006",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8007",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8008",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8009",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800A"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0001",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8004/props/StringProp/pt-joCfJ5nC_1aAN",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8004/props/StringProp/ttKQjHrOETucc7G6",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "`/`-separated segments, Linux-flavored:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8005",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8005",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8004",
    "text": "**Leading `/`** → absolute: anchored at the tracked artifacts root, ignoring any `--base`\n  (§2.1) in effect. This is also what every path meant before `--base` existed — the only\n  grammar there was, so nothing about existing (bare, no-`--base`) callers changes.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8006",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8006",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8004",
    "text": "**No leading `/`** → relative to the base in effect: `--base <path>` if given, the implicit\n  base (§2.1) for callers that have one (wikilink extraction), or the artifacts root by default —\n  which is exactly today's behavior, so a bare path with no `--base` in play resolves exactly as\n  it always has.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8007",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8007",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8004",
    "text": "**`.`** → the base itself. Mostly useful as `kg:resolve --base <path> .` to print what a base\n  resolved to.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8008",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8008",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8004",
    "text": "**`..`** → the base's own parent, one level up (§2.1's walk mechanics) — chainable (`../..`)\n  like any real filesystem.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8009",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8009",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8004",
    "text": "No `#`-anchor syntax, no special marker splitting \"file part\" from \"block part\" beyond the above:\nper the unbounded-tree principle (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §4), there's no structural\nboundary between a folder segment, a file segment, and a heading segment to mark differently in\nthe first place — `/`, `.`, and `..` are the only reserved tokens, and each means the same thing\nat every tier. (A `BlockNode` genuinely titled `.` or `..` becomes unaddressable by title at that\nposition — the same trade a real filesystem already makes.)",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 2.1 `--base` and walking `..`",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800A",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800B",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800C",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800F"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8004",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800A/props/StringProp/VBFwUpF_kfluJrYC",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800A/props/StringProp/cN-rJLIEvB-jEHS0",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "`--base <path>` resolves first, through the full algorithm (§3-4), then each subsequent `<path>`\nargument is resolved relative to that node. `..` moves up one level from wherever the walk\ncurrently stands:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800A",
    "text": "Within a `BlockNode` tree, `..` reads `.parent` (§8's field) — the same hop `resolveIdToPath`\n  already walks, just in reverse.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800C",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800C",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800A",
    "text": "At the artifact/folder tier, `BlockNode.parent` doesn't apply — `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`\n  carry no `.parent` field, only a stored `path` string (§3). `..` there trims the last\n  `/`-segment off that string and re-resolves it via `getFolderRecord` — a property of the path,\n  not a field on the node, same as an ordinary filesystem.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800A",
    "text": "`..` past the artifacts root is a miss (\"already at root\"), not a silent no-op.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800E",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800E",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800A",
    "text": "The base supplies the \"which artifact, which subtree\" that every relative path would otherwise\nhave to repeat: rather than typing `sub/file.md/Heading/Sub A` and `sub/file.md/Heading/Sub B` in\nfull, `kg:resolve --base sub/file.md/Heading \"Sub A\" \"Sub B\"` says the shared prefix once.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800A",
    "text": "Callers that don't go through the CLI at all — wikilink extraction, primarily (`astParser.ts`'s\n`resolveBlockLinks`) — get an *implicit* base: the path of the block containing the\n`[[wikilink]]` text, not the path of that block's owning artifact. A `[[wikilink]]` is written\nfrom a specific place in the tree, and the most-likely-intended target is judged relative to that\nplace — the same way a relative filesystem path is judged relative to the file that contains it,\nnot to some project root; `[[../Sibling Section]]` written inside one heading's subtree to reach\na sibling heading is exactly the case this exists for. Getting \"the containing block's own path\"\nis itself `resolveIdToPath` (§8) — so the wikilink extractor ends up a consumer of both\ndirections of this design, not just the forward one.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. Segmentation algorithm",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800G",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800H",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800K",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800M",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800N",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800P"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0001",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800G/props/StringProp/2ItVhgGbdRS2ezgL",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800G/props/StringProp/Dh5y6I2dtdh3dnvY",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "The stored `path` field on `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` is one exact-match string\n(`artifacts.ts:235`, `folders.ts:141` — `query: { path: ... }`), not resolved segment-by-segment,\nso a multi-segment input like `sub/file.md/Some Heading` can't be split up front — `sub/file.md`\nis itself one atomic stored path. Resolve by longest-prefix search instead:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800H",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800G",
    "text": "Split the input on `/` into segments `[s0, s1, ..., sN]`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800J",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800J",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800G",
    "text": "Try progressively shorter prefixes, longest first: `s0/../sN`, then `s0/../s(N-1)`, ... down to\n   `s0` alone. For each candidate prefix, try `getArtifactRecord` then `getFolderRecord` (same\n   try-both-they-never-collide order every other caller already uses).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800K",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800K",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800G",
    "text": "The first prefix that resolves is the base node; every segment *after* it is a slug segment to\n   descend through the base node's block tree, one `BlockNode` child per segment (§4).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800M",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800M",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800G",
    "text": "No prefix resolves at all → the existing \"not a tracked artifact or folder path\" miss.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800N",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800G",
    "text": "This is a pure extension: an input with no trailing slug segments behaves exactly as today\n(longest-prefix search immediately finds the whole thing, zero slug segments to descend).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800P",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800P",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800G",
    "text": "**Confirmed live**: the synthetic `\"Document Root\"` wrapper every artifact's tree starts with\n(astParser.ts's `convertAstNode`, `type: 'root'`) is a real, addressable segment here, not skipped\n— an `ArtifactNode`'s one descendable child (§4) is that wrapper, not the real first heading\nunderneath it. This matches `resolveIdToPath` (§8), which already walks through it as an ordinary\nhop, so a full path from `kg:path` and one typed into `kg:resolve` round-trip consistently — e.g.\n`file.md/document-root/-my-heading`, not `file.md/-my-heading`. A `FolderNode` has no such\nwrapper (its children — README content and nested folders/artifacts alike — sit directly in its\nown `children` list), so this only ever applies one hop deep, right after an `ArtifactNode`.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 4. Descending slug segments",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800Q",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800R",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800S",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800T",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Y",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Z",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8010"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0001",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Q/props/StringProp/5bpwfnD3axQ4suIo",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Q/props/StringProp/VmMV1gR0QhaclZwo",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "For each remaining segment, filter the current node's direct `children` to `BlockNode`s whose\n`title`, slugified (§5), matches the segment slugified (§5 applied to both sides, so a caller can\ntype either raw heading text or an already-slugified form and it matches either way). Matching is\ntwo-tier, exact before prefix — the same precedence a short git hash resolves under (a full hash\nthat happens to also be a prefix of some other object's hash still just names the object it\nexactly matches):",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800R",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800R",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Q",
    "text": "**Exact**: candidates whose slugified title equals the segment exactly. Exactly one → descend,\n   done — a longer sibling that happens to start with the same text never interferes.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800S",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800S",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Q",
    "text": "**Prefix**, tried only if step 1 found none: candidates whose slugified title *starts with* the\n   segment. Lets a caller type `setup` for a child titled `Setup Guide` without knowing the rest,\n   the same convenience `git show a1b2c3` gets from a hash prefix — as long as it's the *only*\n   sibling starting that way.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800T",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800T",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800V",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800W",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800X"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Q",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800T/props/StringProp/A5BAyOMyq_cWMBFG",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800T/props/StringProp/evpXX0XbHNuF-UaR",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "At either tier:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800V",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800V",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800T",
    "text": "**Zero matches** → miss, same as an unresolvable prefix.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800W",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800W",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800T",
    "text": "**Exactly one match** → descend, continue to the next segment.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800X",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800X",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800T",
    "text": "**More than one match** → decline rather than guess (the same principle `reconcile.ts`'s\n  matching already follows for ambiguous content) — fail with an error naming the id and title of\n  every sibling that matched, rather than silently picking one. Two siblings with identical titles\n  fail at step 1 without ever falling through to step 2 — prefix matching resolves an\n  *abbreviation*, it never papers over a genuine duplicate.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800Y",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800Y",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Q",
    "text": "This matches on *any* `BlockNode.title` uniformly, not just headings — a non-heading block titled\nvia `kg:title` is addressable by that title the same way a heading is, consistent with this\nproject's repeated \"don't special-case by node kind without a real reason\" rule\n(`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §4's pitfall note; `Aperas-interactive-summarization-design.md`\n§5).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX800Z",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX800Z",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Q",
    "text": "**Real bug found live during implementation**: a heading's stored `title` keeps its raw `#`/`##`\nmarker (astParser.ts's convention), which §5's blanket slugify rule turns into *leading dashes* —\nharmless for exact matching (both sides get the same treatment) but fatal for prefix matching as\nfirst implemented: `## Setup Guide` slugifies to `---setup-guide`, so typing `setup` (a caller has\nno reason to know or type the heading's own depth) never matched via plain `startsWith`, making\nthe whole prefix tier dead on arrival for the dominant addressable-content case. Fixed by stripping\nleading dashes off the *candidate* before the `startsWith` check, prefix tier only — exact matching\nis untouched.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8010",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8010",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX800Q",
    "text": "**Reserved tokens apply per-segment, not just to a whole `--base`**: before either tier, a segment\nthat is exactly `.` or `..` is never looked up as a title at all — it's the structural move §2.1\nalready defines (stay, or go to `.parent`), wherever it appears in the path, not only when typed\nalone or as `--base`. `Heading A/../Heading B` mid-path works the same way `../` does at the very\nfront. A real `BlockNode` titled `.` or `..` is simply unreachable via that segment position (§2's\nfootnote) — one more instance of the trade a real filesystem already makes.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. Slugify",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8011",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8012",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8013"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8012",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8012",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8011",
    "text": "```\nslugify(text) = text.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, '-')\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8013",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8013",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8011",
    "text": "One rule, deliberately not collapsing runs or trimming edges: every individual non-alphanumeric\ncharacter becomes its own `-`, preserving exact length/position rather than folding different\ninputs toward the same output — maximal distinction over cosmetic prettiness. No special-casing\nfor a heading's leading `#`/`##` marker either; under this blanket rule it's just another\ncharacter that becomes a `-` like any other, no AST-awareness needed. Applied to both the\ncandidate `BlockNode.title` and the query segment before comparing, so a `kg:title`-set title\nwith no markdown syntax at all and a raw heading title match on equal footing — e.g. a raw\nheading title (`\"# Interactive Title & Cross-Link Authoring — Design\"`) slugifies to\n`-interactive-title---cross-link-authoring---design`, hyphen-for-character, no cleanup.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 6. Where this plugs in",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8014",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8015",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8016",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8017",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8018"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0001",
    "text": "**The shared primitive**: `nodeRef.ts`'s `resolveNodeRefOrNull(client, path)` gains an options\nparameter — `resolveNodeRefOrNull(client, path, opts?: { base?: string; createHolder?: boolean;\ntitles?: string[] })` — carrying §2's grammar (`/`, `.`, `..`), §3's longest-prefix search, §4's\nexact-then-prefix descent, and §7's holder creation, all in one place, added to the function's\nexisting tier-3 path fallback.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8015",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8015",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8014",
    "text": "Every command that already resolves a `<path>` argument through the plain form —\n`kg:assert`/`kg:assertions`/`kg:unassert`, `kg:tree`, `kg:unfold`/`kg:fold`, `kg:title`, `kg:link`\n— gains deep addressing (§3-4) for free, with no `opts` of their own to supply; none of them need\n`--base` or `--create-holder`, so `opts` simply stays empty for those calls.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8016",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8016",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8014",
    "text": "**`kg:resolve`** is the new command that exposes this directly, for inspection or scripting,\nrather than only as a side effect of some other operation — the CLI-level home for `--base` and\n`--create-holder` (§1), which are nothing more than `opts.base`/`opts.createHolder`/`opts.titles`\nparsed off `process.argv`.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8017",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8017",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8014",
    "text": "**The wikilink extractor** (`astParser.ts`'s `resolveBlockLinks`) calls `resolveNodeRefOrNull`\ndirectly, bypassing the CLI entirely — it already has everything `opts` needs without prompting\nanyone: `opts.base` from the containing block's own path (via `resolveIdToPath`, §8),\n`opts.createHolder: true` unconditionally (§7.3), and `opts.titles` from splitting the wikilink's\nraw text on `/` (§7.1) — one source feeding both the matching path and the holder titles.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8018",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8018",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8014",
    "text": "**Not touched**: `kg:project` (`kgCli.ts:233-242`) calls `getArtifactRecord`/`getFolderRecord`\ndirectly rather than through `resolveNodeRefOrNull`, because it needs the record itself (to know\nthe on-disk target file path to write), not just a resolved id. No change needed there — it never\naddresses into the block tree, only ever the artifact/folder itself.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 7. Holder nodes — resolving a miss into a placeholder, not just an error",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8019",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801A",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801M",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801T",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801Z",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8022"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0001",
    "text": "§4's \"decline rather than guess\" covers *ambiguity*. A miss is different: the path is\nunambiguous, there's just nothing there yet. Declining is still the right default — most callers\n(`kg:assert`, a human typing `kg:resolve` at a prompt) want a clear error, not a surprise node\nappearing in the tree. But two real callers want the opposite: a way to *mark* the still-missing\ntarget so something later can find and fill it in, rather than losing the reference entirely.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 7.1 What gets created, and why `--titles` is separate from `<path>`",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801A",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801B",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801C",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801H",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801K"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8019",
    "text": "`--create-holder` creates one node per unresolved segment, continuing to descend into each\nnewly-created one for whatever remains — `mkdir -p`, not just the last component. Segments that\nalready resolve are left untouched; only the miss point onward gets created, `holder: true` set\non each. Every holder `BlockNode` created this way is `type: 'heading'` — not a default among\nseveral possible shapes, the only one supported; see §7.4 for why, and for the one thing it rules\nout.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801A",
    "text": "`<path>` is still what does the *matching* (§4, prefix included) — but §4's prefix matching means\na segment on the way down may be a deliberate abbreviation (`setup` addressing `Setup Guide`), and\nusing that abbreviation verbatim as a new node's permanent title would be wrong: a holder is new,\nreal, lasting content, not a lookup key. So `--create-holder` requires a parallel `--titles` list —\nbut aligned from the *tail*, not the head, and scoped to only the segments that can even use one.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801C",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801C",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801A",
    "text": "**Which segments need a title at all**: only the heading-tier ones (§4's slug-descent). The\nartifact/folder tier (§3) never does — `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode.title` isn't a matching key or\nan authored value anywhere in this codebase, it's a pure derivation from the path segment itself\n(`trackArtifact`, `artifacts.ts:127`: `title: basename(artifactPath)`; the folder-tracking\nequivalent, `folders.ts:44`: `relPath.split('/').pop()!` — both recomputed unconditionally on\nevery track/rebuild; the real match key is `path`, looked up exactly). A holder `ArtifactNode`/\n`FolderNode` gets that same derived title immediately, the moment it's created, with nothing to\nsupply — and when the real file is eventually tracked, `basename` overwrites it again regardless,\nso there was never anything worth preserving there to begin with. `.`/`..` tokens (§2) don't need\none either — they never look anything up.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801A",
    "text": "**Alignment, tail-first**: `--titles` lines up against the heading-tier segment sequence `S`\n(§4's segments only — base's own trailing heading segments too, if `--base` reaches that far,\n§7.2) from the *end*: the last title pairs with the deepest segment, the second-to-last with the\nnext one up, and so on, for as many titles as given. Segments shallower than the shortest prefix\n`--titles` reaches are assumed to already exist — the caller only supplies titles for as deep as\nit actually expects to be creating, not one for every segment regardless of need. Resolution still\ntries the normal match (§4) at *every* segment first, title-covered or not: a title-covered\nsegment that turns out to already exist just leaves that title unused, same as before; an\nuncovered (shallower) segment that unexpectedly misses is a plain error — \"would need to be\ncreated, no title available for it\" — the same \"decline rather than guess\" answer as any other\nmiss, `--create-holder` or not. `--titles` longer than `S` is a usage error up front (extra titles\nwith nothing left to pair against); shorter is fine by construction — that's the whole point.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801E",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801E",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801F",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801G"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801A",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801E/props/StringProp/7YRhnQwQCkl5yBIZ",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801E/props/StringProp/iD_O82XKMp9zjvHJ",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "Both callers this exists for already have the full titles on hand for free, so this isn't the\nceremony it looks like at the call site:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801E",
    "text": "**The wikilink extractor** already has the wikilink's raw, unslugified text — splitting it on\n  `/` gives both `<path>` (via §5's slugify, for matching) and `--titles` (that same split, used\n  as-is) from one source, no separate authoring step.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801G",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801G",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801E",
    "text": "**A \"vision drawer\"** (§7.3) is, by definition, working from real intended titles — that's the\n  content it's sketching, not an afterthought.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801H",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801A",
    "text": "A direct human `kg:resolve --create-holder` call still has to type both `<path>` and `--titles`,\nbut tail-alignment keeps it to just the segments actually being created — no need to restate\ntitles for a path prefix that already resolves.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801J",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801J",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801A",
    "text": "**Real bug found live**: `ArtifactNode`'s one \"child\" is its singular `root` field, not a\n`children` List — the first implementation always wrote `children: [...]` regardless of the\ncurrent node's kind, which is fine for `BlockNode`/`FolderNode` but fails schema check\n(`unknown_property_for_type`) the moment a miss needs a holder created directly under an\n`ArtifactNode` (reachable via `..` popping up past a block tree into its owning artifact — §2.1).\nFixed by branching on kind: an `ArtifactNode` with no `root` yet gets the holder set as `root`\ndirectly; one that already has a `root` declines instead (there's no structural slot for a second,\nsibling root — a real edit adds *inside* the existing root, not beside it).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801K",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801K",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801A",
    "text": "**Output**: one line per segment, not just the final id — §1's plain form only ever prints the\nend result because nothing changed along the way, but `--create-holder` can touch several levels\nat once, and which ones were already there versus newly imagined is exactly what a caller needs to\nsee. Each line carries the same `<id>  [<kind>]  <title>` shape as everywhere else, tagged\n`(existing)` or `(created holder)`.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 7.2 How far \"imagined\" reaches: the whole tree, not just blocks",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801M",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801N",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801P",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801Q",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801R",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801S"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8019",
    "text": "Holder creation isn't bounded to the `BlockNode` tier. If §3's longest-prefix search fails\noutright — no tracked `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` matches any prefix at all — `--create-holder`\nextends the same `mkdir -p` logic up through that tier too: a whole path, folders and file and\nheadings alike, can be sketched into existence with nothing on disk yet. This is what lets a\n\"vision drawer\" (§7.3) actually draw a *tree*, not just fill gaps inside artifacts that already\nexist.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801N",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801M",
    "text": "Disambiguating which imagined segments are `FolderNode`s versus the one `ArtifactNode` versus\n`BlockNode`s beneath it reuses the convention every real path in this project already follows: the\nsegment carrying this project's file extension (`.md`) is the `ArtifactNode`; segments before it\nare `FolderNode`s; segments after it are `BlockNode`s. No new marker syntax — one already exists\nin every path anyone types here.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801P",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801P",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801M",
    "text": "A holder `ArtifactNode` has no real file on disk and no content hash; it's a placeholder for a\nfile that will eventually be written and tracked (`kg:track`+`kg:ingest`) the normal way. Because\n`FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode` need the same `holder` marker `BlockNode` does, it belongs on\n`BaseNode` rather than repeated per concrete kind — the same \"don't special-case by node kind\"\ndefault §4 already follows for title-addressing.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801Q",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801Q",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801M",
    "text": "`--base` composes with `--create-holder` the same way as any other segment: if the base path\nitself doesn't resolve, it's imagined too, root included — being \"the base\" doesn't make it any\nless eligible than a segment reached by descending into it. No separate title list for it either:\n§7.1's tail-aligned `S` is base's own heading-tier segments followed by `<path>`'s, one combined\nsequence in resolution order — `--titles` aligns against the tail of *that*, so the common case\n(base already fully exists, only `<path>` needs creating) costs nothing extra, and the rare case\n(base itself needs imagining too) is just more of the same sequence, not a different mechanism.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801R",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801R",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801M",
    "text": "**Schema: a field, not a `Prop`.** `holder: { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:boolean\" }` on\n`BaseNode` — not an entry in the existing `props: Set<Prop>` (`props.ts`'s `StringProp` et al.).\n`props` is reserved for heterogeneous, mdast/frontmatter-derived metadata read back as an opaque\n`{key, value}` bag — a list's `ordered`/`startIndex`, frontmatter serialized as one whole entry\n(`astParser.ts`, `artifacts.ts:316-318`) — content the parser happens to carry, not core ontology\nstate. `holder` is the opposite: a first-class concept needed on every node of every kind, checked\non essentially every resolve and worth checking on every traversal that wants to honor it — the\nsame reasoning `parent` (§8) already settled for this schema: a real typed field beats a value\nburied in a generic collection once the concept is universal rather than incidental per-node data.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801S",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801S",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801M",
    "text": "`kg:tree`/`kg:search` mark and can filter holders — settled in §7.5, not left open.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 7.3 The two callers this exists for",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801T",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801V"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8019",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801V",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801V",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801W",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801Y"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801T",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801V/props/StringProp/OASLy8X0_VEQmmjR",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801V/props/StringProp/gHJtRy3SZdI66OD3",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- **The wikilink extractor** (`artifacts.ts`'s `resolveBlockLinks` — moved there from `astParser.ts`\n  during implementation, since resolution needs DB access a pure parser doesn't have; §2.1's\n  implicit base): today a `[[wikilink]]` to a heading that hasn't been written yet has nowhere to\n  point and the reference is simply dropped. With `--create-holder` (passed automatically here — a\n  forward reference is exactly the case this exists for, not a per-call user choice), it resolves\n  to a holder instead, and the `[[wikilink]]`-predicate `Link`\n  (`Aperas-interactive-summarization-design.md`) points at something real.\n\n  **Real bug found live, more fundamental than a typo**: resolving links *before* the tree's own\n  write (the original ordering) is a genuine chicken-and-egg failure, not just a style choice —\n  the implicit base (`resolveIdToPath` on the containing block) only works on an already-persisted\n  `.parent` chain, so anything inside the artifact currently being ingested would still be resolved\n  against the *previous* ingestion's stale tree, missing whatever's brand new in this very edit.\n  Fixed by moving link resolution to a separate pass *after* the big write (extracting and\n  stripping each block's raw `linkCodes` beforehand — schema.json has no such field, so leaving it\n  in for the write itself also fails schema check — then resolving and patching each affected\n  block by id once the tree is queryable), at the cost of one extra write per block that actually\n  has a wikilink. A second, smaller bug in the same area: building `--titles` as a raw\n  `code.split('/')` overshoots §7.1's tail-alignment count whenever the code contains `..`/`.`\n  tokens (§2) — fixed by filtering those out before building the titles list, since only name\n  segments ever consume one.\n- **A \"vision drawer\"**: a not-yet-built, not otherwise specified tool for sketching an intended\n  tree shape — folders, files, and headings with no real content yet — ahead of writing any of it.\n  `--create-holder` is the primitive such a tool would need, all the way from the artifacts root\n  (§7.2) down to a single heading: resolve/create the intended structure first, populate it later.\n  Whether that tool ends up an interactive `kg:` command (in the spirit of `kg:title`/`kg:link`)\n  or something else is out of scope here; the point is the primitive it would need already falls\n  out of this flag without a separate design.",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801W",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801W",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801X"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801V",
    "text": "**The wikilink extractor** (`artifacts.ts`'s `resolveBlockLinks` — moved there from `astParser.ts`\n  during implementation, since resolution needs DB access a pure parser doesn't have; §2.1's\n  implicit base): today a `[[wikilink]]` to a heading that hasn't been written yet has nowhere to\n  point and the reference is simply dropped. With `--create-holder` (passed automatically here — a\n  forward reference is exactly the case this exists for, not a per-call user choice), it resolves\n  to a holder instead, and the `[[wikilink]]`-predicate `Link`\n  (`Aperas-interactive-summarization-design.md`) points at something real.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801X",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801X",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801W",
    "text": "**Real bug found live, more fundamental than a typo**: resolving links *before* the tree's own\n  write (the original ordering) is a genuine chicken-and-egg failure, not just a style choice —\n  the implicit base (`resolveIdToPath` on the containing block) only works on an already-persisted\n  `.parent` chain, so anything inside the artifact currently being ingested would still be resolved\n  against the *previous* ingestion's stale tree, missing whatever's brand new in this very edit.\n  Fixed by moving link resolution to a separate pass *after* the big write (extracting and\n  stripping each block's raw `linkCodes` beforehand — schema.json has no such field, so leaving it\n  in for the write itself also fails schema check — then resolving and patching each affected\n  block by id once the tree is queryable), at the cost of one extra write per block that actually\n  has a wikilink. A second, smaller bug in the same area: building `--titles` as a raw\n  `code.split('/')` overshoots §7.1's tail-alignment count whenever the code contains `..`/`.`\n  tokens (§2) — fixed by filtering those out before building the titles list, since only name\n  segments ever consume one.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX801Y",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801Y",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801V",
    "text": "**A \"vision drawer\"**: a not-yet-built, not otherwise specified tool for sketching an intended\n  tree shape — folders, files, and headings with no real content yet — ahead of writing any of it.\n  `--create-holder` is the primitive such a tool would need, all the way from the artifacts root\n  (§7.2) down to a single heading: resolve/create the intended structure first, populate it later.\n  Whether that tool ends up an interactive `kg:` command (in the spirit of `kg:title`/`kg:link`)\n  or something else is out of scope here; the point is the primitive it would need already falls\n  out of this flag without a separate design.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 7.4 Why holders are heading-only",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX801Z",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8020",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8021"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8019",
    "text": "A holder `BlockNode` is always created with `type: 'heading'` (§7.1) — that's what makes\nreconciliation need no new design at all: `reconcile.ts`'s `leafKey` — its Stage A match key — is\n`node.title` for a heading and `node.text` for anything else (`reconcile.ts:96-98`). A holder's\n`.title` is already the real, intended title (§7.1's whole reason `--titles` exists), so when\nreal content finally arrives with a matching heading, Stage A's Gestalt match keys on title\nequality and matches the holder exactly like it would match any unedited heading being carried\nforward — same key, same code path; `carryForwardFields` (`reconcile.ts:112`) reuses the holder's\n`blockId` on the incoming real node with zero extra logic. Empty `text` never enters into it.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8020",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8020",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8020",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801Z",
    "text": "A non-heading titled block (the `kg:title` style of addressable-but-not-a-heading block) keys on\n`text` instead, and an empty `text` won't Gestalt-match a real populated one automatically —\nthere's no working reconciliation path for that case, so `--create-holder` doesn't offer it: a\nsegment that would need a non-heading holder still declines, same as any miss without the flag.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8021",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8021",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8021",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX801Z",
    "text": "This restriction sits only at the bottom of the descent (§4's slug tier) — the artifact/folder\ntier above it (§7.2) is unrestricted, root included. `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` reconcile by\npath/abstract similarity (`matchLeftoverByAbstract`, `reconcile.ts:368`), not by this\nheading-vs-text key distinction, so nothing about imagining a whole tree from the root down\ndepends on this choice — only what a leaf holder is allowed to become.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8022",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 7.5 Marking and filtering in `kg:tree` / `kg:search`",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8022",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8023",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8026",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8029"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8019",
    "text": "§7.2 left this open; settling it now, since both commands already have a natural place for it.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8023",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8023",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8023",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8024",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8025"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8022",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8023/props/StringProp/OEtIXUXOxeHsJ9CK",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8023/props/StringProp/R3U05o9XjG08xPGx",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**The mark**: one more trailing field on the line, the same convention `--create-holder`'s own\noutput (§7.1) already uses for exactly this distinction:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8024",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8024",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8024",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8023",
    "text": "`kg:tree`: `<id>  [<kind>]  <title>  (holder)` — `printTree` (`kgCli.ts:63-79`) already fetches\n  the full doc to read `.title`; reading `.holder` off that same fetch costs nothing new.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8025",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8025",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8025",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8023",
    "text": "`kg:search`: `<id>  [<kind>]  <field>  <value>  (holder)` — `searchNodes` (`woql.ts:120`)\n  returns a bare `{id, field, value}` straight from a WOQL triple query, no full document read.\n  The print loop (`kgCli.ts:320-327`) already does one `getNode` fetch per match, but today only\n  conditionally, for `BlockNode`'s `type` label (`if (label === 'BlockNode')`) — reading `.holder`\n  needs that same fetch unconditionally, for every match kind, not only `BlockNode`. Accepted\n  cost: one extra `getNode` round trip per `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` match that previously\n  skipped it entirely (`BlockNode` matches already paid this).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8026",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8026",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8026",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8027",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8028"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8022",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8026/props/StringProp/R2b6iKADdmX7gF8L",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8026/props/StringProp/as4SSY1CwnxoFOMt",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**The filter**: `--no-holders` on both commands, print-time only — neither command's underlying\nquery changes, since a match or a tree node still has to be fetched/visited to know whether it's\na holder in the first place.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8027",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8027",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8027",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8026",
    "text": "`kg:search --no-holders`: matches are a flat list, so this just drops holder matches before\n  printing — nothing else to reconcile.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8028",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8028",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8028",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8026",
    "text": "`kg:tree --no-holders`: matches are a tree, so dropping is per-node, not per-subtree. §7.2\n  already established holders exist at per-node granularity, not all-or-nothing (a still-holder\n  `FolderNode` can have a real `ArtifactNode` underneath it once that one piece gets tracked), so\n  hiding a holder's own line can't also hide what's beneath it. `printTree` recurses into a\n  filtered node's children at the *same* depth it would have used for the hidden node itself — the\n  holder becomes transparent for indentation purposes, not a pruning point. A holder whose entire\n  subtree is also holders (the common case: one `mkdir -p` chain, nothing real underneath it yet)\n  simply produces no output for that whole branch, one hidden line at a time — reading the same as\n  pruning, without a separate subtree-level check to get wrong.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8029",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX8029",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX8029",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX8022",
    "text": "Without `--create-holder` (the default), a miss behaves exactly as §3/§4 already specify — no\nnode is created, resolution simply declines.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 8. `kg:path` — the reverse direction (id→path) — **implemented and live-verified**",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX802A",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802B",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802H",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802K"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX0001",
    "text": "Checked first whether `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §3.A's \"native backlinks\" claim already\ngave this for free: it doesn't. Confirmed live: `t(X, 'links', <a real Link id>)` correctly\nreturns the owning `BlockNode`, but `t(X, 'children', <a real child id>)` returns **nothing**,\neven though that id is genuinely in its parent's `children`. `Set`-typed fields produce a direct\ntriple; `List`-typed ones (`BlockNode.children`, `ArtifactNode.root`, `FolderNode.children` — the\ntree's actual containment, `List` rather than `Set` because child order is semantically\nmeaningful) don't — TerminusDB represents `List` membership through cons-cell/RDF-list\nindirection, not a direct `Parent predicate Child` triple. §3.A has been corrected to state this\nprecisely rather than leave the disproven generalization standing.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX802B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX802B",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802C",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802F",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802G"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802A",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802B/props/StringProp/C7o1NeU5FYjm-NqH",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802B/props/StringProp/HmOlOeT_61o1w4uJ",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**The fix**: a direct `parent: Optional<BaseNode>` field on `BlockNode` (`schema.json`) — not a\n`parentId: xsd:string` requiring kind-guessing to become useful, a real typed reference read\nstraight off the document, same as `Link`/`Assertion`'s `target`/`source` already work. Points at\nthe direct container's full id — another `BlockNode`, or the owning `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`\nfor a tree's own root.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX802C",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX802C",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802B",
    "text": "**`astParser.ts`**: `stampParents`, a post-pass over the *finished* tree (not threaded through\n  construction) setting `child.parent` for every direct child. Exported, not auto-run only once —\n  see the next bug.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX802D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX802D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802B",
    "text": "**`artifacts.ts`**: the tree's own root has no parent from parsing alone (nothing inside the\n  parser knows the owning `ArtifactNode`) — stamped externally: `finalRoot.parent =\n  ArtifactNode/<artifactId>`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX802E",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX802E",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802B",
    "text": "**`folders.ts`**: README absorption relocates top-level parsed blocks directly into\n  `FolderNode.children`, bypassing a root block entirely — those get re-stamped to\n  `FolderNode/<folderId>`, overriding what `astParser.ts` set before the splice.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX802F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX802F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802B",
    "text": "**`nodeRef.ts`**: `resolveIdToPath` walks `.parent` up to the first `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`,\n  collecting each hop's slugified `title`, then prepends that node's own `path` (already the full\n  nested path from the artifacts root — no further upward walk needed once reached). `slugify`\n  (§5) lives here too, shared with the not-yet-built `kg:resolve` side.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX802G",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX802G",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802B",
    "text": "**`kgCli.ts`**: `kg:path <id>`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX802H",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX802H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802A",
    "text": "**Real bug found and fixed before this worked at all**: `stampParents` originally ran once,\npre-reconciliation, inside `parseMarkdownTree`. `reconcile.ts`'s `carryForwardFields` then\nreassigns `blockId` for every matched pair (the fresh parse's transient id → the old carried-\nforward one) — so every already-stamped child's `.parent` pointed at its container's *discarded*\npre-reassignment id, a reference to a document that never actually gets written. Confirmed live as\n`references_untyped_object` (`SchemaCheckFailure`) on the very next real re-ingest of any existing\nartifact — not a hypothetical. Fixed by calling `stampParents` a second time in `artifacts.ts`,\n*after* reconciliation, on the final tree.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX802J",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX802J",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802A",
    "text": "**Second real bug, found by `verify:phase0 -- --db`'s own cleanup step failing**: `parent` makes\n`ArtifactNode`↔root-`BlockNode` a genuine reference cycle for the first time (root points down via\n`children`/`root`, now also points up via `parent`) — the same class of cycle\n`findLinkIdsTargeting`'s doc comment already covers for `Link`↔`BlockNode`, with the same fix:\ndelete everything in the cycle together, in one combined batch, not the `ArtifactNode` separately\nand first. `verifyPhase0.ts`'s `resetDemoState` did exactly the latter; fixed to match.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZAX802K",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZAX802K",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZAX802A",
    "text": "**Migration caveat, same pattern as every prior schema addition in this project**: `parent` is\n`Optional`, so existing content isn't broken, but a `BlockNode` ingested before this field existed\nhas no `parent` set until its artifact is actually re-ingested (content-hash-unchanged artifacts\nare correctly skipped by `kg:ingest`, so this doesn't happen automatically). `kg:path` reports a\nclear, specific miss for this case rather than crashing — confirmed live against both a freshly\nre-ingested block (full path resolved correctly, multiple levels deep) and a stale one (correct,\nactionable miss message).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZCTG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Document Root",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZCTG000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZCTG001"
    ],
    "parent": "ArtifactNode/00C7BJHG28000",
    "type": "root",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZCTG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZCTG001",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZCTG001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZCTG002"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZCTG000",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZCTG001/props/StringProp/U5m3KcVP9MrL60lu",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZCTG001/props/StringProp/jd78CXy2Gw-8crKj",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- ## Aperas\n\t- Aperas Master Architectural Index\n\t\t- Overview\n\t\t\t- Aperas is an architecture for agentic knowledge systems operating over a fluid, unconditioned semantic core and crystallizing typed boundaries on demand.\n\t\t\t- To maintain modularity and deep clarity, the specification is decomposed into three focused documents.\n\t\t- Core Architecture Suite\n\t\t\t- Philosophy and Ontology of Aperas\n\t\t\t\t- Metaphysical foundation: Apeiron (macrocosm), Aperas (microcosm), and Peras (interface).\n\t\t\t\t- Conceptual triad: Unbounded – Unbound – Bound.\n\t\t\t\t- Flatbread metaphor and linguistic emergence.\n\t\t\t\t- Multi-faceted node epistemology: Wikipedia reference model (Article, Source, Talk, History).\n\t\t\t- System Architecture and Mechanics of Aperas\n\t\t\t\t- Architectural philosophy: Fluid core vs. crystallized interfaces.\n\t\t\t\t- Multi-Agent Projection Pattern and dynamic read–write transducers.\n\t\t\t\t- System taxonomy and component boundaries.\n\t\t\t\t- Async execution model, command dispatch, and self-healing boundary validation.\n\t\t\t- Node, AST, and Storage Design in Aperas\n\t\t\t\t- On-demand reification and lazy atomization mechanics.\n\t\t\t\t- Three parallel planes: Rendered plane, Stored plane, and Graph plane.\n\t\t\t\t- Universal inline boundary: `<span>` tag addressability and offset tracking.\n\t\t\t\t- Unified.js AST mapping to TerminusDB storage substrate.\n\t- Philosophy and Ontology of Aperas\n\t\t- Metaphysics of Form and Freedom (Unbounded – Unbound – Bound)\n\t\t\t- Apeiron – Unbounded (Macrocosm – Objective Infinite)\n\t\t\t\t- The outer universe existing in a state of infinite, unmanifested potential.\n\t\t\t\t- Represents the unconditioned, boundless origin – an inexhaustible, schema-free field holding all possibilities without being trapped by any single form.\n\t\t\t- Aperas – Unbound (Microcosm – Subjective Liberation)\n\t\t\t\t- The inner human spirit achieving freedom through the liberation of the soul.\n\t\t\t\t- Etymologically layered:\n\t\t\t\t\t- *A-* (without) + *Peras* (limit) = **Unbound**: Not merely “unlimited” in size, but intrinsically free from rigid constraints.\n\t\t\t\t\t- *Aperas* in Esperanto = “Appears / Emerges”: The active manifestation where structured forms materialize out of the fluid core on demand.\n\t\t\t\t\t- *Aperas* (Spanish verb meaning “equip, harness”): The functional act of provisioning an agent with the vital instruments, tools, and memory required to navigate and execute operations.\n \t\t\t\t\t- *Apera* (Botanical Genus / [Windgrass](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apera)): The organic paradigm of fluid resilience, the ability to keep wholeness (*a-pēros*) by gracefully bending and naturalizing across any environment without breaking.\n\t\t\t\t\t- *Aperis* (Latin verb meaning “open, uncover”): The open-source act of laying knowledge bare, mirroring its legal fluidity under [The Unlicense](https://unlicense.org/) by refusing rigid cages or ownership.\n\t\t\t\t- While daily interaction imposes limits and fixed roles, the liberated soul breaks through the illusion of rigid division, realizing its inner ground is identical in essence to the boundless Apeiron.\n\t\t\t- Peras – Bound (The Interface – Transient Boundary)\n\t\t\t\t- The limit, boundary, or transient interface where interaction occurs.\n\t\t\t\t- Form is not a rigid cage, but a temporary, playful instrument. Content remains formless (Apeiron), while the interface solidifies temporarily into defined structures (Peras) to accelerate interaction, then dissolves effortlessly back into the origin.\n\t\t- Multi-Faceted Node Epistemology (The Wikipedia Reference Model)\n\t\t\t- Historical precedent in collaborative knowledge work\n\t\t\t\t- Systems like Wikipedia demonstrate that knowledge nodes are multi-perspective entities rather than flat text blobs.\n\t\t\t\t- Aperas adopts this multi-tab structure as an illustrative reference model for node projections.\n\t\t\t- Projection faces of a node\n\t\t\t\t- Surface view (Article – Peras projection): Markdown and HTML outliner blocks rendered for human reading and fast LLM context window ingestion.\n\t\t\t\t- Graph view (Source – Canonical triples): Semantic graph triples, explicit parent-child hierarchies, typed predicates (`impacts`, `verifies`, `derived_from`), and IRI references.\n\t\t\t\t- Discourse view (Talk – Agent trails): Agent trails, deliberations, conflicting perspectives, and reasoning traces behind decision states.\n\t\t\t\t- Provenance view (History – Audit deltas): Immutable commit logs, author signatures (human or agent ID), and atomic revision deltas.\n\t\t- Meta-Aperas / Self-Aperas (Recursive Methodology)\n\t\t\t- System evolution as ontological practice\n\t\t\t\t- The engineering roadmap of Aperas is itself a self-instantiation of its underlying philosophy.\n\t\t\t\t- Phase 0 begins in the uncarved, schema-free substrate (Apeiron), avoiding premature interfaces or heavy abstractions.\n\t\t\t\t- Projections, skills, and tools (Perata) crystallize organically on demand only as concrete human–agent coordination points emerge.\n\t\t\t\t- The platform builds itself using the exact principles it uses to manage knowledge: fluid at rest, bound and structured when touched.\n\t- System Architecture and Mechanics of Aperas\n\t\t- Architectural Philosophy: Fluid Core vs. Crystallized Interfaces\n\t\t\t- Fluid core substrate (Apeiron – formless content)\n\t\t\t\t- The canonical state substrate remains a continuous, unconditioned semantic field – a rich graph of associations, atomic assertions, decisions, and raw evidence without rigid schema impedance.\n\t\t\t\t- Avoids premature schema-on-write constraints that destroy nuance or cause friction during exploratory thought and multi-agent reasoning.\n\t\t\t- Schema-as-projection (Peras – crystallized interfaces)\n\t\t\t\t- Schemas, types, enums, and unique identifiers are not intrinsic properties of the data substrate; they are temporary, disposable crystallizations generated on demand.\n\t\t\t\t- Interfaces represent agreed-upon, lossy compressions between two parties (agents, APIs, UI engines) designed to minimize interaction friction and maximize transaction speed.\n\t\t\t- Perimeter boundary transducers\n\t\t\t\t- Type systems (e.g., Pydantic in Python, Zod/TypeBox in TypeScript) live strictly at the system perimeter (boundary transducers) rather than polluting the core graph engine.\n\t\t\t\t- Boundary agents sample the fluid core (Apeiron) and project it into rigid, type-safe structures (Peras) strictly when interacting with deterministic runtimes (databases, AST compilers, UI outliners, or structured LLM tool endpoints).\n\t\t- Multi-Agent Projection Pattern\n\t\t\t- Pattern definition\n\t\t\t\t- An architectural paradigm where a high-dimensional, fluid state graph (Apeiron) acts as the single source of truth, while specialized agents act as bidirectional transducers (lenses) mediating human and machine interactions.\n\t\t\t- Read-side projection lenses (deep read)\n\t\t\t\t- Specialized projection agents extract subgraphs and crystallize them into low-dimensional, context-specific interfaces (Peras) like Markdown outliners, source code ASTs, or sequence diagrams.\n\t\t\t\t- Enables deep read capabilities where every rendered element retains provenance anchors and backlinks, allowing readers to drill down into the supporting evidence subgraph.\n\t\t\t- Write-side control and delta ingestion (deep write)\n\t\t\t\t- Intake and impact agents parse view-level edits, calculate semantic diffs, and commit reified assertion nodes or validity updates back to the substrate.\n\t\t\t\t- Enables deep write capabilities where edits trigger impact traversals along `affects` or `constrains` edges, calculating downstream ripple effects and queuing advisory invalidations.\n\t\t- System Taxonomy and Vocabulary\n\t\t\t- System / Platform: **Aperas** – The unbound environment where structure emerges (“appears”).\n\t\t\t- Fluid Core Substrate: **Apeiron** – The unconditioned, schema-free continuum where thoughts and semantic links flow without artificial boundaries.\n\t\t\t- Boundary Projections / Interfaces: **Peras** – The transient, typed structures (JSON Schema, Zod/Pydantic models, AST trees, DB mutations).\n\t\t\t- Transduction Process: **Aperas** (*to appear / materialize*) – The active emergence of structured Peras out of the unconditioned Apeiron.\n\t\t- Coding Agent Interface Continuum\n\t\t\t- Manual skill mode (procedural recipes)\n\t\t\t\t- Direct execution of `SKILL.md` procedures and local scripts without external service layers or extra API token costs.\n\t\t\t- Active tool mode (on-demand query)\n\t\t\t\t- Explicit tool calling (via service APIs) where the coding agent queries or mutates perata through high-level skills at an abstract level.\n\t\t\t- Automatic hook mode (context engineering)\n\t\t\t\t- Event-driven injection where relevant perata projections are automatically synthesized and placed directly into the coding agent's prompt/context window.\n\t\t- Async Execution and Command Dispatch Mechanics\n\t\t\t- Unconstrained reasoning to batch command emission\n\t\t\t\t- Reasoner agents operate with free-flow, unconstrained thinking to analyze context and draft mutations without suffering from structured output reasoning constraints.\n\t\t\t\t- At turn termination, the agent emits a single batch tool call or tagged payload containing array-based command intents (e.g., `list[CommandIntent]`).\n\t\t\t- Event-driven async fan-out\n\t\t\t\t- Emitted command batches are pushed to an event-driven dispatcher and executed concurrently across worker coroutines and service endpoints.\n\t\t\t\t- Agents remain non-blocking and decoupled from slow, synchronous tool-calling loops.\n\t\t\t- Self-healing boundary validation\n\t\t\t\t- If a command payload violates boundary schema rules (Peras), type validation tracebacks are fed back to the boundary agent for immediate, localized repair without polluting the fluid core (Apeiron).\n\t- Node, AST, and Storage Design in Aperas\n\t\t- On-demand reification (lazy atomization)\n\t\t\t- Coarse by default, fine when touched\n\t\t\t\t- Content is preserved at coarse granularity (full paragraph, list item, or document block) without forcing upfront micro-fragmentation into tokens or words.\n\t\t\t\t- The moment a sub-clause, phrase, word, or punctuation mark is addressed, linked, commented on in the Talk view, or bound to a predicate, that specific slice crystallizes into an addressed node with its own identity (URI or UUID).\n\t\t\t\t- Untouched surrounding text remains unfragmented text chunks.\n\t\t- Three parallel planes of architecture\n\t\t\t- Rendered plane (Artifact / Surface Peras)\n\t\t\t\t- The continuous, readable Markdown or HTML text flow where prose reads naturally without visual disruption.\n\t\t\t- Stored plane (Substrate Apeiron)\n\t\t\t\t- The backing store in TerminusDB preserving content as coarse or fine AST nodes and character offset ranges.\n\t\t\t\t- Mirrored bidirectionally as plain JSON-LD files under `AperasKG/Apeiron/` (`Aperas-architecture.md` §5) — a portable, engine-agnostic round-trip of the same content, independent of TerminusDB's own storage internals.\n\t\t\t- Graph plane (Relational Peras)\n\t\t\t\t- The explicit network of nodes and edges connecting addressed entities (documents, blocks, clauses, words, punctuation) to the rest of the knowledge graph.\n\t\t- Universal inline addressability (`<span>` tag pattern)\n\t\t\t- Zero layout interference\n\t\t\t\t- Unlike `<div>` or `<p>`, `<span>` elements introduce no visual breaks or line collapses in the rendered plane.\n\t\t\t- Attribute anchoring\n\t\t\t\t- Acts as an inline hook for unique node identifiers (`id=\"node-123\"`), graph attributes (`data-predicate=\"refutes\"`), and event listeners directly over any target character range.\n\t\t\t- Arbitrary granularity\n\t\t\t\t- Can wrap a sentence, phrase, single word, or punctuation mark without disrupting document layout or styling.\n\t\t- Unified.js AST mapping to TerminusDB substrate\n\t\t\t- Granularity mapping\n\t\t\t\t- Document level (`root` in mdast) corresponds to the page or section document container node.\n\t\t\t\t- Block level (`listItem` / `paragraph` in mdast) corresponds to individual outliner blocks with parent-child graph edges.\n\t\t\t\t- Phrase or entity level (`link` / `span` in hast) corresponds to on-demand reified nodes for addressed inline spans.\n\t\t\t\t- Word or token level (`nlcst`) acts as ephemeral parser nodes used strictly during transduction passes, reified into graph nodes only when explicitly touched.\n\t\t\t- Offset tracking and synchronization\n\t\t\t\t- Unified tracks exact source offsets (`position: {start, end}`) across all AST nodes.\n\t\t\t\t- When an inline span is addressed, the transducer uses offsets to split the parent AST text node and assign an ID to the newly created span node without disturbing surrounding text.\n\t- Development Roadmap of Aperas\n\t\t- Meta-Aperas Ontological Alignment\n\t\t\t- The engineering roadmap is itself a self-instantiation of the Aperas ontology: starting in the unconditioned substrate (Apeiron) and crystallizing typed interfaces (Perata) on demand as human–agent coordination needs emerge.\n\t\t- Phase 0: Infrastructure and basic tools (Apeiron direct)\n\t\t\t- Direct database integration\n\t\t\t\t- The primary coding agent connects directly to the **TerminusDB** substrate without any intermediate UI layer.\n\t\t\t\t- System provides baseline skill definitions (`SKILL.md`) covering schema initialization, document CRUD, WOQL and GraphQL query execution, and temporal commit management.\n\t\t\t- Verification without UI overhead\n\t\t\t\t- Validates core graph operations, transaction safety, and agent reasoning loops directly against the canonical store.\n\t\t\t- Basic assertion authoring (moved earlier from Phase 2, see `Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`)\n\t\t\t\t- `Assertion` (the concrete `BaseEdge`) was already a working CRUD/WOQL primitive; this adds manual CLI commands (`kg:assert`/`kg:assertions`/`kg:unassert`) so a human or agent can actually write and inspect a real one, with no automated traversal or agent behavior attached.\n\t\t\t\t- Built here rather than waiting for Phase 1, so Phase 1's skill-building work starts with it already available instead of blocked on it — and ahead of the SolidJS UI, since Artifact Projection (mode 1 of §5.A) already covers the monitoring need the UI was originally assumed to be required for.\n\t\t\t- Agentic query tools (moved earlier from Phase 1, see `Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md`)\n\t\t\t\t- Mode 3 of §5.A (\"Agentic Interface, BFS Traversal\") made concrete as three CLI tools: `kg:tree` (a deep, title-only structural map — depth-first, not BFS despite the mode's name), `kg:unfold`/`kg:fold` (the real breadth-first primitive — one level, full content, persisting the long-inert `BlockNode.unfolded` flag for the first time), and `kg:search` (keyword/regex content search via WOQL, closing a previously-unfilled gap).\n\t\t\t\t- Moved here for the same reason as basic assertion authoring: closes the block-level addressing gap that authoring left open, and doesn't need the UI to exist first.\n\t\t- Phase 1: Core skills & UI Peras (Human–agent co-navigation)\n\t\t\t- Apeiron operating skills\n\t\t\t\t- Agent Skills (`SKILL.md`) teaching a coding agent how to use the CLI to operate and manage the KG.\n\t\t\t- SolidJS read projection\n\t\t\t\t- Constructs the initial **SolidJS + Unified.js** projection interface for deep-read human inspection.\n\t\t\t\t- Renders continuous Markdown outliner trees directly from TerminusDB AST nodes.\n\t\t\t- Multi-faceted node interface\n\t\t\t\t- Surface projections reveal the multi-tab reference model (Article, Source, Talk, History) so humans and agents navigate the exact same operational state.\n\t\t- Phase 2: Skill expansion and perata maturation\n\t\t\t- Incremental capability growth\n\t\t\t\t- Develops specialized procedural skills including ingestion (parsing external text into AST nodes), *automated* graph search (structural traversals and WOQL/GraphQL pattern matching, with vector support evaluated later — the manual assertion primitive itself moved to Phase 1, above), and refactoring (lazy atomization of spans and node merging).\n\t\t\t- Co-evolution of tools and perata\n\t\t\t\t- Each new skill definition naturally introduces corresponding tooling and new **perata** (specialized view projections).\n\t\t\t- Collaboration perata: review/merge-request workflow\n\t\t\t\t- TerminusDB's `rebase`/`apply` give branch reconciliation but no GitHub-style review layer — no proposal-with-discussion, approval state, or inline comment thread sits between \"diverged branches\" and \"merged.\"\n\t\t\t\t- Aperas extends the multi-faceted node model (Discourse view / Talk) to a first-class review document type: a proposal node wrapping a source/target branch pair, with discussion threads, review state, and conflict-resolution notes stored as subdocuments in the KG rather than in an external tool.\n\t\t\t\t- Keeps Aperas GitHub-like as well as Git-like: comments, issues, and review decisions become graph-native, queryable, and provenance-tracked alongside the commits they discuss, instead of living in a bolted-on external system.\n\t\t- Phase 3: Multi-agent specialization and Aperas Agent interfaces\n\t\t\t- Sub-agent spawning for coding agents without background API access\n\t\t\t\t- Equips primary coding agents with specialized `SKILL.md` definitions that allow them to spawn local sub-agents via headless CLI calls, native sub-agent tools, or local execution scripts.\n\t\t\t\t- Preserves the direct Phase 0/2 skill set so local coding agents perform deep structural graph operations, AST transductions, and multi-node updates using existing lab harnesses without secondary API costs.\n\t\t\t- Dedicated agent swarms (API-enabled environments)\n\t\t\t\t- For environments with secondary API access, specialized multi-agent subgraphs (built with **LangGraph**) run on dedicated model endpoints for complex background tasks.\n\t\t\t- Triple interface continuum for user coding agents\n\t\t\t\t- **Manual skill mode (procedural / CLI execution):** The coding agent executes raw procedural skills (`SKILL.md`) directly against the substrate, preserving full operational autonomy without API dependencies.\n\t\t\t\t- **Tool mode (active query):** Exposes high-level perata tools and service endpoints for the coding agent to interact directly with the Aperas Agent on demand.\n\t\t\t\t- **Hook mode (automatic context engineering):** Injects perata and context projections directly into the context window of the coding agent via background events and triggers.\n\t\t- Phase 4: Substrate Evolution — ApeironNgn (native Rust successor, speculative)\n\t\t\t- Motivation: right-sizing the substrate to what Aperas actually exercises\n\t\t\t\t- TerminusDB Community brings a general-purpose Prolog/WOQL Datalog engine, enterprise-oriented tooling, and its own bundle/layer format for versioning — capability well beyond what Phase 0-3 skills exercise (document CRUD, one-hop impact traversal, branch/commit/merge as a review workflow).\n\t\t\t\t- The bidirectional JSON-LD mirror (`AperasKG/Apeiron/`, `Aperas-architecture.md` §5) demonstrates this directly: the canonical graph content already round-trips as plain JSON-LD files, decoupled from TerminusDB's own storage internals — nothing about the substrate's actual content depends on TerminusDB being the engine underneath it.\n\t\t\t- ApeironNgn: a minimal engine sized to exactly what Aperas needs\n\t\t\t\t- A small, embeddable Rust graph engine scoped to the primitives Phase 0-3 actually use: the `BaseNode`/`BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` document model and simple pattern-match queries (source/predicate/target lookups, bounded-depth traversal) — no general Datalog/Prolog solver, no auto-generated GraphQL schema, no multi-tenant/enterprise surface.\n\t\t\t\t- No separate commit-layer engine to build: `AperasKG/Apeiron/` already lives inside a real git repository, so a JSON-LD write followed by an ordinary `git commit` over that directory *is* the version control — immutable history, diffing, and branching all come from git itself, not a bespoke bundle/layer format ApeironNgn would otherwise have to reimplement. TerminusDB needed its own commit-layer machinery because it owns an opaque store with no git underneath it; once Aperas is managing history directly against `AperasKG/Apeiron/`, ApeironNgn doesn't need to.\n\t\t\t- Migration surface: the same modules stand in for the same role, one changing shape\n\t\t\t\t- `client.ts`, `crud.ts`, `woql.ts`, and `graphql.ts` (`Aperas-architecture.md` §3) already isolate every TerminusDB-specific call behind a small, named set of modules — that boundary is what ApeironNgn would need to implement to stand in for TerminusDB, with `AperasKG/Apeiron/` as the on-disk state `kg:export`/`kg:import` already round-trip through.\n\t\t\t\t- `versionControl.ts` doesn't carry over as-is — its branch/commit/diff/merge calls would delegate straight to real `git` operations on the `AperasKG` repository instead of TerminusDB's git-like API, since the KG's \"commit\" *is* a git commit once ApeironNgn is the engine.\n\t\t\t- Status: unscheduled and speculative\n\t\t\t\t- Contingent on Phase 0-3 confirming that TerminusDB Community edition's rough edges — cross-store `bundle`/`unbundle` incompatibility (`terminusdb/terminusdb#2509`) and the `List`-typed `@unfold` gap (`terminusdb/terminusdb#2512`), both documented in `Aperas-dev-status.md` — are structural rather than incidental before committing engineering effort to a replacement.\n\t- Open-Source Stack for Agentic Knowledge Graph Architectures\n\t\t- System architecture layers\n\t\t\t- Canonical graph storage layer (the substrate)\n\t\t\t\t- **TerminusDB** (chosen primary substrate) provides revision control on knowledge graphs with Git-like branches, delta layers, diffs, and merge requests.\n\t\t\t\t- **FalkorDB** and **Neo4j Community** offer property graph engines with sub-millisecond Cypher querying.\n\t\t\t\t- **SQLite** and **DuckDB** with DuckPGQ offer local-first, lightweight deployments.\n\t\t\t- UI rendering and projection layer\n\t\t\t\t- **SolidJS** (chosen UI framework) provides fine-grained signal-based reactivity without Virtual DOM overhead for rendering deep-read outliners and reified inline spans.\n\t\t\t\t- **React Flow** / **XYFlow** and **Mermaid.js** offer visual graph and diagram rendering utilities.\n\t\t\t- Agent swarm and orchestration layer\n\t\t\t\t- **LangGraph** enables stateful, cyclical multi-agent orchestration to model read–write loops across intake, validation, and impact sweep agents.\n\t\t\t\t- **PydanticAI** provides type-safe, schema-guided agent definitions ensuring outputs adhere to exact block-tree entity schemas.\n\t\t\t- Interoperability, procedures, and boundary validation\n\t\t\t\t- **Agent Skills** (`SKILL.md`) serves as an open standard for defining modular, loadable agent instructions and view templates.\n\t\t\t\t- **TypeBox** / **Zod** (TypeScript) and **Pydantic** (Python) serve as boundary transducers enforcing schema contracts at network edges.\n\t\t\t\t- Direct Service APIs (GraphQL or REST) serve as direct communication interfaces between agents and backend services.\n\t\t\t- Surface editor and AST parsing layer\n\t\t\t\t- **Unified.js** (`remark` / `rehype`) acts as the bidirectional AST parsing engine for Markdown to MDAST conversion, offset tracking, and block extraction.\n\t\t- Minimal prototype architecture\n\t\t\t- View layer\n\t\t\t\t- SolidJS reactive tree views render projected subgraphs and inline `<span>` addressability.\n\t\t\t\t- Unified.js converts raw Markdown into structural AST nodes for extraction and offset tracking.\n\t\t\t- Agent layer\n\t\t\t\t- LangGraph and PydanticAI service agents equipped with dynamically loaded Agent Skills.\n\t\t\t\t- Projection agents read subgraphs via GraphQL or REST and render Markdown or specs according to skill workflows.\n\t\t\t\t- Ingestion agents parse unstructured inputs directly into structured block-tree deltas.\n\t\t\t\t- Impact agents traverse edges and flag stale views.\n\t\t\t- Storage layer\n\t\t\t\t- TerminusDB maintains reified assertion nodes, immutable commit deltas, and temporal branch management.\n\t- Stack Selection Rationales & Discussions\n\t\t- Canonical graph storage substrate rationale\n\t\t\t- Selection: TerminusDB\n\t\t\t- Immutable delta-layer architecture\n\t\t\t\t- Operates on succinct, bit-packed immutable layers backed by Rust and Prolog, where every mutation appends a diff layer without overwriting past state.\n\t\t\t\t- Grants native Git-like time travel, branching, diffing, and merging, allowing agents and humans to draft speculative updates on proposal branches.\n\t\t\t\t- Provides strict revision auditability where every assertion and invalidation is intrinsically tied to a commit author, timestamp, and message.\n\t\t\t- Isomorphism with block-tree and hybrid projection models\n\t\t\t\t- Models data as JSON-LD Document Classes, offering direct structural mapping to outliner block-trees (Logseq/Roam style) and nested subdocuments.\n\t\t\t\t- Supports the multi-faceted node reference pattern, storing full Markdown text for human UI rendering alongside structured assertion fields and triple relationships.\n\t\t\t- Dual-query interface capabilities\n\t\t\t\t- Employs WOQL (Web Object Query Language) powered by Datalog for pattern matching, recursive transitive dependency traversals, and impact propagation calculations.\n\t\t\t\t- Exposes automatically generated GraphQL endpoints for projection agents to perform fast, multi-hop contextual fetches during view rendering.\n\t\t\t- Future substrate alternative under consideration\n\t\t\t\t- ApeironNgn — a minimal Rust graph engine scoped to exactly what Aperas exercises — is sketched as a speculative future replacement; see Development Roadmap \"Phase 4: Substrate Evolution\".\n\t\t- UI projection layer rationale\n\t\t\t- Selection: SolidJS\n\t\t\t- Fine-grained signal reactivity without Virtual DOM overhead\n\t\t\t\t- Compiles template JSX into direct DOM mutations without virtual DOM reconciliation cascades.\n\t\t\t\t- Updating an inline `<span>`, reified node badge, or discourse trail tag updates only the exact target DOM element with near-zero CPU footprint.\n\t\t\t\t- Prevents micro-stutters and re-rendering cascades across massive, deeply nested outliner trees.\n\t\t\t- Optimization for agentic deep-read views\n\t\t\t\t- Since AI agents execute structural graph edits via backend transducers, the UI functions primarily as a high-performance **deep-read projection layer** with light human annotation.\n\t\t\t\t- Signals subscribe directly to background streaming deltas, updating rendered views surgically as agents commit updates.\n\t\t\t- Relativistic context ergonomics\n\t\t\t\t- Solid primitives (`createSignal`, `createContext`, `createStore`) map naturally to relativistic node scoping, allowing child nodes and inline spans to bind locally without triggering parent component re-evaluations.\n\t\t- Preservation of manual skills & zero-background-API sub-agent execution rationale\n\t\t\t- Background constraint: Coding-agent-only environment\n\t\t\t\t- In enterprise or research lab environments, developers are provided access exclusively to primary coding agents (e.g., Claude Code, Antigravity) via local harnesses, while secondary external LLM API keys are strictly blocked or restricted.\n\t\t\t\t- Traditional agentic memory frameworks (such as Letta/MemGPT, Mem0, or Zep) rely on background API servers running continuous summarization and vector indexing loops, making them unusable under zero-API constraints.\n\t\t\t\t- Basic model-less agent wikis provide local file reading, but lack structured multi-faceted node semantics (Article, Source, Talk, History), on-demand reification, and dependency tracking.\n\t\t\t- Solution: Local sub-agent spawning via standard `SKILL.md` contracts\n\t\t\t\t- Aperas retains the lightweight procedural skill set (`SKILL.md`) in Phase 3 rather than forcing background API dependencies.\n\t\t\t\t- Primary coding agents leverage native CLI primitives (headless process execution like `claude -p`), internal sub-agent tools, or direct local execution scripts to spawn isolated local child agents.\n\t\t\t\t- Child sub-agents execute focused graph operations (AST parsing, WOQL traversals, span reification, Talk ledger updates) using the developer's existing authorized agent harness without incurring secondary API costs.\n\t\t\t- Cognitive shift: Intelligence in data topology and transducers\n\t\t\t\t- Advanced agentic memory is achieved through deterministic TerminusDB schemas, Unified.js transducers, and structured `SKILL.md` instructions rather than perpetual background LLM calls.\n\t\t- Boundary validation & schema generation rationale\n\t\t\t- Selection: TypeBox / Zod (TypeScript) & Pydantic (Python)\n\t\t\t- Role as perimeter transducers\n\t\t\t\t- Enforces strict schema contracts exclusively at external API, LLM tool-calling, and DB mutation boundaries.\n\t\t\t\t- Keeps the core graph substrate fluid and unconstrained while guaranteeing type safety when communicating with deterministic runtimes.\n\t\t- AST parsing & surface synchronization rationale\n\t\t\t- Selection: Unified.js (`remark` / `rehype`)\n\t\t\t- Role in surface translation\n\t\t\t\t- Parses Markdown surface views into clean syntax trees (MDAST), enabling bidirectional extraction of block IDs, wikilinks, and metadata tags for sync back to the underlying graph.\n\t\t- Agent procedure and communication layer rationale\n\t\t\t- Selection: Loadable Agent Skills (`SKILL.md`) + Direct Service APIs\n\t\t\t- Primary role of Agent Skills\n\t\t\t\t- Encapsulates procedural knowledge, view-projection templates, sub-agent spawning contracts, and domain rules into human-readable Markdown files.\n\t\t\t\t- Enables progressive context loading where agents load specific skill definitions dynamically when executing targeted tasks rather than bloating the system prompt.\n\t\t\t\t- Provides portable, vendor-agnostic behavioral definitions allowing any agent model or framework to execute projection and ingestion routines predictably.\n\t\t\t- Elimination of persistent protocol middleware\n\t\t\t\t- Replaces permanently running RPC servers with direct service calls (GraphQL / REST endpoints) to system service agents.\n\t\t\t\t- Maintains a clean service boundary between procedural guidance (skills) and backend execution (service endpoints).\n\t- Multi-Agent Division of Labor on a Knowledge Graph  \n\t\t- Historical precedent and human wetware substrate\n\t\t\t- Multi-faceted reference model: Inspired by long-standing collaborative systems like Wikipedia (Article, Talk, History, Source), human knowledge work has always relied on multi-perspective nodes rather than monolithic text blobs.\n\t\t\t- Software engineering and science operate as a distributed graph running across human mental models and neural networks.\n\t\t\t- Artifacts like source code, architecture diagrams, and tickets are lossy projections rendered from an underlying mental graph.\n\t\t\t- Deep reads historically relied on manual navigation, while deep writes relied on mental impact tracing across dependencies.\n\t\t- Role of LLMs and agents in the architecture\n\t\t\t- LLMs function as bidirectional projection engines rather than the primary enterprise brain.\n\t\t\t- The knowledge graph serves as the shared, explicit substrate representing the externalized mental model.\n\t\t\t- Specialized agents act as dynamic lenses translating between high-dimensional graph topology and low-dimensional human interfaces.\n\t\t- Division of agent roles\n\t\t\t- Projection agents (read side / rendering)\n\t\t\t\t- View-specific synthesis extracts subgraphs – such as nodes, edges, evidence, and ADRs – projecting them into target formats like source code, executive summaries, API specs, or Mermaid diagrams.\n\t\t\t\t- Context pruning and zoom levels filter graph depth based on the target audience.\n\t\t\t\t- Transclusion annotators attach backlink references and provenance pointers to UI elements so readers can drill back into the underlying subgraph.\n\t\t\t- Intake and ingestion agents (write side / interpretation)\n\t\t\t\t- Input parsing ingests unstructured human artifacts – including PR comments, chat threads, commit messages, and raw notes – resolving them into structured semantic entities and relationships.\n\t\t\t\t- Reification and provenance create explicit assertion nodes tracking who stated a requirement, when, and under what rationale instead of overwriting nodes.\n\t\t\t- Impact and coherence agents (ripple managers)\n\t\t\t\t- Semantic dependency traversal walks the graph along `affects`, `derives_from`, or `constrains` edges to map potential impact zones when a node changes.\n\t\t\t\t- Advisory invalidation marks affected views or nodes as “stale” or “questioned”, preparing diff proposals or triage queues for human review rather than blindly rewriting downstream documents.\n\t\t\t\t- Contradiction sweeping identifies edge conflicts before they silently pollute other views.\n\t\t- Pitfalls of complete automation\n\t\t\t- Full automation creates brittle, over-constrained systems that fight human intuition and fluid workflow context.\n\t\t\t- Human mental graphs handle ambiguity, shifting constraints, and informal consensus seamlessly, which deterministic engines collapse.\n\t\t\t- Complete automated overwriting risks context collapse by treating exploratory comments or questions as hard state updates.\n\t\t\t- The goal is explicit externalization and human-in-the-loop advisory feedback, not autonomous machine control.\n\t\t- Key design challenges\n\t\t\t- Idempotent round-tripping requires updating only the modified delta when an agent parses human edits on rendered Markdown without clobbering unrendered graph metadata.\n\t\t\t- Granularity of nodes requires balancing atomic entities to avoid noise and coarse documents to preserve deep read benefits.\n\t\t\t- Loose coupling of agent roles keeps agents stateless and lets the graph own canonical state so any agent can crash or be swapped out seamlessly.\n\t- State of the Art in Agentic Knowledge Graphs\n\t\t- Convergence of paradigm\n\t\t\t- Current frontier systems combine neurosymbolic AI, dynamic GraphRAG, and bidirectional knowledge maintenance.\n\t\t\t- Knowledge graphs are treated as living, canonical state machines rather than static retrieval databases, with multi-agent swarms acting as read–write transducers.\n\t\t- Key architectural pillars\n\t\t\t- Temporal and non-destructive graph maintenance\n\t\t\t\t- Systems attach assertion timestamps and validity intervals rather than mutating or overwriting nodes.\n\t\t\t\t- Reified evidence nodes store assertions alongside provenance pointers like source conversation, commit, and rationale.\n\t\t\t\t- State-of-the-art frameworks maintain continuous memory graphs that track state transitions across multi-hop reasoning paths.\n\t\t\t- Multi-agent division of labor\n\t\t\t\t- Intent and parsing agents convert unstructured human inputs into structured graph operations.\n\t\t\t\t- Graph traversal and subgraph fetchers execute multi-hop traversals to pull contextual clusters including decisions, backlinks, and evidence.\n\t\t\t\t- Synthesis and projection agents render retrieved subgraphs into target artifacts like Markdown runbooks, API specs, or Mermaid diagrams.\n\t\t- Spectrum of maturity across read–write capabilities\n\t\t\t- Deep read (provenance projection)\n\t\t\t\t- Uses subgraph extraction and multi-hop GraphRAG.\n\t\t\t\t- Reached high maturity in frameworks like Microsoft GraphRAG, Neo4j Agentic ADK, and HippoRAG.\n\t\t\t- Write ingestion (text to knowledge graph)\n\t\t\t\t- Uses schema-guided extraction with human-intent agents.\n\t\t\t\t- Reached medium–high maturity in systems like AGENTiGraph and LlamaIndex Property Graphs.\n\t\t\t- Advisory impact propagation\n\t\t\t\t- Uses dependency traversal flagging stale nodes or diff queues.\n\t\t\t\t- Reached medium maturity in temporal KG agents and reactive engines.\n\t\t\t- Bidirectional round-tripping\n\t\t\t\t- Edits a rendered view to update only delta nodes in the knowledge graph.\n\t\t\t\t- Remains an emerging bleeding-edge capability explored in moldable development and project lenses.\n\t\t- Open challenges on the bleeding edge\n\t\t\t- Lossless round-trip synchronization struggles to parse human edits on high-level summaries back into exact graph diffs without dropping unrendered metadata.\n\t\t\t- Granularity calibration frequently pulls too much AST-level noise or too little contextual rationale during subgraph extraction.\n\t\t\t- Human–agent advisory loops shift away from autonomous destructive overwrites toward advisory invalidation, generating visual diffs and triage queues for human verification.",
