  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Document Root",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG001"
    ],
    "parent": "ArtifactNode/00C7BJX82G000",
    "type": "root",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Interactive Title & Cross-Link Authoring — Design",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG002",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG004",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG008",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00C",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00N",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00P",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00T"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG000",
    "text": "**Implemented and live-verified.** `kg:title`/`kg:link` (`kgCli.ts`), `updateBlockNode` (`crud.ts`\n§2), and the `reconcileNode`/`resolveBlockLinks` persistence fix (§4/§7) are all live against the\nreal `aperas` DB — a title and a cross-link set via these commands both survived a real\nre-ingestion round-trip (confirmed via direct `doc get`, not just the tools' own success output).\nOne implementation-time bug found and fixed beyond what this doc anticipated: `rl.question()`\n(readline/promises) races against stream-end when real `await`s happen between prompts — piped,\nnon-interactive stdin (the coding-agent-driving-the-tool case this doc is built around) hit both\nan immediate throw and a silently-abandoned hung call depending on timing. Fixed by consuming the\nreadline interface's own async iterator instead of `.question()` — see `createLineReader` in\n`kgCli.ts`. Also found live: `graphql.ts`'s block field selection never included `links` at all,\nso `oldNode.links` was always `undefined` regardless of the `reconcileNode` fix — added `links {\n_id }` to `blockFieldSelection` plus a `normalizeLinks` step (mirroring the existing\n`normalizeProps`/`_json` pattern) to convert GraphQL's full-IRI `_id` down to the short ref-id\nstring form `updateDocument` expects.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 0. Why interactive, not an LLM API call",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG002",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG003"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG001",
    "text": "`Aperas-design.md`'s Development Roadmap (Phase 3, \"Preservation of manual skills &\nzero-background-API sub-agent execution\") already commits this project to a zero-background-API\nposture: no tool embeds a secondary LLM API key or spawns a background summarization service.\nStage 2 item 5 (`Aperas-fractal-ontology-implementation-plan.md` §Stage 2) was originally framed\nas \"an AI summarization agent... integrated\" during ingestion, but can be revised following the\nzero-API-key principle.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG003",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG003",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG002",
    "text": "The actual principle, restated for this feature: whoever is running the tool — a human, or a\ncoding agent that already *is* an LLM — already has the intelligence needed to write a title, or\nname a cross-link. So the tool just asks, interactively, instead of calling out to anything. This\ngeneralizes past titles: any future \"AI task\" in this codebase should be shaped the same way —\nturn it into a CLI prompt for input, not a backend call. Cross-links (`BlockNode.links`) are the\nsecond instance of this pattern covered here (§7) — the existing extraction is real but narrow\n(literal `[[wikilink]]` syntax only, `astParser.ts:92-106`), so plenty of genuine references a\nblock's prose makes never become a `Link` today unless the author happened to use that syntax.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. Scope",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG004",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG005",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG006",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG007"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG001",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG004/props/StringProp/qmgC53pTYXavL_DM",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG004/props/StringProp/qu9YMl3RJqoK9To4",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "Two independent gaps, same interaction shape:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG005",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG005",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG004",
    "text": "`BlockNode.title`'s fallback — replacing the naive `title = blockId` default (`astParser.ts` —\n  see `convertAstNode`'s default branch) for blocks that never got a real title. Heading blocks\n  are excluded by construction: their `title` is already the heading's own text (never the\n  blockId fallback), so there's nothing to prompt for.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG006",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG006",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG004",
    "text": "`BlockNode.links` — adding cross-links a block's prose actually makes but that wikilink syntax\n  extraction (`astParser.ts:92-106`) didn't catch. See §7.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG007",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG007",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG004",
    "text": "`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode.text` (the file/folder abstract, naive first-paragraph fallback per\n`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`) is explicitly **out of scope for this pass** — see §6.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. Shared write primitive (new — nothing like it exists today)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG008",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG009",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00A",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00B"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG001",
    "text": "Confirmed live: `crud.ts` has no update/patch function at all — its only write path is\n`insertAssertion`'s `client.addDocument(...)` (insert-only), plus the delete helpers. Nothing\nwraps the TerminusDB client's own `updateDocument`/`replaceDocument`, and both `kg:title` and\n`kg:link` need to change one field on an already-ingested `BlockNode` by id.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG009",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG009",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG008",
    "text": "One shared primitive covers both, `crud.ts`:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00A",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00A",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG008",
    "text": "```ts\nasync function updateBlockNode(\n  client, blockId: string, patch: Partial<Pick<BlockNode, \"title\" | \"links\">>\n): Promise<void>\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG008",
    "text": "Fetch the existing `BlockNode/<blockId>` document, shallow-merge `patch` onto it (for `links`,\nappend rather than replace — see §7), call the client's `updateDocument`/`replaceDocument` with\nthe merged full document (TerminusDB has no partial-field PATCH; a full-document replace is the\nonly write shape available, same as `artifacts.ts`'s existing per-`BlockNode` writes during\ningestion). Both `kg:title` and `kg:link` call this instead of re-running ingestion/reconciliation\njust to change one field on one already-identified document.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. `kg:title` command",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00C",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00F"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00C",
    "text": "```\nkg:title <path>              # just the single node at <path> (no descendants)\nkg:title <path> --recursive  # <path>'s full uniform tree, descendants included\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00E",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00E",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00C",
    "text": "`FolderNode`, `ArtifactNode`, and `BlockNode` aren't separate structural tiers with a boundary\nbetween them — they're all just nodes in one unbounded tree, and `kg:tree`'s own\n`childRefs`/`printTree` already walk all three through identical recursion with no kind-based\nbranching (`kgCli.ts:47-51,59` — `ArtifactNode`'s child is `root`, `FolderNode`/`BlockNode`'s are\n`children`, same call either way). So `--recursive` here isn't gating a boundary between kinds —\nit's the same uniform walk `kg:tree` does, just opt-in rather than opt-out. `kg:tree` can default\nto unbounded depth because it's read-only and printing more costs nothing; `kg:title`/`kg:link`\nare interactive, so defaulting to full depth would mean `kg:title .` could silently start a\nthousand-block prompt session by accident. `--recursive` is that opt-in, not a kind distinction.\nPass the artifacts root with `--recursive` to sweep everything in one run.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00F",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00G",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00H"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00C",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00F/props/StringProp/E7nEGaDsgBSP1EQi",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00F/props/StringProp/O1N5nedGTNOg8FYb",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "For each `BlockNode` matching the fallback sentinel (§4), print the block's raw content (its\n`text`, plus enough parent/path context to disambiguate) and read one line from stdin as the new\ntitle.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00G",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00G",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00F",
    "text": "**Empty input / EOF** → leave the existing fallback untouched, move to the next block. Matches\n  the original \"AI unavailable → ID fallback\" framing from `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`,\n  just with \"no operator input given\" standing in for \"AI unavailable.\"",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00H",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00F",
    "text": "**Non-empty input** → `updateBlockNode(client, blockId, { title })` (§2) immediately, then move\n  to the next block.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 4. Real bug this surfaces: titles don't survive re-ingestion (must fix first)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00J",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00K",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00M"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG001",
    "text": "Confirmed live in `reconcile.ts`: `reconcileNode` (`reconcile.ts:234-236`) only carries\n`newNode.blockId = oldNode.blockId` forward for a matched (content-unchanged) pair — every other\nfield, including `title`, comes from the fresh parse. `astParser.ts`'s `ingestArtifact` then\nwrites the whole reconciled tree back as one nested `ArtifactNode.root` document\n(`artifacts.ts:344-360`), a full per-`BlockNode` replace. Net effect: a title set via `kg:title`\ntoday would be silently wiped back to the `blockId` fallback on the artifact's next\n`kg:ingest`/`kg:track` re-run — the feature would appear to work once and then quietly regress.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00K",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00K",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00J",
    "text": "**Fix, in `reconcileNode`**: for a matched pair, also carry `newNode.title = oldNode.title`\nforward, not just `blockId`. This is safe unconditionally — \"matched\" already means\ncontent-equivalent, so for a heading (whose title *is* its content) `oldNode.title` and the\nfresh parse's title are identical anyway; for every other block, whatever real title exists\n(operator-set or still the ID fallback) is exactly what should persist.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00M",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00M",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00J",
    "text": "Same gap already exists for `unfolded` — currently preserved only on tombstoned nodes\n(`reconcile.ts:205`, inside `tombstoneSubtree`), never on matched ones, so a block's `unfolded`\nstate today already silently resets to `false` on every re-ingest. Not this feature's bug to\nown, but the `title` fix should carry `unfolded` forward the same way while touching this code,\nsince it's the identical class of gap with an identical fix shape.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. Scan criterion: what counts as \"not yet titled\"",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG001",
    "text": "`block.title === block.blockId || !block.title` — the exact fallback sentinel, *or* simply empty\n(covers a title later cleared by hand, or any future write path that leaves it blank without\ngoing through the fallback default). Heading blocks never match either condition (their title is\nalways their own non-empty heading text), so they're naturally skipped without any special-casing.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 6. Out of scope",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00P",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00Q"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00Q",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00Q",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00R",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00S"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00P",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00Q/props/StringProp/B6s8PTT4RixYPEJT",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00Q/props/StringProp/LrjLmFDGGYRzY_16",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- **`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode.text` abstract override.** The naive fallback there is \"first\n  paragraph,\" not a distinguishable sentinel like `title === blockId` — there's no cheap way to\n  tell \"still the naive fallback\" from \"someone's real prose that happens to start the file\"\n  without a marker. Doing this properly needs a fallback-provenance flag (the existing `props`\n  mechanism, e.g. `abstractIsFallback: true`, cleared once overridden) that isn't designed yet.\n  Deferred as a follow-up once `kg:title`'s narrower `BlockNode` case is live and the interaction\n  pattern is proven.\n- **Any automatic/bulk invocation.** Neither `kg:title` nor `kg:link` is ever called by\n  `kg:ingest`/`kg:track` — those stay exactly as fast and non-interactive as they are today.\n  These are deliberate, separate passes an operator runs when they actually want better titles\n  or richer cross-linking.",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00R",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00R",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00Q",
    "text": "**`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode.text` abstract override.** The naive fallback there is \"first\n  paragraph,\" not a distinguishable sentinel like `title === blockId` — there's no cheap way to\n  tell \"still the naive fallback\" from \"someone's real prose that happens to start the file\"\n  without a marker. Doing this properly needs a fallback-provenance flag (the existing `props`\n  mechanism, e.g. `abstractIsFallback: true`, cleared once overridden) that isn't designed yet.\n  Deferred as a follow-up once `kg:title`'s narrower `BlockNode` case is live and the interaction\n  pattern is proven.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00S",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00S",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00Q",
    "text": "**Any automatic/bulk invocation.** Neither `kg:title` nor `kg:link` is ever called by\n  `kg:ingest`/`kg:track` — those stay exactly as fast and non-interactive as they are today.\n  These are deliberate, separate passes an operator runs when they actually want better titles\n  or richer cross-linking.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 7. `kg:link` command",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00T",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00V",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00W",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00X",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00Y",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00Z"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00V",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00V",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00T",
    "text": "```\nkg:link <path>                       # just the single node at <path> (no descendants), if unlinked\nkg:link <path> --recursive           # <path>'s full uniform tree, unlinked BlockNodes only\nkg:link <path> --recursive --all     # <path>'s full uniform tree, every BlockNode\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00W",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00W",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00T",
    "text": "Same `--recursive` semantics and reasoning as `kg:title` §3 — an opt-in walk of the same uniform\ntree `kg:tree` already traverses, not a kind-based boundary; defaulting to full depth would risk\nan accidental thousand-block interactive session. `--all` is an independent filter on top of\nwhatever scope `--recursive` selects: skip already-linked blocks, or don't. `--all` without\n`--recursive` still means something (re-prompt `<path>` itself even if already linked), just a\nnarrow one — `<path>`'s own tree is only entered at all once `--recursive` is given.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00X",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00X",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00T",
    "text": "For each `BlockNode` in scope, print its raw content and loop reading target references from\nstdin — one per line, blank line ends this block's loop and advances to the next block. Each\nnon-blank line is resolved the same way `kg:assert`'s `source`/`target` already are\n(`Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md` §2: a full node id passed through directly, or a bare\nartifact/folder path resolved via `getArtifactRecord`/`getFolderRecord` — reusing that existing\nresolver rather than inventing a second one) and appended — not replacing existing entries — as\n`{ \"@type\": \"Link\", target, predicate: \"references\" }` via `updateBlockNode(client, blockId,\n{ links: [...existing, newLink] })` (§2).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00Y",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00Y",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00T",
    "text": "Unlike `title`, there's no fallback sentinel for \"still needs a link\" — an empty `links` array is\na legitimate, common state, not evidence of an unfilled default. Default scan skips any block\nthat already has at least one `Link` (on the assumption a first pass already looked at it); a\n`--all` flag re-prompts every block in scope regardless, for a deliberate second pass. Re-running\nwithout `--all` is therefore not idempotent-complete the way `kg:title` is — it's \"catch what's\nstill empty,\" not \"catch everything under-specified.\" Flagged here as a real asymmetry between\nthe two commands, not resolved further; revisit if it proves too coarse in practice.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZNKG00Z",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZNKG00Z",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZNKG00T",
    "text": "Same re-ingestion risk as §4 applies here too, structurally: `reconcileNode` doesn't carry\n`links` forward for matched pairs either (only `blockId`, confirmed live), and\n`resolveBlockLinks` (`artifacts.ts:266-284`) already doesn't diff against previous `links` on\nre-ingestion regardless (`artifacts.ts:260-264`'s own comment flags stale `Link` docs as a known\ngap). The §4 fix (carry fields forward for matched pairs in `reconcileNode`) should include\n`links` alongside `title`/`unfolded` while that code is being touched.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Document Root",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001"
    ],
    "parent": "ArtifactNode/00C7BJX8MR000",
    "type": "root",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Markdown → Fractal Tree Mapping — Design",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G002",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G003",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G007",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00B",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G011",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G017",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00W",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R018",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R019",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01A"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G000",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 0. Why this doc exists",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G002",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "text": "Markdown/mdast is a **loose** structure: node shapes vary per type (a `heading` carries its\ncontent differently from a `paragraph`; `list`/`listItem` nest oddly; whole categories of block\nexist that the parser doesn't even recognize without extra plugins), and remark itself parses\nheadings as flat siblings rather than a nesting construct. `BlockNode` is meant to be a **tight**,\nuniform structure with no fixed depth bound — the same three-part shape at every depth, described\nby `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5's \"Abstraction by Folding\": *\"an unbounded subtree folds\ninto an abstract (`text`), which folds into a semantic label (`title`), which folds into a hidden\n`@id`.\"* That's a claim of **uniformity** — every node, regardless of its mdast origin, is\nsupposed to look the same shape-wise. In practice, the mapping in `astParser.ts` has grown\nad hoc, one exception at a time (heading gets special title handling, containers get emptied\ntext, `ordered`/`start`/`checked` bolted on for `list`/`listItem` specifically). This doc is the\nsettled mapping: what `title`/`text`/`children` mean for every block type, uniformly, and an\nhonest audit of what's covered today versus silently isn't.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. `title`/`text` are core content — one consistent meaning, everywhere",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G003",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G004",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G005",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G006"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G003/props/StringProp/1B96w9Y7YqN-H2T3",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G003/props/StringProp/Lvq7Rm_E9G0acIVB",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "Both fields mean the same thing for every node, not a per-type special case:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G004",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G004",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G003",
    "text": "**`title`**: this node's own folded label. Meaningful today only where the source actually\n  supplies one (a `heading`'s literal heading line); everywhere else it's a `blockId` fallback,\n  standing in for \"nothing has summarized me yet\" (AI-driven title summarization is a separate,\n  unbuilt enhancement — `Aperas-dev-status.md`, \"Fractal Ontology Enhancements\"). That fallback\n  is itself meaningful signal (`Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md` §2), not a gap to hide.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G005",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G005",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G003",
    "text": "**`text`**: this node's own *immediate* content — not a cache, not a derived read-only summary\n  computed from elsewhere and bolted on after the fact. If a node has a `text`, that content is\n  *this node's*, the same way a `paragraph`'s `text` unquestionably belongs to it and to no\n  other node.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G006",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G006",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G003",
    "text": "That second point is the one worth stating plainly, because the code shipped earlier this\nsession violated it: `heading`/`listItem`'s `text` was computed via `extractAbstract(block)` —\na **copy** of whatever descendant paragraph happened to be found first, deep search included,\nwhile that same paragraph *also* remained, fully duplicated, as its own independent child\n`BlockNode`. That's inconsistent with every leaf type, where `text` is never a duplicate of\nsomething addressable elsewhere. §2 below supersedes that with a consuming (not copying) rule\nfor `heading`/`listItem` specifically, which is what makes `text` mean the same thing — *my own\ncontent, not a copy of someone else's* — across every node type uniformly.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. `heading` and `listItem`: equal, and *consume* their leading paragraph",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G007",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G008",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G009",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00A"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "text": "**Settled**: `heading` and `listItem` are structurally equal — both are \"the main block type\": a\nlabel (title, or its blockId-fallback equivalent) plus optional own content (`text`) plus\nstructural children. A `heading` is not special-cased relative to a `listItem`; the only\ndifference is that a heading's `title` happens to already be meaningful (the heading line) while\na listItem's isn't (no natural label).",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G008",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G008",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G007",
    "text": "**Consuming, not copying**: when a `heading` or `listItem` node's *immediate* first child (after\n`groupByHeadings`, for headings) is a `paragraph`, that paragraph's `text` becomes this node's\nown `text`, and the paragraph is **not** kept as a separate child — it's absorbed, one node\nfewer in the tree, not two nodes carrying identical content. If the first child isn't a\nparagraph (e.g. a heading immediately followed by a sub-heading, or a listItem containing only a\nnested list with no leading sentence — both legal Markdown), `text` stays empty; there's nothing\nof the node's own to consume, same as today.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G009",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G009",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G007",
    "text": "This is a **shallow** rule — the immediate child only, not a recursive search through nested\ncontainers. That's a deliberate change from the shipped `extractAbstract`-based version, which\ndug arbitrarily deep (through a `list`/`listItem` chain) to find *some* text to copy. Under the\nconsuming model, a heading whose first child is a `list` (not a `paragraph`) simply has no\n`text` of its own — its content genuinely lives only in its children, and that's an accurate\nstatement about the document, not a gap to paper over by reaching deeper for something to show.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00A",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00A",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G007",
    "text": "**What this supersedes**: an earlier `astParser.ts` version (`extractAbstract` reused for\n`heading`/`listItem`, called after `children` exist) was **not** this design — it was a copy, and\na deep one. **Implemented**: `convertChildren` now performs the shallow consuming version\ndescribed here instead.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. `blockquote`: decided — opaque leaf, structured content inside is an anti-pattern",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00B",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00C"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "text": "Settled: `blockquote` is a true opaque leaf, the same category as `code`/`table`/\n`thematicBreak`/`html` — `text = rawText` (the full raw slice, markers included, as the source\nwrote them) and **no children at all**, regardless of what's actually nested inside it. A\nblockquote *can* contain a full sub-tree in theory (nested lists, multiple paragraphs, further\nstructure) — but doing so is a discouraged anti-pattern, not something this ontology\ndecomposes. Authors should be warned against putting structured content inside a blockquote;\nthe system doesn't attempt to structurally model it either way.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00C",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00C",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00B",
    "text": "**Implemented and verified**: `astParser.ts`'s `convertAstNode` no longer recurses into a\n`blockquote`'s children at all (forced to `[]`, the same treatment `table`'s `tableRow`/\n`tableCell` already get by simply not being structural — here made explicit, since blockquote's\ninner content *would* otherwise recurse, using the same node types as everything else).\n`reconcile.ts`'s `blockquote` moved from `CONTAINER_TYPES` to `LEAF_TYPES` (it now has real\n`text` and no children, exactly like any other leaf — matched by content equality, not\ntype+position). `project.ts`'s `blockquote` case now reads `node.text` directly (strip-then-\nreapply `> ` markers, same normalization as before) instead of recursing into now-empty\nchildren. Verified: `verify:phase0 -- --db` clean (one fewer block per blockquote in the sample,\nas expected — its former paragraph child no longer exists as a separate node).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 4. Coverage audit: what's done, what's decided, what's still to discuss",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00D",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00K",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G010"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "text": "Checked empirically against the actual parser throughout, not assumed — including a second pass\nafter two follow-up questions (\"have you actually checked gfm's table support\" / \"what about\nbold and italic\") turned out not to have been verified as precisely as they should have been the\nfirst time.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Done (implemented and verified)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00E",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00F"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00D",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00F",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00G",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00H"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00E",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00F/props/StringProp/6QGzNjAgQ_VL34Zc",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00F/props/StringProp/PH-5n4ayvMj_0UZD",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- **`thematicBreak`/`html`**: both now in `astParser.ts`'s `isStructural`, treated as ordinary\n  leaves (`text = rawText`, same as `code`). Added to `reconcile.ts`'s `LEAF_TYPES` (otherwise\n  invisible to matching, showing as removed+added on every re-ingestion regardless of change) and\n  to `project.ts`'s leaf case (otherwise silently dropped again at projection time, having no\n  children to fall back on). Verified: `verify:phase0 -- --db` clean.\n- **`table`, and `remark-gfm` wired in for real**: `.use(remarkParse).use(remarkGfm)` in\n  `astParser.ts`'s pipeline. `table` added to `isStructural` as one more opaque leaf (no\n  per-row/per-cell decomposition — its children come out empty for free, since `tableRow`/\n  `tableCell` aren't structural either), plus the same `LEAF_TYPES`/`project.ts` additions as\n  above. Confirmed GFM's table support itself is complete (header row, all three alignment\n  forms, arbitrary rows/columns) before committing to the opaque treatment. **Side effect,\n  genuinely fixed**: task-list `checked` — previously always `null` regardless of source syntax,\n  since GFM task-list parsing needs `remark-gfm` too; `verifyPhase0.ts` now asserts the actual\n  value, not just round-trip shape. Verified: `verify:phase0 -- --db` clean, sample markdown\n  exercises a real aligned table and both checked/unchecked items.\n  **Not yet exercised against real content**: three real artifacts already contain genuine\n  tables (`Aperas-architecture.md`, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`,\n  `tdb-cli-status-walkthrough.md`), stored as opaque `paragraph`s from before this change —\n  `kg:track`'s hash-gate means they won't re-ingest until touched. Reasoned (not forced) that the\n  eventual `paragraph`→`table` type change reconciles cleanly rather than removed+added, since\n  `reconcile.ts`'s Stage A matches on `leafKey`'s string value only, never `.type`.",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00G",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00G",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00F",
    "text": "**`thematicBreak`/`html`**: both now in `astParser.ts`'s `isStructural`, treated as ordinary\n  leaves (`text = rawText`, same as `code`). Added to `reconcile.ts`'s `LEAF_TYPES` (otherwise\n  invisible to matching, showing as removed+added on every re-ingestion regardless of change) and\n  to `project.ts`'s leaf case (otherwise silently dropped again at projection time, having no\n  children to fall back on). Verified: `verify:phase0 -- --db` clean.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00H",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00F",
    "text": "**`table`, and `remark-gfm` wired in for real**: `.use(remarkParse).use(remarkGfm)` in\n  `astParser.ts`'s pipeline. `table` added to `isStructural` as one more opaque leaf (no\n  per-row/per-cell decomposition — its children come out empty for free, since `tableRow`/\n  `tableCell` aren't structural either), plus the same `LEAF_TYPES`/`project.ts` additions as\n  above. Confirmed GFM's table support itself is complete (header row, all three alignment\n  forms, arbitrary rows/columns) before committing to the opaque treatment. **Side effect,\n  genuinely fixed**: task-list `checked` — previously always `null` regardless of source syntax,\n  since GFM task-list parsing needs `remark-gfm` too; `verifyPhase0.ts` now asserts the actual\n  value, not just round-trip shape. Verified: `verify:phase0 -- --db` clean, sample markdown\n  exercises a real aligned table and both checked/unchecked items.\n  **Not yet exercised against real content**: three real artifacts already contain genuine\n  tables (`Aperas-architecture.md`, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`,\n  `tdb-cli-status-walkthrough.md`), stored as opaque `paragraph`s from before this change —\n  `kg:track`'s hash-gate means they won't re-ingest until touched. Reasoned (not forced) that the\n  eventual `paragraph`→`table` type change reconciles cleanly rather than removed+added, since\n  `reconcile.ts`'s Stage A matches on `leafKey`'s string value only, never `.type`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Decided — inline content is out of scope, permanently",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00J",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00D",
    "text": "Bold/italic/strikethrough/footnotes: none of these are block-level, none go in `isStructural`,\nnone get special-cased — confirmed correct today regardless (`**bold**`/`*italic*` are core\nCommonMark; strikethrough's literal text survives verbatim in the raw slice with or without\n`remark-gfm` either way, since this project never re-serializes inline content — every leaf's\n`text` is a raw source-slice, replayed byte-for-byte, independent of what remark's own AST\nrecognizes inline). This is a closed decision, not a gap — inline *structural* extraction of\naddressable spans is a different, separately-tracked concern (Fractal Ontology Enhancements).",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Implemented — `BlockNode.links` extraction",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00K",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00M",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00N",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00P",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00Q",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00R",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00Y",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00Z"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00D",
    "text": "Links are **not** in the \"permanently out of scope\" bucket above — unlike bold/italic/etc.,\nextracting them was an already-designed feature\n(`Aperas-fractal-ontology-implementation-plan.md` Stage 2 item 4; tracked in\n`Aperas-dev-status.md`'s \"Fractal Ontology Enhancements\"): inline links become `Link` objects\n(the concrete `BaseLink` leaf — `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.E, added alongside this\nwork, the same gap `Assertion` already closed for `BaseEdge`) in `BlockNode.links`, `Link.target`\npointing at a real node.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00M",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00M",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00K",
    "text": "**Scope, clarified this turn**: extraction isn't wikilink-specific — it applies to *any* link\nwhose target resolves to an internal node reference (a snowflake code), not just wikilink syntax\nused on its own. The deciding factor is what the target *is* (internal code vs. external URL).\nA link to an external URL never becomes a `BaseLink`; there's nothing internal to target.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00N",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00K",
    "text": "**Corrected, confirmed live**: `[[...]]` isn't a separate, competing link syntax needing its own\nplugin — it's how the *target* of an ordinary link is written to signal \"this is an internal\ncode, not a URL\": `[internal link]([[00C5H15NT0000]])`. Tested directly: plain `remark-parse`\n(no plugin, no `remark-gfm`) already parses this as a completely ordinary `link` mdast node —\n`{ url: \"[[00C5H15NT0000]]\", children: [{ type: \"text\", value: \"internal link\" }] }` — exactly\nthe same node shape as `[external](https://example.com)` (confirmed side-by-side in the same\ntest), just with a different-looking `url` string. So the parsing half is *entirely* free\nalready, for both forms — no wikilink plugin needed at all for this project's actual usage. An\nearlier revision of this doc wrongly assumed `[[...]]` would be used the Obsidian/Logseq way (as\na standalone replacement for `[text](url)`, tested in isolation as bare `[[Some Page]]`) and\nconcluded a plugin like `remark-wiki-link` was required — that conclusion doesn't apply to the\nform actually being designed here.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00P",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00P",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00K",
    "text": "**Implemented — walking mdast `link` nodes directly, as originally designed above, not a raw-text\nregex.** A first pass tried regexing the already-captured raw `title`/`text` slice directly for\n`\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\(\\[\\[([^\\])]+)\\]\\]\\)`, reasoning that every leaf's `title`/`text` is already a raw,\nbyte-for-byte source slice (§1) so a plain string match would find every occurrence uniformly.\n**That was wrong, confirmed live**: a plain string regex can't tell a real `link` node from an\n`inlineCode`/`code` span's literal contents — remark never re-parses backtick-wrapped text for\nnested constructs, but a raw-text regex doesn't know that boundary exists at all. This doc's own\nauthoring-convention example a few paragraphs up, `` `[title]([[code]])` `` (deliberately\nbacktick-wrapped, illustrating the syntax as inert prose), triggered exactly this false positive\non ingestion. Fixed by reverting to the originally-designed approach: `astParser.ts`'s\n`collectLinkCodes` walks the actual mdast (sub)tree recursively, matching only genuine `link`\nnodes' `url` field against `^\\[\\[(.+)\\]\\]$`, and never descending into an `inlineCode`/`code`\nnode at all — so backtick-wrapped example text stays correctly inert. Since this pure, DB-less\nparser can't resolve a code into a real node id, it only captures the raw codes onto an ephemeral\n`ParsedBlockNode.linkCodes` field (never written to the DB); `artifacts.ts`'s `resolveBlockLinks`\n(which has DB access) resolves each one at ingestion time and replaces it with a real\n`links: [{\"@type\": \"Link\", target, predicate: \"references\"}]` entry, deleting `linkCodes` before\nthe tree is written.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00Q",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00Q",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00K",
    "text": "**Authoring convention, settled — bare `[[code]]` never needs parser support at all.** The\ncanonical stored form is always `[title]([[code]])` — a human never has to hand-type that. The\n(unbuilt) Human UI is where a bare `[[code]]` — typed or pasted — gets auto-expanded into the\nfull `[title]([[code]])` form, filling in `title` for the human, before it's ever saved as\nMarkdown. This closes the wikilink question definitively: there is no scenario where the\nMarkdown *parser* needs to understand standalone `[[...]]` on its own — that form only exists\ntransiently as a UI input convenience, never in a saved file. A wikilink plugin genuinely isn't\nneeded, not now and not later, unless a future decision changes what gets *saved*.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00R",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00R",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00S",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00T",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00V",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00W",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00X"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00K",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00R/props/StringProp/GgPTRFN4Nz2i-qx1",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00R/props/StringProp/UnFb-q36zeYEFpni",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**Open questions, found on audit — resolved:**",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00S",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00S",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00R",
    "text": "**What does the inner code resolve against, and what about dangling references?** Resolved via\n  the same addressing scheme used elsewhere (`resolveDirectOrSnowflake`, `directResolve.ts`): a\n  full node id, or a bare snowflake code (tried as `BlockNode`, then `ArtifactNode`, then\n  `FolderNode` — Snowflake ids share one generation scheme, so a bare code alone doesn't say\n  which). Resolution is best-effort and non-fatal: an unresolvable code (typo, forward reference,\n  or a target genuinely gone) is skipped with a console warning, never fails the whole\n  artifact's ingestion — confirmed live with a deliberately-dangling `[[ZZZZZZZZZZZZZ]]` code.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00T",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00T",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00R",
    "text": "**`Link.predicate` value?** A fixed constant, `\"references\"` — not the link's visible text\n  (arbitrary, not a stable relation name); distinguishes structural inline links from\n  `Assertion`'s deliberately-chosen semantic predicates at query time.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00V",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00V",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00R",
    "text": "**Does extraction touch `text`?** No — confirmed as implemented: `text`/`title` stay the exact\n  raw slice, `links` is a purely additive index built from a *copy* of the matched substrings,\n  never a mutation of the leaf's own content.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00W",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00W",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00R",
    "text": "**Multiple links / `Set` ordering?** Left as `Set<Link>`, no ordering added — `links` remains a\n  lookup index only, `text` is always the rendering/reading-order source of truth.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00X",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00X",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00R",
    "text": "**Reconciliation/projection consequences?** None needed, confirmed (not just inferred) by the\n  live round-trip tests in `verifyPhase0.ts`: `text` is unaffected, so reconciliation and\n  projection both continue to work unchanged.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00Y",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00Y",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00K",
    "text": "**One real prerequisite gap found during implementation, not previously flagged even in this\naudit**: `BaseLink` is `@abstract`, and — unlike `BaseEdge`, which already had `Assertion` as its\none concrete leaf — nothing inherited directly from `BaseLink` was instantiable at all.\n`BlockNode.links: Set<BaseLink>` literally couldn't be written to until a concrete `Link` class\nwas added (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.E), mirroring `Assertion` exactly (no fields of\nits own, just makes the lineage instantiable).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G00Z",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G00Z",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00K",
    "text": "**A genuine reference cycle, discovered live while extending `verifyPhase0.ts`'s cleanup for\nthis feature**: a block whose `links` field points at a `Link`, which in turn `target`s some\nother block (a self-link makes this a literal cycle back to the same root) can't be cleaned up\nby deleting the `Link` and the blocks as separate sequential calls — each side fails as\nstill-referenced by the other. TerminusDB's referential-integrity-on-delete check only allows a\nreference from *outside* the set of documents being deleted in one call, so the fix is deleting\nthe `Link` ids together with the blocks that form the cycle, in one combined batch\n(`findLinkIdsTargeting`, `crud.ts`) — not a design change, but a real operational detail anyone\nwriting cleanup/deletion code touching `Link` needs to know.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Pointer",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G010",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G00D",
    "text": "YAML frontmatter — parsing shape confirmed, stage-appropriate design settled. See §5.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. YAML frontmatter as node props — opaque for now, extensible later",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G011",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G012",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G013",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G016"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "text": "**Scope: `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` both, decided.** A `README.md`'s own frontmatter (if it\nhas any) extracts into `FolderNode.props` via the exact same mechanism as an ordinary artifact's\nfrontmatter → `ArtifactNode.props` — not an `ArtifactNode`-only feature. This falls out for\nclose to free: `parseMarkdownTree`'s signature change (below) already has to touch `folders.ts`'s\nREADME call site regardless (it's the same function), so handling both node kinds is barely more\nwork than handling one.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G012",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G012",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G011",
    "text": "**Confirmed parsing shape**: `remark-frontmatter` (now installed, tested, not yet wired into\n`astParser.ts`) with `.use(remarkFrontmatter, ['yaml'])` recognizes a leading `---\\n...\\n---`\nblock as its own mdast node, `{ type: 'yaml', value: '<raw yaml body, delimiters stripped>' }` —\nconfirmed live against a real multi-key example. Critically, this only *isolates* the block; it\ndoes **not** parse the YAML into an object — `.value` is still just a string (e.g.\n`\"title: My Doc\\ntags: [foo, bar]\\ndraft: true\"`). Turning that into real key/value data needs a\nYAML parser too (e.g. the `yaml` or `js-yaml` package — neither installed yet).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G013",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G013",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G014",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G015"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G011",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G013/props/StringProp/PzIbN-b0eWPTcWAN",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G013/props/StringProp/sdzkiY_ssDhWKy_v",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**Shape of the change, not yet built**:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G014",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G014",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G013",
    "text": "Frontmatter is file-level metadata, not a block — it should never become a `BlockNode` at all\n  (parallel to how `folders.ts` already treats a directory's own `README.md` specially, never\n  exposing it as a separate `ArtifactNode`). `parseMarkdownTree`'s signature would need to\n  change: return the parsed props alongside the root `BlockNode`, not fold them into the tree.\n  That ripples into every caller — `artifacts.ts`'s `ingestArtifact` (→ `ArtifactNode.props`)\n  and `folders.ts`'s README handling (→ `FolderNode.props`, per the scope decision above), plus\n  `verifyPhase0.ts`.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G015",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G015",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G013",
    "text": "**Realized through §7's unified `props` mechanism, not a bespoke per-node-kind field.** An\n  earlier revision of this doc proposed a dedicated `ArtifactNode.props: Optional xsd:string`\n  field specifically for frontmatter. Superseded: §7 designs one generic `props` mechanism for\n  *any* node kind's extensible metadata, and frontmatter is simply one more user of it — a\n  single entry (e.g. `{key: \"frontmatter\", value: \"<raw yaml body, as-is>\"}`) in whichever\n  node's (`ArtifactNode` or `FolderNode`) own `props` list, not a field of its own. Still opaque\n  for this stage (the raw\n  body, not JSON-parsed — no YAML-parsing dependency needed yet), same reasoning as before, just\n  expressed through the shared mechanism instead of a one-off field.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G016",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G016",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G011",
    "text": "**Implemented**: §7's schema addition, `remark-frontmatter` wired into the pipeline, and\n`parseMarkdownTree`'s signature changed to `{root, frontmatter?}`, threaded through every caller\n(`artifacts.ts`'s `ingestArtifact`, `folders.ts`'s README handling, `verifyPhase0.ts`) to extract\nand return the frontmatter body separately from the `BlockNode` tree.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 6. README ingestion: same copy-not-consume gap, and no round-trip at all",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G017",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G018",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R000"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G017/props/StringProp/7MhSBXygoZy7H3Ru",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G017/props/StringProp/JRJbG078nIJJIy3y",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "Investigated directly, per a specific concern: was `FolderNode`'s README absorption actually\nbuilt the way it was designed — README's abstract *consumed* (not copied) into `FolderNode.text`,\nwith the parsed README content as the folder's own leading children? Re-reading `folders.ts`:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1G018",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1G018",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G017",
    "text": "**The same copy-not-consume gap §2 fixed for `heading`/`listItem` also existed here — now\n  fixed the same way.** `buildFolderTree` no longer copies via `extractAbstract`; it consumes:\n  when the README's parsed root's first child is a `paragraph`, that paragraph's `text` becomes\n  `FolderNode.text` and is dropped from `readmeChildren`, with any list *that paragraph had\n  itself adopted* (§8) promoted to `FolderNode`'s own leading children — the same consume-then-\n  adopt symmetry §8 already establishes for heading/listItem, applied one level up.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R000",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R001"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G017",
    "text": "**`FolderNode` → README.md projection — implemented.** `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A\n  names this as part of Artifact Projection: *\"For `FolderNode`s, the block children are\n  serialized back out to the folder's `README.md`.\"* `graphql.ts`'s new\n  `getFolderTreeViaGraphQL` and `project.ts`'s new `serializeFolderToReadme`/\n  `projectFolderToReadme` close this — `kg:project` now accepts either an artifact or a folder\n  path (tried in that order, same as everywhere else), and full round-trip is live-verified\n  (`verifyPhase0.ts` step 6b: project → write → re-ingest → project again reproduces the exact\n  same output, a stable fixed point).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R001",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R002",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R003",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R004",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R005",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R006"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R000",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R001/props/StringProp/VG5s2zB7bzf9vJyF",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R001/props/StringProp/Wq8o4botMpq1OmKn",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**Open questions, resolved:**",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R002",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R002",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R001",
    "text": "`FolderNode` isn't a `BlockNode`, so it has no mdast `type` for `serializeBlock`'s dispatch to\n    key on — but no wrapper was needed at all: `renderChildren` never switches on `node.type`\n    itself (only `serializeBlock`'s dispatch does), so `serializeFolderToReadme` just calls it\n    directly against the `FolderNode`, emitting `folder.text` (the consumed leading sentence)\n    ahead of it.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R003",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R003",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R001",
    "text": "Frontmatter round-trip: yes, for both kinds. A shared `withFrontmatter` helper in `project.ts`\n    reads `getProp(node, 'frontmatter')` and prepends a re-emitted `---\\n...\\n---\\n\\n` block —\n    fixing the same pre-existing gap in `ArtifactNode` projection at the same time (it never\n    re-emitted frontmatter either, until now).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R004",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R004",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R001",
    "text": "Read path: a small purpose-built `getFolderTreeViaGraphQL`, not a fully generic \"any node\"\n    abstraction. It uses the hybrid polymorphism pattern from §7's GraphQL findings: `_type`/\n    `_json` for the one polymorphic `FolderNode.children` hop, then reuses the *existing*\n    `fetchBlockSubtree`/truncation-refetch machinery (now exported from `graphql.ts`) for each\n    `BlockNode` child's own concrete subtree — no duplicated logic, and no need for the plain\n    Document API fallback originally guessed at here.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R005",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R005",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R001",
    "text": "**Corrected from the original proposal**: `kg:project` writes to disk **by default** for\n    both `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` (a `--dry-run` flag prints instead) — not print-only as\n    first proposed. `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §1.B is explicit that once a tree is\n    ingested, *\"the DB becomes the source of truth. Edits are made directly in the DB, and\n    artifact files are regenerated (projected) from it\"* — re-ingestion is named the *fallback*\n    path, only for hand-edits outside the DB. The original print-only proposal had this backwards\n    (an artifact of Phase 0 not yet having DB-native content-authoring tools, which made\n    file-editing the *practical* norm so far — not the intended end-state).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R006",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R006",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R001",
    "text": "`FolderNode.children` mixes the README's own `BlockNode`s with references to nested\n    `FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode` documents — `serializeFolderToReadme` filters `children` down to\n    `BlockNode`-typed entries (identified by the absence of a GraphQL `_type` tag, which\n    `getFolderTreeViaGraphQL` only attaches to the reference stubs it doesn't inline) before\n    calling `renderChildren`, exactly as proposed.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 7. `props`: one extensible mechanism, not one field per feature",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R008",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R009",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00A",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00B",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00H",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00K",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00M",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00N",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00P",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00T",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00V"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "text": "Settled direction, designed now (not deferred): a generic, per-node `props` mechanism, so a new\npiece of type-specific metadata never again needs its own schema field. `ordered`/`start`/\n`checked` (shipped earlier this session) are exactly the pattern being moved away from — three\nseparate fields, one per feature — and **all three migrate onto `props`**, not just the two list\nadoption forces to move (see §8: an earlier revision of this doc left `checked` in place since\nnothing *forces* it to move, but there's no good reason to leave the one remaining hardcoded\nper-feature field once everything else is uniform — consistency, not necessity, is the actual\nargument, and it's the stronger one).",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R008",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R008",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "text": "**Schema** (revised — abstract base + one concrete leaf, mirroring `BaseEdge`/`Assertion`):",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R009",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R009",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"Prop\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@abstract\": [],\n  \"@subdocument\": [],\n  \"key\": \"xsd:string\"\n}\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00A",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00A",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"StringProp\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@subdocument\": [],\n  \"@inherits\": [\"Prop\"],\n  \"value\": \"xsd:string\"\n}\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00B",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00C",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00F",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00G"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00B/props/StringProp/XSd1Q6m2WE7HndiV",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00B/props/StringProp/engkCJZaSazro1Z0",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- **`Prop` is abstract and carries only `key`** — deliberately, so `value`'s type is never fixed\n  at the mechanism's own level. This is the exact same pattern already used for `BaseEdge`\n  (abstract) → `Assertion` (the one concrete leaf, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.C-D): the\n  shared shape lives on the abstract parent, the concrete detail lives on whichever leaf\n  actually gets instantiated. Here it buys real freedom later — a future `BooleanProp` (`value:\n  xsd:boolean`) or `IntegerProp` (`value: xsd:integer`) could be added as another concrete leaf\n  of the same abstract `Prop`, genuinely WOQL-typed and queryable on `value` itself, without\n  touching `Prop` or anything already built on it — `BaseNode.props: Set<Prop>` is already\n  polymorphic over whatever concrete leaf a given prop actually is.\n- **For now, `StringProp` is the only concrete leaf, exactly as designed before the rename**:\n  `value` is a single `xsd:string`, JSON-encoded for anything that isn't naturally a string\n  (a boolean as `\"true\"`, a number as `\"3\"`, a list as `\"[\\\"foo\\\",\\\"bar\\\"]\"`), decoded at the\n  application layer. Every prop this doc actually needs today (`orderedList`, `startIndex`,\n  `checked`, frontmatter) uses `StringProp` — nothing forces reaching for a hypothetical typed\n  variant yet.\n- `@subdocument`, deliberately unlike `BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` — a prop is owned entirely by the one\n  node it belongs to, with no reification use case (nobody needs to assert something *about* a\n  prop the way `Assertion` lets you assert about an edge), so it doesn't need the global `@id`\n  `BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` specifically require for that (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2's \"No\n  Subdocuments\" rule doesn't apply here — that rule's reasoning was reification, which doesn't\n  apply to `Prop`).\n- `key` stays a plain top-level field regardless of which concrete leaf carries a given prop, so\n  \"find every node with a `checked` prop\" is a real, direct WOQL triple match either way; only\n  `value` (and only for `StringProp`) stays opaque to the query engine.\n- Added to `BaseNode` (not just `BlockNode`) as `props: { \"@type\": \"Set\", \"@class\": \"Prop\" }`\n  (polymorphic over `Prop`'s concrete leaves; `Optional` in effect via an empty `Set`, no\n  migration needed for existing documents) — so any node kind (`BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`,\n  `FolderNode`) can use the same mechanism, matching the user's own framing (\"ordered & start\n  for list, frontmatter for artifact\" — two different node kinds, one shared mechanism).",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00C",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00C",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00B",
    "text": "**`Prop` is abstract and carries only `key`** — deliberately, so `value`'s type is never fixed\n  at the mechanism's own level. This is the exact same pattern already used for `BaseEdge`\n  (abstract) → `Assertion` (the one concrete leaf, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.C-D): the\n  shared shape lives on the abstract parent, the concrete detail lives on whichever leaf\n  actually gets instantiated. Here it buys real freedom later — a future `BooleanProp` (`value:\n  xsd:boolean`) or `IntegerProp` (`value: xsd:integer`) could be added as another concrete leaf\n  of the same abstract `Prop`, genuinely WOQL-typed and queryable on `value` itself, without\n  touching `Prop` or anything already built on it — `BaseNode.props: Set<Prop>` is already\n  polymorphic over whatever concrete leaf a given prop actually is.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00B",
    "text": "**For now, `StringProp` is the only concrete leaf, exactly as designed before the rename**:\n  `value` is a single `xsd:string`, JSON-encoded for anything that isn't naturally a string\n  (a boolean as `\"true\"`, a number as `\"3\"`, a list as `\"[\\\"foo\\\",\\\"bar\\\"]\"`), decoded at the\n  application layer. Every prop this doc actually needs today (`orderedList`, `startIndex`,\n  `checked`, frontmatter) uses `StringProp` — nothing forces reaching for a hypothetical typed\n  variant yet.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00E",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00E",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00B",
    "text": "`@subdocument`, deliberately unlike `BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` — a prop is owned entirely by the one\n  node it belongs to, with no reification use case (nobody needs to assert something *about* a\n  prop the way `Assertion` lets you assert about an edge), so it doesn't need the global `@id`\n  `BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` specifically require for that (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2's \"No\n  Subdocuments\" rule doesn't apply here — that rule's reasoning was reification, which doesn't\n  apply to `Prop`).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00B",
    "text": "`key` stays a plain top-level field regardless of which concrete leaf carries a given prop, so\n  \"find every node with a `checked` prop\" is a real, direct WOQL triple match either way; only\n  `value` (and only for `StringProp`) stays opaque to the query engine.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00G",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00G",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00B",
    "text": "Added to `BaseNode` (not just `BlockNode`) as `props: { \"@type\": \"Set\", \"@class\": \"Prop\" }`\n  (polymorphic over `Prop`'s concrete leaves; `Optional` in effect via an empty `Set`, no\n  migration needed for existing documents) — so any node kind (`BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`,\n  `FolderNode`) can use the same mechanism, matching the user's own framing (\"ordered & start\n  for list, frontmatter for artifact\" — two different node kinds, one shared mechanism).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00H",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "text": "**Plain field vs. `props` — the actual boundary, stated explicitly.** This has been applied\ncase-by-case throughout this doc without ever being written down as a general rule: a plain\n`BaseNode`/`BlockNode` field (`blockId`, `type`, `title`, `text`, `children`, `unfolded`) is for\nsomething *every* node conceptually has, regardless of type — even a fallback/empty value is\nstill that concept, present universally. A `props` entry is for something only *some* node\n*types* have at all — `orderedList` means nothing on a `paragraph`, `checked` means nothing on a\n`heading`, frontmatter means nothing on a `BlockNode`. That's the actual test for \"does a new\nfield belong on the schema directly, or as a `props` entry\": is it universal (→ field) or\ntype-conditional (→ `props`)? `unfolded` staying a plain field (not migrating here) is exactly\nthis — every node has a fold state, so it's not a `props` candidate at all, unlike\n`orderedList`/`checked`/frontmatter, which are all conditional on the node being some specific\nkind of thing.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00J",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00J",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "text": "**\"Each type of block decides its own props\"** is enforced by convention in ingestion code\n(`astParser.ts` decides which keys a `list`/`listItem`/etc. populates), not by the schema —\nthe schema itself stays generic and open, consistent with this project's existing perimeter-\nboundary-transducer philosophy (type constraints belong to the code that populates a node, not\nto a rigid schema-level enum of allowed keys per type).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00K",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00K",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "text": "**`orderedList`/`startIndex` are this mechanism's first real user — decided in §8, not\ndeferred.** (Renamed from `ordered`/`start` — see §8.) An earlier revision of this doc treated\nmigrating these onto `props` as a single, indefinitely-deferred future item. Corrected: they\nmigrate now, as part of §8's list-adoption design — once a `list` node can dissolve, they need\nsomewhere to live that isn't a `list`-only field, and `props` is exactly that. `checked` joins\nthem (§8) for consistency, not because anything forces it to.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00M",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00M",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "text": "**Implemented**: schema addition (`Prop`/`StringProp`, `BaseNode.props`), a shared `getProp`/\n`setProp` helper (`props.ts`, used by `astParser.ts`, `project.ts`, `folders.ts`, `artifacts.ts`),\nand the full migration (§8/§9's projection mechanics, below). `StringProp` needs its own\n`@key: {\"@type\": \"Random\"}` (confirmed live — TerminusDB rejects a `@subdocument` class with no\nkey strategy at all, even though nothing outside the owning node ever needs to address a `Prop`\ndirectly); `Prop` itself stays keyless since it's `@abstract` and never instantiated.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00N",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "text": "**GraphQL representation, confirmed live — no inline fragment.** `props: Set<Prop>` can't be\nqueried as `props { key ... on StringProp { value } }`: TerminusDB's auto-generated GraphQL\nschema materializes `Prop` as its own concrete `OBJECT` type (confirmed via introspection —\n`possibleTypes: null`), not an interface/union `StringProp` implements, so `value` (a\n`StringProp`-only field) is simply absent from `Prop`'s GraphQL type — asking for it via a\nfragment errors (\"objects of type Prop can never be of type StringProp\"). **This is a TerminusDB\ncodegen gap, not a GraphQL spec limitation** — the spec fully supports exactly this pattern\n(an abstract/interface type plus `... on ConcreteType` inline fragments, `possibleTypes` naming\nits implementors); TerminusDB's schema generator just doesn't map `@abstract` classes to that\nconstruct at all. Checked TerminusDB's own GraphQL and schema reference docs for a flag or\nannotation that might enable it — neither mentions interfaces, unions, or `... on` anywhere. The\nonly documented related mechanism is `_type` (\"useful when a super-class is queried, as we can\nobtain what concrete subclass it corresponds to\") — confirming the intended pattern is inspect-\n`_type`-yourself, not spec-standard fragments. The fix is `_json`, an escape hatch every\ngenerated GraphQL type carries: `props { key _json }` returns each prop's full subdocument as a\nJSON string (`value` included), which `graphql.ts`'s `normalizeProps` parses back into the\nordinary `{key, value}` shape every other reader already expects. Anything reading `props`\nthrough GraphQL (only `graphql.ts`'s artifact-tree read path does — the CLI's `kg:tree`/\n`kg:unfold`/`kg:search` all use the plain Document API instead) needs this same `_json`-then-\nparse step; the plain Document API's own `getDocument` needs no such workaround — confirmed live,\nit already returns each prop's `key`/`value` directly, unmodified, since it's just returning the\nJSON-LD exactly as stored with no generated type-projection layer in between.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00P",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00P",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00Q",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00R",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00S"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00P/props/StringProp/-oAKj2Ok3Q5CTM6f",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00P/props/StringProp/zMcjqzr6dl2_swdn",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**This generalizes — confirmed via introspection on `BaseNode`/`BaseLink`/`BaseEdge`, not\n`Prop`-specific.** Every `@abstract` class in this schema, when it's the type of some field\n(`Set`/`List`/`Optional`), hits the identical restriction: its GraphQL object exposes only the\nfields defined *directly on that abstract class itself* — never a concrete leaf's own additional\nfields — with no fragment escape, only `_json`. Concretely, in this schema:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00Q",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00Q",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00P",
    "text": "`BaseNode` (fields: `links`, `props`, `tombstonedAt` only — not `blockId`/`title`/`text`/`path`,\n  which live on the concrete subclasses `BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00R",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00R",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00P",
    "text": "`BaseLink` (adds `predicate`, `target` — both defined on `BaseLink` itself, so these two work\n  fine without any fragment/`_json` workaround).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00S",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00S",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00P",
    "text": "`BaseEdge` (adds `source`, likewise defined on `BaseEdge` itself, also fine as-is).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00T",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00T",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "text": "Nothing currently queries `BaseNode.links: Set<BaseLink>` via GraphQL, so `source`/`target`/\n`predicate` being directly queryable there is moot today — but it will matter once `BlockNode.links`\nextraction (§4) ships and something needs to read it back. More immediately relevant:\n**`FolderNode.children: List<BaseNode>` has never been queried via GraphQL at all**, and per the\nrule above, a naive attempt would only ever surface `links`/`props`/`tombstonedAt` per child —\nnever the `blockId`/`title`/`text` (`BlockNode`), `path`/`artifactId` (`ArtifactNode`), or\n`folderId` (`FolderNode`) fields any real reader actually needs, since those all live on the\nconcrete subclasses, not `BaseNode`. This is exactly why `kgCli.ts`'s `kg:tree`/`kg:unfold`\ndeliberately read folder trees via the plain Document API (`client.getDocument`) instead of\nGraphQL — confirmed, now, to be a necessary choice rather than an incidental one.\n`ArtifactNode.root`/`BlockNode.children` are unaffected by any of this, since `BlockNode` itself\nis concrete, not abstract.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00V",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00V",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R007",
    "text": "**WOQL does not share this restriction — confirmed live, no workaround needed at all.** Unlike\nGraphQL's generated type system, `WOQL.triple` matches directly against the underlying RDF\ntriples, with no notion of \"which GraphQL object type exposes this field\" to get in the way.\nTested two ways against real data: (1) `WOQL.triple(v.Node, 'props', v.PropDoc)` →\n`WOQL.triple(v.PropDoc, 'key', v.Key)` → `WOQL.triple(v.PropDoc, 'value', v.Value)`, zero type\nconstraint, returned all 50 real `key`+`value` pairs directly — no `_json`, no fragment. Adding\nan explicit `WOQL.triple(v.PropDoc, 'rdf:type', '@schema:StringProp')` constraint changed nothing\n(same 50 bindings), confirming it's optional, not required. (2) The deeper case —\n`BaseLink → BaseEdge → Assertion`, two abstract levels rather than one — queried `source`/\n`predicate`/`target` off a real `Assertion` document with zero type constraint and got a clean\nmatch. So `queryNodeAssertions`/`traceImpactPropagation` (`woql.ts`) and `kg:search` (which also\nqueries `title`/`text` untyped across all three node classes at once, §5 of\n`Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md`) were already, unknowingly, relying on exactly this\nbehavior — it just happened to work because WOQL never needed the workaround GraphQL does.\nNet implication for the two features audited above: **any future `BlockNode.links` read path\nbuilt on WOQL (rather than GraphQL) needs no `_json`-style workaround at all**, regardless of how\ndeep the abstract hierarchy gets.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 8. List refactor: adoption instead of a standalone list block",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00W",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00X",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00Y",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00Z",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R010",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R011",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R012",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R013",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R014",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R017"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "text": "**The problem**: today, a `list` is always its own `BlockNode`, sitting between its logical\n\"owner\" (the paragraph/heading/listItem that introduces it) and its own `listItem` children —\nan artificial intermediate node the fractal-outliner model doesn't actually need in the common\ncase. Settled: a `list` should be **adopted** by whatever block immediately precedes it, when\nthat preceding sibling is a `paragraph`, `listItem`, or `heading` — its own `listItem`s become\ndirect children of that preceding sibling instead, and the `list` wrapper itself doesn't exist\nas a node at all. Only when no such preceding sibling exists (a list is the very first thing in\nits parent's children — e.g. the first content in a document, or the first thing inside a\nblockquote) does it remain an orphaned \"list block,\" exactly as today (no title, no text).",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00X",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00X",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00W",
    "text": "**Scope of \"immediately precedes,\" precisely**: the list's previous sibling *within its current\nparent's children, at the point `groupByHeadings` has already run* (so a heading's own nested\ncontent already sees its restructured, non-flat sibling list) — checked once per list, against\nthe original parse structure, not recomputed after other adoptions have already happened at the\nsame level.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00Y",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00Y",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00W",
    "text": "**Interaction with §2's consuming rule — explicit, not just implied.** `heading` and `listItem`\nare equal here too: when a list's immediate predecessor is exactly the leading paragraph that\n§2 just consumed into that heading/listItem's own `text`, the list adopts into the\n*consumer* — the heading/listItem itself — not into nothing. A consumed-away paragraph still\ncounts as a valid adoption anchor via whatever absorbed it; it doesn't make the list orphaned\njust because the specific node it originally sat next to no longer exists as its own entry in\n`children`. Concretely, for `## Heading\\n\\nIntro sentence.\\n\\n- item one\\n- item two`: the\nleading paragraph \"Intro sentence.\" consumes into `heading.text` per §2, and the list right\nafter it adopts into the *heading*, so `heading.children` becomes the two `listItem`s directly\n— no intermediate `list` node, exactly as if the heading were a `listItem` in the already-given\nnested example below. This is the same rule stated twice for two symmetric cases, not two\ndifferent rules — worth stating explicitly rather than leaving it to be inferred from only the\n`listItem` example, which is the ambiguity that prompted this clarification.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R00Z",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R00Z",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00W",
    "text": "This also covers the common nested-list case: `- Parent item\\n  - Nested item` today produces\n`listItem(\"Parent item\") → [paragraph, list → [listItem(\"Nested item\")]]`; under adoption, since\nthe nested list's immediately preceding sibling *is* `listItem`'s own leading paragraph (already\nconsumed into `listItem.text` per §2), the nested list's own `listItem`s become direct children\nof the outer `listItem` — no intermediate `list` node for the ordinary single-nested-list-\nunder-a-bullet case at all.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R010",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R010",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00W",
    "text": "**Two lists directly adjacent to each other — the second is orphaned, not merged into the\nfirst's adoption.** \"Immediately precedes\" is checked against the literal raw sibling right\nbefore a given list, and a `list` is never itself a valid anchor (only `paragraph`/`listItem`/\n`heading` are). So a bullet list directly followed by an ordered list — nothing but a blank line\nbetween them, a common Markdown pattern — does not have the second list adopt into whatever the\nfirst list adopted into: by the time the second list is reached, its own immediately-preceding\nraw sibling is the first `list` itself, not a valid anchor, so it stays its own orphaned node.\nThis isn't just tidy — a node can only ever host one list's `orderedList`/`startIndex` pair (see\nbelow), so letting a second, unrelated list merge into the same adoption would silently corrupt\none of the two lists' own numbering.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R011",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R011",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00W",
    "text": "**Renamed for clarity: `ordered`/`start` → `orderedList`/`startIndex`.** Once these can live on\na node that isn't itself a `list` (an adopting `heading`/`paragraph`/`listItem`), the old names\nread wrong — \"`ordered`\" on a heading's `props` doesn't say what it's describing. `orderedList`/\n`startIndex` read correctly regardless of which node kind carries them.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R012",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R012",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00W",
    "text": "**Where they live once the `list` node dissolves — decided.** `orderedList`/`startIndex` become\n`props` (§7) of whichever block *now functions as a list* — the adopting parent when adoption\nhappens, or the orphaned `list` block itself when it doesn't (uniform treatment, not a special\ncase for either side). This is `props`'s first real user, motivated directly by adoption needing\nsomewhere for these two fields to go once the `list` node that used to hold them can disappear.\nA node can only ever end up hosting *one* logical list's worth of adopted `listItem`s, never two\nunrelated ones needing different `orderedList`/`startIndex` values — because adoption is checked\nper-list against that specific list's own unique immediately-preceding sibling; two sibling\nlists can never share the same immediate predecessor (something — even just the first list\nitself — always separates them), so one set of `props` per node is always enough.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R013",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R013",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00W",
    "text": "**`checked` migrates too, for consistency, not necessity.** An earlier revision of this doc left\n`checked` on `listItem` as a plain field, reasoning that adoption never dissolves `listItem`\nnodes so nothing *forces* it to move. True, but incomplete: once `orderedList`/`startIndex` are\non `props`, leaving `checked` as the one remaining hardcoded per-feature field is inconsistent\nwith the whole point of §7 — one mechanism, not \"some metadata uses it, some doesn't.\" `checked`\nmoves too.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R014",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R014",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R015",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R016"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00W",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R014/props/StringProp/5JcGXUXZKTNcQ4AI",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R014/props/StringProp/v9PbQQIE9zqizuZY",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**Artifact Projection — designed, not just flagged.** Today, `serializeBlock`'s `list` case is\nthe only place list-marker/indentation logic lives, reading `node.ordered`/`node.start` directly\nand iterating `node.children` (all `listItem`s, homogeneous). Under adoption, that's no longer\nenough — a `heading`/`paragraph`/`listItem`'s own `children` can be a *mix* of ordinary blocks\nand a contiguous run of adopted `listItem`s. Proposed: generalize `renderChildren` (used by every\ncase, not just `list`) to scan its node's `children` for contiguous runs of `type === 'listItem'`\n— render each such run exactly like today's `list` case does (via `serializeListItem`, reading\n`orderedList`/`startIndex` from *this* node's own `props`, `checked` from each item's own\n`props`), and render everything else via the existing per-type dispatch, one block at a time.\nThis subsumes the old `list` case entirely rather than sitting alongside it: an orphaned `list`\nis just the special case where the *entire* `children` array is one such run — no separate case\nneeded. The scan checks every position rather than assuming the run is always last, but in\npractice a *mid-children* run is an anti-pattern, not a case worth designing around as if it\nwere normal:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R015",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R015",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R014",
    "text": "**`paragraph`**: structurally can't happen at all. A paragraph never has children of its own\n  except an adopted list — nothing else ever becomes a paragraph's child — so its `children` is\n  either empty or *entirely* one run. There's no \"after\" to worry about.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R016",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R016",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R014",
    "text": "**`heading`/`listItem`**: *can* have other genuine children (sub-headings, more prose) after\n  an adopted list, since these node kinds do have real structural children beyond what any list\n  contributes — but doing so is a discouraged anti-pattern: once a section's content becomes\n  a list, further prose at the same level reads better as a new section (a following heading)\n  than as more content trailing after a list under the same one. Worth a warning to authors, not\n  a reason to complicate the algorithm — the general \"scan every position\" design already\n  handles it correctly if it happens anyway, which is the right way round: correct by\n  construction for the exceptional case, without needing to special-case *for* it.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R017",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R017",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R00W",
    "text": "**Reconciliation — designed, and it turns out nothing needs to change.** `diffChildren` already\noperates generically on whatever `children` array it's handed — it doesn't know or care whether\na `listItem` used to live under a `list` wrapper. `listItem` is already in `CONTAINER_TYPES`\n(Stage B: matched by type and relative position within the segments Stage A's leaf-anchors\ndefine), and that classification is keyed purely on `.type`, unaffected by *whose* children array\na node sits in. So adopted `listItem`s mixed in among an adopting parent's other children match\nexactly the same way they already do today as a `list`'s own children — grouped by type within\nwhichever segment they fall into. No changes needed to `reconcile.ts` at all; this was flagged\nas an open risk in an earlier revision of this doc before actually re-reading `diffChildren`\nclosely enough to confirm it.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 9. Consequences for Artifact Projection — implemented",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R018",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "text": "§2's consuming rule required `project.ts`'s `serializeBlock` to change for `heading`/\n`serializeListItem` — under the old *copying* model, the leading paragraph was still there to\nrecurse into via `renderChildren`; under the *consuming* model it no longer exists as a child at\nall, so both cases now emit `node.text` explicitly before rendering the rest of `children`. This\nshipped in the same change as §2 itself, not after, since skipping it would have silently\ndropped the leading sentence from every projected document.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 10. Consequences for Reconciliation (a positive side effect, worth naming)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R019",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "text": "`reconcile.ts` matches `heading` by `.title` (Stage A) and `listItem` by type+position (Stage B)\n— neither key depends on the *value* of `.text`. So consuming the leading paragraph doesn't\nchange either node's own matching behavior. What it does change: today, that leading paragraph\nis a separate child, itself Stage-A-matched by *exact text equality* — editing just that\nsentence makes it fail to match (declined, shows as remove+add, per the reconciliation design's\n\"decline rather than guess\" principle) even though the heading/listItem around it is unchanged.\nOnce consumed, that same edit becomes an in-place field update on the already-matched\nheading/listItem (no separate node to fail-to-match) — arguably a more natural outcome (\"I\ntweaked the section's opening line\" reads as an edit, not a delete-and-recreate), but a real,\nworth-flagging shift in reconciliation stats for anything that edits leading sentences under\nheadings or list items.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 11. Status",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R01A",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01B",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01C",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01F"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1G001",
    "text": "**Implemented and verified**: §1 (uniform title/text semantics); §2 (heading/listItem consume,\nshallow — `convertChildren` in `astParser.ts`); §3 (blockquote, opaque leaf); §4's\n`thematicBreak`/`html`/`table` + `remark-gfm` wiring; §5 (frontmatter, extracted via\n`remark-frontmatter` and returned separately from `parseMarkdownTree` as `{root, frontmatter}`,\nrealized as one `props` entry on both `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode`); §6's first finding\n(the same copy-not-consume fix applied to `folders.ts`'s README absorption, including transferring\nany list the consumed leading paragraph had itself adopted up to the `FolderNode` itself); §7\n(the full `Prop`/`StringProp` schema, `BaseNode.props`, the shared `getProp`/`setProp` helper in\n`props.ts`); §8 (list adoption — including the heading/listItem consume-then-adopt symmetry, the\n`orderedList`/`startIndex` rename, and the two-adjacent-lists orphaning case found during\nimplementation); §9 (`project.ts`'s `serializeBlock`/`renderChildren`/`serializeListItem`\ngeneralized for consumed text and adopted-listItem runs, reading `orderedList`/`startIndex`/\n`checked` from `props`). `verify:phase0 -- --db` clean end-to-end (AST transduction, projection\nround-trip, live TerminusDB read/write via the new GraphQL `props`/`_json` path, reconciliation,\nFolderNode ingestion, Assertion CRUD); `npm run build` clean.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R01B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R01B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01A",
    "text": "**Known limitation, inherited from precedent, not new**: `kg:track`'s hash-gate means real\nartifacts already ingested before this change won't pick up the new heading/listItem text,\n`props`-based `orderedList`/`checked`, or frontmatter until something changes their file content\nand forces re-ingestion — the same limitation already disclosed for the earlier heading/listItem\nabstract fix (`Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md` §2) and the `table`/`remark-gfm` wiring\nabove. Not fixed here, by the same reasoning: parser-version isn't part of the change-detection\nkey, and forcing a bulk re-ingest is a separate, disruptive operation from a parser-logic fix.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R01C",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R01C",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01A",
    "text": "**Implemented and verified — Agentic Query Tools** (`Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md`):\n`kg:tree`, `kg:unfold`/`kg:fold`, `kg:search` all live in `kgCli.ts`, smoke-tested against the\nreal KG. `kg:unfold`/`kg:fold` work uniformly against any node kind for *display* (title/text of\nthe node and its immediate children), but only persist `unfolded` when the target is actually a\n`BlockNode` (the only kind the field exists on) — a no-op note, not an error, otherwise.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R01D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R01D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01A",
    "text": "**Implemented and verified — `BlockNode.links` extraction** (§4's last subsection): a new\nconcrete `Link` class (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.E — `BaseLink` was abstract with no\nconcrete leaf at all, a real prerequisite gap found while implementing this), `astParser.ts`'s\npure `extractLinkCodes` capturing raw `[[code]]` targets, `artifacts.ts`'s `resolveBlockLinks`\nresolving them at ingestion time (best-effort, `directResolve.ts`'s shared full-id/bare-snowflake\nresolution). `verify:phase0 -- --db` exercises both a resolving and a deliberately-dangling link.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R01E",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R01E",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01A",
    "text": "**Implemented and verified — `FolderNode` → `README.md` projection** (§6's second finding):\n`graphql.ts`'s `getFolderTreeViaGraphQL`, `project.ts`'s `serializeFolderToReadme`/\n`projectFolderToReadme`, `kgCli.ts`'s `kg:project` extended to accept a folder path and to\n**write to disk by default** (corrected from an earlier print-only proposal — see §6's resolved\nopen questions). `verify:phase0 -- --db` confirms the full project → write → re-ingest → project\ncycle is a stable fixed point.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZQ1R01F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZQ1R01F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZQ1R01A",
    "text": "**Deferred deliberately**: inline features (§4, bold/italic/strikethrough/footnotes) confirmed\npermanently out of scope — no future revisit implied.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Document Root",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX8000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8001"
    ],
    "parent": "ArtifactNode/00C7BJX980000",
    "type": "root",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Reconciliation Matching — Design",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX8001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8002",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8007",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8008",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800G",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800N"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8000",
    "text": "Re-ingesting an already-ingested artifact today just orphans its entire prior `BlockNode` tree. The strategy for doing better was settled in `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §1.B and Appendix F; this doc works out the algorithm that actually does it. The design below is settled; nothing has been implemented yet.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Settled already (prior to this doc)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX8002",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8003"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX8003",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX8003",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8004",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8005",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8006"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8002",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8003/props/StringProp/_fya3nnP-uDV8I3I",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8003/props/StringProp/sywBnx83NlEPOAjo",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- Reconciliation fires when `fileHash !== ingestedHash` — an edit landed on disk outside the DB (§1.B).\n- The approach is best-effort content matching over the whole tree, not a positional or hash-prefiltered pass — artifacts are bounded, human-document-sized trees, so there's no need to scope the search first (Appendix F).\n- A persisted, per-edit-maintained structural hash (`treeHash`) was considered and explicitly dropped, for both identity and reconciliation-scoping purposes (Appendix F).",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX8004",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX8004",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8003",
    "text": "Reconciliation fires when `fileHash !== ingestedHash` — an edit landed on disk outside the DB (§1.B).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX8005",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX8005",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8003",
    "text": "The approach is best-effort content matching over the whole tree, not a positional or hash-prefiltered pass — artifacts are bounded, human-document-sized trees, so there's no need to scope the search first (Appendix F).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX8006",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX8006",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8003",
    "text": "A persisted, per-edit-maintained structural hash (`treeHash`) was considered and explicitly dropped, for both identity and reconciliation-scoping purposes (Appendix F).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Blocking prerequisite: heading nesting must ship first",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX8007",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8001",
    "text": "`astParser.ts` currently treats `heading` as a flat block type — a document's headings and their following content all end up as siblings under the root, with no nesting by heading level (tracked in `Aperas-dev-status.md`). This has to be fixed *before* reconciliation goes live, not after: if reconciliation ships against today's flat shape and the heading fix lands later, every existing document reshapes at once (every block's parent changes), and the matcher will read that as \"everything changed,\" orphaning the whole graph in one re-ingest — precisely the outcome reconciliation exists to prevent. Lists (ordered and unordered) already nest correctly and aren't affected.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. The matching algorithm: Gestalt, not Myers",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX8008",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8009",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800A",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800B",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800C"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8001",
    "text": "Chosen deliberately over Myers/LCS-style diffing: Myers optimizes for the fewest edit operations (a machine-optimal cost function); Gestalt Pattern Matching (Ratcliff/Obershelp — the algorithm behind Python's `difflib.SequenceMatcher`) finds the longest matching contiguous run first, then recurses on the remainder either side of it. The two produce different notions of \"correct\" — Myers can produce confusing criss-cross matches around repeated content (the same reason git offers `--patience`/`--histogram` as alternatives to its default Myers diff), where what matters for reconciliation isn't a compact edit script, it's whether the match a human would recognize as \"the same block, edited\" is the one preserved. Natural-over-optimal is the right cost function here, since the actual consequence of a match is whether a `BaseLink`/`Assertion` stays attached to what a person would agree is the same content.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX8009",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX8009",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8008",
    "text": "Applied recursively, level by level: the root is always trivially matched (one root per `ArtifactNode`); for any matched parent pair, their `children` lists are diffed in two stages, then each matched child pair recurses into its own children.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800A",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800A",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8008",
    "text": "**Stage A — anchor on leaf content.** Filter each level's children down to content-bearing nodes (`heading`, `paragraph`, `code`) and run Gestalt on that subsequence. The comparison key is **heading XOR text**: a heading's own line when the node is a heading, its own `text` otherwise — never the placeholder `title` a non-heading block currently carries (which is just its own `blockId`, and would always differ between old and new by construction, silently defeating any matcher that touched it).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8008",
    "text": "**Stage B — anchor containers by context, not content.** Container-type nodes (`list`, `listItem`, `blockquote`) carry no reliable content of their own to compare (see the container-text gap below) — and don't need to. Their meaning comes from what's written around them (\"...with the following list:\", \"The quote above is...\"), so the leaf matches from Stage A partition both children lists into segments (before the first anchor, between each consecutive anchor pair, after the last); within a segment, containers align to their counterpart by type and relative position. No subtree signature, no hash, transient or otherwise — the surrounding matched prose is the anchor.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800C",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800C",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8008",
    "text": "**Gap to close alongside this**: `list`/`listItem` currently store the *entire subtree's* raw slice as their own `text` — confirmed live, e.g. a `listItem`'s `text` byte-for-byte duplicated one level down in its own paragraph child. Container-type nodes should carry no own `text` at all; content lives only in their leaf descendants. This is what makes Stage B's \"containers have no content to compare\" true by design rather than by omission.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. Duplicate/ambiguous leaves: let it go",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8001",
    "text": "If two leaf blocks with identical content can't be told apart by context (unanchored by anything on either side, the \"two Notes blocks\" case), decline to match rather than guess. Both old candidates get tombstoned, the new one mints a fresh id. Precision over recall — a wrong guess silently misattributes whatever pointed at the old block to its content-twin; declining just means the tombstone stands unresolved, which is visible and inspectable rather than silently wrong.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. Fate of unmatched old blocks: explicit tombstone",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800E",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800F"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8001",
    "text": "Not implicit (a document that just stops being referenced carries no signal it was ever a deliberate removal, versus an orphan-by-bug, versus mid-reconciliation limbo — that's not a weaker tombstone, it isn't one at all). An explicit `tombstonedAt` field, set when a node is determined to have no match: removed from whatever `children`/`root` list held it, but the document persists at its own `@id` — any edge still resolves, and can render \"removed on `<date>`\" instead of either dangling silently or reattaching to the wrong thing.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800E",
    "text": "Applies uniformly across `BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`, and `FolderNode` (see §4).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 4. Scope: the whole unbounded tree, and a prerequisite identity fix",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800G",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800H",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800K",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800M"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8001",
    "text": "Reconciliation covers `FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode` structural drift too, not just within-artifact `BlockNode` content — a file renamed or moved on disk is the same kind of event as a block moved to a different section, and gets the same treatment.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800H",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800G",
    "text": "This surfaced a real prerequisite: `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` were Lexical-keyed on `path`, an undesigned choice from an early prototyping pass, not a deliberated one. That's the same category error Appendix F already fixed once for `BlockNode` — identity conflated with a mutable property, content there, location here — and it means a rename is destructive by construction (the `@id` is recomputed from the new path, so the old identity, and everything that referenced it, breaks). **Resolved**: `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` get Snowflake-generated ids like `BlockNode`; `path` becomes an ordinary mutable field. A detected rename becomes a field update on the existing document, not a new identity — no tombstone-and-transplant needed for the artifact/folder itself (only `root`/tree-content ever needed transplanting, and only because the container around it couldn't survive a move under the old scheme). Full rationale in `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` Appendix G.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800J",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800J",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800G",
    "text": "This also means `ArtifactNode` needs `title`/`text` fields it didn't have before — the folding philosophy (§5) applied uniformly: full dir tree → README → abstract (`text`, first paragraph as a naive stand-in until real AI summarization exists) → filename (`title`).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800K",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800K",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800G",
    "text": "**Matching rule for files/folders — title first, abstract as fallback, not \"never trust the name.\"** Unlike a non-heading block's placeholder title (always meaningless, always differs), a filename is a real, human-authored, sticky signal — the same category as a heading's own text. So: match unchanged paths trivially (already free — `path` is a plain field now, so an unchanged path is an exact match with no reconciliation logic needed at all). For the leftover set only (an old path gone from disk, a new file with no tracked `ArtifactNode`), fall back to matching by `text` (abstract) similarity to detect a genuine rename — same \"let it go if ambiguous\" rule as leaf blocks. A rename is a real, deliberate edit (like changing a heading), not something to be blind to by default; it should just take a radical-enough change in the abstract to actually get missed.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800M",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800M",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800G",
    "text": "Cost check before committing: a `path`-filtered query replacing today's free direct-id lookup benchmarked *faster* (~7.5ms vs ~48ms at 50 documents) — not a cost at all. Losing the database-enforced path-uniqueness guarantee is real in shape (an application-level check-then-upsert race could in principle create duplicate paths) but not practically significant given this project's actual deployment — one local TerminusDB per machine, reconciled via fetch/push, not concurrent writers against a shared server.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. Visibility: report changed and moved, not just added/removed",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800N",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800P",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800Q",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800R",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800S",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800T"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX8001",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800N/props/StringProp/5CygPBl6hOsLeYqi",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800N/props/StringProp/hkSfC6yVZRs6lHjD",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "A wrong match is silent by nature — nothing errors, an edge just ends up pointing at different content than a human expects — so a reconciliation pass should produce a summary. Not just counts of added/removed: **changed** and **moved** are their own categories, and they're not separate machinery, they fall directly out of classifying each match's outcome, applied uniformly at every level of the fractal tree (`FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`BlockNode`):",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800P",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800P",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800N",
    "text": "same content, same position/path → unchanged",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800Q",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800Q",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800N",
    "text": "same content, different position/path → **moved**",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800R",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800R",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800N",
    "text": "different content, same identity → **changed**",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800S",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800S",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800N",
    "text": "no match → added (new) or removed (tombstoned)",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZRX800T",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZRX800T",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZRX800N",
    "text": "A renamed file is the `ArtifactNode`-level instance of the same operation as a block moved to a different section: an orphaned old path matched against an untracked new path by content similarity, one mechanism, three fractal layers.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Document Root",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G001"
    ],
    "parent": "ArtifactNode/00C7BJXATR000",
    "type": "root",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# TerminusDB CLI Status Walkthrough & Operational Reference",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G002",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G003",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G004",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G007",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00G",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00Q",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00T",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G015",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01G",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01P"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G000",
    "text": "Operational reference for checking status, commit logs, document contents, and remote state of the `aperas` database using the `tdb` CLI shortcut. For complete reference of TerminusDB CLI, see the [official documentation](https://terminusdb.org/docs/terminusdb-cli-commands/).",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G002",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G002",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G001",
    "text": "```bash\nalias tdb='docker exec terminusdb /app/terminusdb/terminusdb'\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G003",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G003",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G001",
    "text": "---",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. Database & Branch Verification",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G004",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G005",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G006"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G001",
    "text": "Confirm database presence and inspect active branches across all stored databases:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G005",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G005",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G004",
    "text": "```bash\ntdb db list --branches\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G006",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G006",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G004",
    "text": "---",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. Commit Log Inspection & Document Filtering",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G007",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G008",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00A",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00C"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Standard Branch Log",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G008",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G009"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G007",
    "text": "Inspect full commit history for the main branch:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G009",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G009",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G008",
    "text": "```bash\ntdb log admin/aperas | less\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Compact Local-Time History",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00A",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00B"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G007",
    "text": "Use `tdb-log.sh` (located in `Aperas/scripts/`) for local-time formatting and auto-paging. Output defaults to 20 commits (`--count` parameter is only needed when overriding, e.g., `--count 50` or `--count -1`):",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G00B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00A",
    "text": "```bash\nAperas/scripts/tdb-log.sh\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Document History Filtering",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00C",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00F"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G007",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G00D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00C",
    "text": "> **Performance Warning**: `tdb history --id=\"...\"` is measurably slow — over 6× slower than dumping the entire log (572 commits: `log --count -1` ~2.2s vs. `history` for one document ~13s). It's not walking an index of changed ids per commit; the exact mechanism wasn't confirmed, but the timing is consistent with checking the document's actual state at every commit along the way. Use message-filtering on `tdb log` or `tdb-log.sh` as the fast alternative:",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G00E",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00E",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00C",
    "text": "```bash\n# Fast message filter via tdb-log.sh\nAperas/scripts/tdb-log.sh --filter \"Aperas-dev-status.md\"\n\n# Fast JSON filter via raw CLI & jq\ntdb log admin/aperas --json --count -1 2>/dev/null \\\n  | jq '.[] | select(.message | contains(\"Aperas-dev-status.md\"))'\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G00F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00C",
    "text": "---",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. Substrate Document Inspection",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00G",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00H",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00M"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G001",
    "text": "Retrieve stored documents by JSON-LD class (`ArtifactNode`, `BlockNode`, `BaseLink`, `BaseEdge`):",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Raw JSONL Output",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00H",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00K"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00G",
    "text": "`tdb doc get` outputs raw JSONL (one JSON object per line) with UTC timestamps and literal `\\n`/`\\t` escape sequences:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G00J",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00J",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00H",
    "text": "```bash\ntdb doc get admin/aperas --type=\"ArtifactNode\" | jq | less\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G00K",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00K",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00H",
    "text": "> **Note**: Add `--as-list=true` to convert JSONL lines into a single JSON array for program analysis.",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Unescaped Local-Time View (`tdb-doc.sh`)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00M",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00N",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00P"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00G",
    "text": "Use `tdb-doc.sh` to unescape embedded line breaks into readable text and convert ISO UTC timestamps to local time:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G00N",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00M",
    "text": "```bash\nAperas/scripts/tdb-doc.sh --type=\"BlockNode\"\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G00P",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00P",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00M",
    "text": "---",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 4. Direct WOQL Graph Queries",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00Q",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00R",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00S"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G001",
    "text": "Execute arbitrary WOQL queries against the substrate:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G00R",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00R",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00Q",
    "text": "```bash\ntdb query admin/aperas \"t(X, 'docId', Y)\" --json\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G00S",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00S",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00Q",
    "text": "---",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. SSH-Tunneled Remotes",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00T",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00V",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00W",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00Y",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G010",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G012"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Transport Overview",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00V",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00T",
    "text": "TerminusDB's native remote commands (`push`/`pull`/`fetch`/`clone`) communicate exclusively over HTTP(S) REST APIs without native SSH transport support. TerminusDB itself listens on `0.0.0.0:6363` inside the container — the loopback-only restriction (`127.0.0.1:6363`) is this project's own `docker run -p` configuration, not a TerminusDB default. Either way, cross-machine communication between separate hosts requires an SSH local port forward tunnel here.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 1. Establish SSH Local Port Forwarding",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00W",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00X"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00T",
    "text": "Forward local port `6364` on the source host to port `6363` on the remote target host. Bind to `0.0.0.0` so the local Docker container network can reach the tunnel:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G00X",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00X",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00W",
    "text": "```bash\nssh -N -f -L 0.0.0.0:6364:localhost:6363 <user>@<remote-host>\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 2. Configure Host Firewall (UFW)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00Y",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00Z"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00T",
    "text": "Allow traffic from the Docker bridge subnet (`172.17.0.0/16`) to port `6364`:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G00Z",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G00Z",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00Y",
    "text": "```bash\nsudo ufw allow from 172.17.0.0/16 to any port 6364 proto tcp\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 3. Add Remote & Fetch Tracking Heads",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G010",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G011"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00T",
    "text": "Point the remote URL to the host's Docker bridge gateway (`172.17.0.1`):",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G011",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G011",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G010",
    "text": "```bash\n# Verify bridge gateway IP\ndocker network inspect bridge --format '{{(index .IPAM.Config 0).Gateway}}'\n\n# Register remote and fetch remote-tracking reference\ntdb remote add admin/aperas remote_node \"http://172.17.0.1:6364/admin/aperas\"\ntdb fetch admin/aperas --remote=remote_node --user=admin --password=<password>\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 4. Inspect Remote Tracking State Read-Only",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G012",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G013",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G014"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G00T",
    "text": "Inspect fetched remote branches before merging into local state:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G013",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G013",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G012",
    "text": "```bash\n# View remote commit log\ntdb log admin/aperas/remote_node/branch/main | less\n\n# View remote document content\nTERMINUSDB_DB_SPEC=\"admin/aperas/remote_node/branch/main\" Aperas/scripts/tdb-doc.sh --id=\"ArtifactNode/Aperas-dev-status.md\"\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G014",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G014",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G012",
    "text": "---",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 6. Reconciling Diverged Histories (`rebase`)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G015",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G016",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G018",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01C"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G001",
    "text": "`push`/`pull` only fast-forward — if the local and remote branches have both moved on from their common point, both fail with `no_common_history` rather than merging. `rebase` is the reconciliation path (see `skills/terminusdb/references/cli.md` §\"Merging\" for its general semantics: replays one side's divergent commits onto the other, new commit IDs, no merge commit).",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 6.1. `rebase`'s `FROM` argument rejects a remote-tracking spec directly",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G016",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G017"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G015",
    "text": "`terminusdb rebase TO FROM` silently falls back to printing usage (exit `0`, no error text) when `FROM` is a remote-tracking branch spec (`admin/aperas/remote_node/branch/main`) — it only accepts a local branch spec. Materialize the remote-tracking head as a local branch first:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G017",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G017",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G016",
    "text": "```bash\ntdb branch create admin/aperas/local/branch/from_remote --origin=admin/aperas/remote_node/branch/main\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 6.2. `rebase TO FROM`: `TO ← FROM` is the direction of the write",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G018",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G019",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01A",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01B"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G015",
    "text": "`TO` is the destination and gets overwritten; `FROM` is the source/base and stays untouched — same as git rebase (`rebase TO FROM` ≡ `git checkout TO && git rebase FROM`), see `skills/terminusdb/references/cli.md` §\"Merging\" for the full mapping. Direction matters for performance, not just correctness:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G019",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G019",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G018",
    "text": "Given a long-lived local `main` (1120 commits) and a short remote-derived branch (`from_remote`, 9 commits unique beyond the common ancestor), only one direction reconciles cleanly — put the branch you want *updated* as `TO`:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01A",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01A",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G018",
    "text": "```bash\n# TO=main (the branch that would be overwritten), FROM=from_remote (supplies the base).\n# Replays main's 1120 commits on top of from_remote's small base — hangs / spins CPU, kill and avoid:\n# tdb rebase admin/aperas admin/aperas/local/branch/from_remote\n\n# TO=from_remote (the branch that gets overwritten), FROM=main (supplies the base, untouched).\n# Forwards main's 1120 commits as the new base, then replays from_remote's own 9 unique\n# commits on top of it — completes quickly, no conflicts:\ntdb rebase admin/aperas/local/branch/from_remote admin/aperas\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G018",
    "text": "After the working direction, `from_remote` (`TO`) contains the full reconciled history (1129 commits); `main` (`FROM`) is untouched by the operation — promoting the reconciled branch into `main`, or force-syncing a remote, is covered in §7 below.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 6.3. Cleaning up the scratch branch",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01C",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01F"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G015",
    "text": "Once `from_remote` (or any working branch created for a reconciliation like this) has served its purpose — its history promoted into `main` via §7.1, or abandoned — delete it:",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01C",
    "text": "```bash\ntdb branch delete admin/aperas/local/branch/from_remote\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01E",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01E",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01C",
    "text": "Same `BRANCH_SPEC` shape `branch create`/`rebase` take (full `local/branch/<name>` path, not a bare db spec). This only removes the branch pointer, not any commits/layers still reachable from `main` or elsewhere — nothing reachable only from `from_remote` and nowhere else survives, same as any content-addressed VCS.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01C",
    "text": "---",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 7. Force-Syncing Branches (`reset` — no merge, discard-and-replace)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01G",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01H",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01K",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01M",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01N"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G001",
    "text": "`push`/`pull` have no `--force` flag (checked live via `--help`) — there's no wire-protocol way to make one side's branch simply overwrite the other's. The actual hard-reset primitive is `reset`, exactly like `git reset --hard <commit>`, and it's purely local: it only ever rewrites a branch ref on the store the CLI is currently talking to.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01H",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01G",
    "text": "```bash\ntdb reset BRANCH_SPEC COMMIT_OR_COMMIT_SPEC\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01J",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01J",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01G",
    "text": "Unlike §6's `rebase`, no intermediate local branch needed (like `rebase`'s `FROM`-argument) — `reset` takes a raw commit id, and any commit `fetch` has already pulled the layers for works directly, remote-tracking spec or not:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01K",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01K",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01G",
    "text": "```bash\ntdb fetch admin/aperas --remote=remote_node --user=admin --password=<password>\ntdb log admin/aperas/remote_node/branch/main | head -1              # note the tip commit id\ntdb reset admin/aperas/local/branch/main <remote-tracking-tip-commit-id>\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01M",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01M",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01G",
    "text": "**Reciprocal**: this recipe *is* both directions — \"forced pull\" (above) is just \"forced push\" run **from the other end**. We cannot do a forced push from this end, because `reset` is local-only.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01N",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01G",
    "text": "---",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 8. Backup & Recovery Operations",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01P",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01Q"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01Q",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01Q",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01R",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01S"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01P",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01Q/props/StringProp/8_rEjvunDjSmBVRP",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01Q/props/StringProp/DDkxDxHYSSN9swmW",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- **Whole-Volume Tarball (`Aperas/scripts/restore.sh backup-full | verify-full | restore-full`)**:\n  **Primary adopted backup & transfer mechanism**. Captures consistent cold snapshots of `terminusdb_storage` for cross-machine host transfers.\n- **Same-Store Snapshot (`Aperas/scripts/restore.sh backup | verify | restore`)**:\n  CLI `.bundle` exports are restricted to same-instance local rollbacks only (due to cross-store layer reference incompatibility issue #2509).",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01R",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01R",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01Q",
    "text": "**Whole-Volume Tarball (`Aperas/scripts/restore.sh backup-full | verify-full | restore-full`)**:\n  **Primary adopted backup & transfer mechanism**. Captures consistent cold snapshots of `terminusdb_storage` for cross-machine host transfers.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZT1G01S",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZT1G01S",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZT1G01Q",
    "text": "**Same-Store Snapshot (`Aperas/scripts/restore.sh backup | verify | restore`)**:\n  CLI `.bundle` exports are restricted to same-instance local rollbacks only (due to cross-store layer reference incompatibility issue #2509).",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Document Root",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF8000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8001"
    ],
    "parent": "ArtifactNode/00C7BJXCC8000",
    "type": "root",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Upstream issue: `bundle`/`unbundle` cannot move data between two independent stores",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF8001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8002",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8003"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8000",
    "text": "Filed against `terminusdb/terminusdb` as [#2509](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/2509) (2026-08-26). Related: [#1147](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/1147), [#1235](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/pull/1235).",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Title",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF8002",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8001",
    "text": "`bundle`/`unbundle` cannot move data between two independent stores — even on the same host (reproducible; related to #1147, #1235)",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Body",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF8003",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8004",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8006",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800E",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800H",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800R",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800S"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8001",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Summary",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF8004",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8005"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8003",
    "text": "`terminusdb bundle`/`terminusdb unbundle` (CLI) only round-trips correctly when the destination is the **same store** that produced the bundle (i.e. a different database within the same running server/storage directory). The moment the destination is a genuinely **different store** — a separate `store init`, even one created moments earlier from the identical Docker image, on the identical physical machine — `unbundle` deterministically fails with `unknown_layer_reference`.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF8005",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF8005",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8004",
    "text": "This is very likely the same underlying bug reported in #1147 (closed as not planned, unreproduced by maintainers) and investigated without success in #1235. I believe I've found the reason #1235's tests didn't catch it: every test in `tests/test/cli-bundle.js` calls `cli.store.init()` exactly once in the top-level `before()` hook and shares that single store across all three tests — so none of them ever exercise a bundle moving between two *different* stores, which is the one scenario that actually fails.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Environment",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF8006",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8007"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8003",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF8007",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF8007",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8008",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8009",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800A",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800B",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800C",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800D"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8006",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8007/props/StringProp/aPD-j3mPWDmaL0rO",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8007/props/StringProp/qt65w9Vr38diXdtn",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- `terminusdb/terminusdb-server:v12` (Docker image), pinned digest `sha256:385faf298ad77aaf2d4d6df5e84a4cbe3596d01dab2e3b991af905639ae56388`\n- `TerminusDB v12.0.7 (57f2093baeafd65e16004e84b7b58e0c5cf72858)`\n- `terminusdb-store v0.19.8`\n- Confirmed identical versions on two independent physical machines (originally reported as a cross-machine issue), **and** reproduced with two containers on a single machine (see below) — ruling out network/host/version/edition as factors.\n- Community edition (no license key). The only documented guarantee for moving a database between servers I could find (https://terminusdb.org/docs/enterprise-backup-restore/) is explicitly scoped to Enterprise edition and uses a different mechanism (`/api/bundle` + `/api/unbundle` REST endpoints, target database pre-created). This report is about the CLI `bundle`/`unbundle` commands in Community edition.\n- `auto-optimize` community plugin (probabilistic layer squash/GC) was disabled for all tests below (moved `/plugins/auto-optimize.pl` out and restarted) to rule it out as a factor. Made no difference.",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF8008",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF8008",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8007",
    "text": "`terminusdb/terminusdb-server:v12` (Docker image), pinned digest `sha256:385faf298ad77aaf2d4d6df5e84a4cbe3596d01dab2e3b991af905639ae56388`",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF8009",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF8009",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8007",
    "text": "`TerminusDB v12.0.7 (57f2093baeafd65e16004e84b7b58e0c5cf72858)`",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800A",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800A",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8007",
    "text": "`terminusdb-store v0.19.8`",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8007",
    "text": "Confirmed identical versions on two independent physical machines (originally reported as a cross-machine issue), **and** reproduced with two containers on a single machine (see below) — ruling out network/host/version/edition as factors.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800C",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800C",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8007",
    "text": "Community edition (no license key). The only documented guarantee for moving a database between servers I could find (https://terminusdb.org/docs/enterprise-backup-restore/) is explicitly scoped to Enterprise edition and uses a different mechanism (`/api/bundle` + `/api/unbundle` REST endpoints, target database pre-created). This report is about the CLI `bundle`/`unbundle` commands in Community edition.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8007",
    "text": "`auto-optimize` community plugin (probabilistic layer squash/GC) was disabled for all tests below (moved `/plugins/auto-optimize.pl` out and restarted) to rule it out as a factor. Made no difference.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Reproduction (single machine, two independent stores)",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800E",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800F",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800G"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8003",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800E",
    "text": "```bash\n# Store A: existing long-running instance\ndocker run -d --name tdb-a -p 6363:6363 -v tdb_storage_a:/app/terminusdb/storage terminusdb/terminusdb-server:v12\n\n# Store B: brand-new, independent instance, same image, same host\ndocker run -d --name tdb-b -p 6364:6363 -v tdb_storage_b:/app/terminusdb/storage terminusdb/terminusdb-server:v12\n\n# In store B: create a minimal scratch database\ndocker exec tdb-b terminusdb db create admin/test --schema=true\ndocker exec tdb-b terminusdb doc insert admin/test --graph-type=schema \\\n  --data='{\"@type\":\"Class\",\"@id\":\"Person\",\"@key\":{\"@type\":\"Random\"},\"name\":\"xsd:string\"}' -m \"schema\"\ndocker exec tdb-b terminusdb doc insert admin/test --data='{\"@type\":\"Person\",\"name\":\"Test1\"}' -m \"commit 1\"\ndocker exec tdb-b terminusdb bundle admin/test -o /tmp/test.bundle\ndocker cp tdb-b:/tmp/test.bundle ./test.bundle\n\n# Positive control: unbundle back into store B itself (a different db name, same store) — works\ndocker cp ./test.bundle tdb-b:/tmp/test.bundle\ndocker exec tdb-b terminusdb db create admin/test_verify\ndocker exec tdb-b terminusdb unbundle admin/test_verify /tmp/test.bundle\n# => \"Unbundle successful\"\n\n# The actual test: unbundle into store A (different store, same host, same image/version)\ndocker cp ./test.bundle tdb-a:/tmp/test.bundle\ndocker exec tdb-a terminusdb db create admin/test_verify\ndocker exec tdb-a terminusdb unbundle admin/test_verify /tmp/test.bundle\n# => Error: error(unknown_layer_reference(\"<hash>\"))\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800G",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800G",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800E",
    "text": "Result is 100% deterministic across repeated runs and across multiple independent bundle files (I tested this against three separate scratch databases with different content/commit counts, always the same failure mode, always a different specific missing-layer hash each time).",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### What this rules out",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800H",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800J"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8003",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800J",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800J",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800K",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800M",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800N",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800P",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800Q"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800H",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800J/props/StringProp/58D-k3mr6zTP5Kjx",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800J/props/StringProp/_8i-_AdDd0N9qzRp",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- **Version mismatch** — both stores run the exact same image digest.\n- **`auto-optimize` plugin** — disabled on both stores before testing; no change.\n- **Store \"age\"** — reproduced with a store that was `store init`'d seconds before the test, same as the destination.\n- **Network/transport corruption** — reproduced with both stores on one machine, files moved via `docker cp` only.\n- **Content-specific corruption** — reproduced with a minimal, freshly-created 6-commit scratch database, not just a large/organically-evolved one.",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800K",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800K",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800J",
    "text": "**Version mismatch** — both stores run the exact same image digest.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800M",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800M",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800J",
    "text": "**`auto-optimize` plugin** — disabled on both stores before testing; no change.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800N",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800J",
    "text": "**Store \"age\"** — reproduced with a store that was `store init`'d seconds before the test, same as the destination.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800P",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800P",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800J",
    "text": "**Network/transport corruption** — reproduced with both stores on one machine, files moved via `docker cp` only.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800Q",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800Q",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800J",
    "text": "**Content-specific corruption** — reproduced with a minimal, freshly-created 6-commit scratch database, not just a large/organically-evolved one.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### What still works",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800R",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8003",
    "text": "Bundling and unbundling **within the same store** (different database, same running server/storage directory) works reliably every time, including after forced/probabilistic layer squashes.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Ask",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800S",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800T",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800X"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF8003",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800T",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800T",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800V",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800W"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800S",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800T/props/StringProp/BwA6n2vPUEOVGcqc",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800T/props/StringProp/qujNJadqFBMwsbjT",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- Given #1235's tests never actually exercise the two-store case, would a PR adding that missing test case (spin up two `store.init()` stores, bundle from one, unbundle into the other) be welcome, to at least turn this into a real reproducible regression test?\n- Is cross-store portability of `bundle`/`unbundle` intended to work at all in Community edition, or is it only ever meant for same-store snapshot/restore (with genuine cross-server moves being an Enterprise-only capability, as the backup-restore docs suggest)? If the latter, it'd be worth stating explicitly in the CLI docs/help text, since \"a pack ... that can then be reconstituted\" (the current `bundle --help` description) reads as store-agnostic.",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800V",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800V",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800T",
    "text": "Given #1235's tests never actually exercise the two-store case, would a PR adding that missing test case (spin up two `store.init()` stores, bundle from one, unbundle into the other) be welcome, to at least turn this into a real reproducible regression test?",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800W",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800W",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800T",
    "text": "Is cross-store portability of `bundle`/`unbundle` intended to work at all in Community edition, or is it only ever meant for same-store snapshot/restore (with genuine cross-server moves being an Enterprise-only capability, as the backup-restore docs suggest)? If the latter, it'd be worth stating explicitly in the CLI docs/help text, since \"a pack ... that can then be reconstituted\" (the current `bundle --help` description) reads as store-agnostic.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZVF800X",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZVF800X",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZVF800S",
    "text": "Related: #1147, #1235",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Document Root",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR8000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8001"
    ],
    "parent": "ArtifactNode/00C7BJXDYG000",
    "type": "root",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Upstream issue: property-level `@unfold: true` is a no-op on `List` properties",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR8001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8002",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8000",
    "text": "Filed against `terminusdb/terminusdb` as [#2512](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/2512) (2026-08-29). Related: [#2321](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/pull/2321) (introduced field-level `@unfold`).",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Title",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR8002",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8001",
    "text": "Field-level `@unfold: true` is a no-op on `List`-typed properties (works on `Set`/`Optional`/`Cardinality`); likely a gap in #2321",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Body",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR8003",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8004",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8007",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8008",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8009",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800A",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800B",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800C",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800J",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800K",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800M",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800N",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800P",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800T",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800V",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800W",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800X",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800Y"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8001",
    "text": "**Describe the bug**\nProperty-level `\"@unfold\": true` (added in #2321) works correctly for `Optional`, `Set`, and `Cardinality` properties, but is silently ignored for `List` properties: the field is returned as plain id references, with no error or warning. Class-level `@unfoldable` on the target class does not have this problem, it correctly unfolds through `List` properties, including recursive self-referential ones.",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR8004",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR8004",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8005",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8006"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8004/props/StringProp/ikC4CbWms0scxcAj",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8004/props/StringProp/qhbKn17Py4qIuRlH",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "Docs describe `List` as supported, with the same example syntax as the other container types:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR8005",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR8005",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8004",
    "text": "https://terminusdb.org/docs/document-unfolding-reference/: table listing \"List: `{ \"@type\": \"List\", \"@class\": \"Customer\", \"@unfold\": true }`\" under \"works with all property type families\"",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR8006",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR8006",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8004",
    "text": "https://terminusdb.org/docs/schema-reference-guide/: \"Add `\"@unfold\": true` to any complex property definition (Optional, Set, Array, List, or Cardinality)\"",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR8007",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR8007",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "**To Reproduce**\nSteps to reproduce the behavior:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR8008",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR8008",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "```\ndocker exec tdb terminusdb db create admin/unfold_test --schema=true\ndocker exec tdb terminusdb doc insert admin/unfold_test --graph-type=schema --data='[\n  {\"@type\":\"Class\",\"@id\":\"Item\",\"@key\":{\"@type\":\"Lexical\",\"@fields\":[\"name\"]},\"name\":\"xsd:string\"},\n  {\"@type\":\"Class\",\"@id\":\"SetHolder\",\"@key\":{\"@type\":\"Lexical\",\"@fields\":[\"name\"]},\n   \"name\":\"xsd:string\",\"items\":{\"@type\":\"Set\",\"@class\":\"Item\",\"@unfold\":true}},\n  {\"@type\":\"Class\",\"@id\":\"ListHolder\",\"@key\":{\"@type\":\"Lexical\",\"@fields\":[\"name\"]},\n   \"name\":\"xsd:string\",\"items\":{\"@type\":\"List\",\"@class\":\"Item\",\"@unfold\":true}}\n]' -m \"schema\"\n\ndocker exec tdb terminusdb doc insert admin/unfold_test --data='[\n  {\"@type\":\"Item\",\"name\":\"i1\"},\n  {\"@type\":\"Item\",\"name\":\"i2\"},\n  {\"@type\":\"SetHolder\",\"name\":\"sh1\",\"items\":[\"Item/i1\",\"Item/i2\"]},\n  {\"@type\":\"ListHolder\",\"name\":\"lh1\",\"items\":[\"Item/i1\",\"Item/i2\"]}\n]' -m \"data\"\n\ndocker exec tdb terminusdb doc get admin/unfold_test --id=SetHolder/sh1\n# => items unfolds inline: \"items\":[{\"@id\":\"Item/i1\",\"@type\":\"Item\",\"name\":\"i1\"}, ...]\n\ndocker exec tdb terminusdb doc get admin/unfold_test --id=ListHolder/lh1\n# => items stays as bare refs: \"items\":[\"Item/i1\",\"Item/i2\"]   <-- @unfold:true had no effect\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR8009",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR8009",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "**Current behavior**\n`ListHolder.items` (a `List` with `@unfold: true`) is returned as bare id references. Confirmed this is independent of the request-level `unfold` query/CLI flag: passing `unfold=true` or `unfold=false` explicitly made no observable difference in either direction for either class, only the schema-level annotations (`@unfold`, `@unfoldable`) determine the outcome.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800A",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800A",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "**Expected behavior**\n`ListHolder.items` unfolds inline, the same as `SetHolder.items` does, matching the docs linked above.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800B",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800B",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "**What still works**\nMarking the target class `@unfoldable: []` (class-level) instead of the property `@unfold: true` correctly unfolds `List` properties, including through a self-referential recursive `List<Self>` (verified 3 levels deep, cycle-protected). So the container-type gap is specific to the property-level `@unfold` mechanism, not to unfolding-through-`List` in general.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800C",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800C",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800D",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800E"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800C/props/StringProp/ThsJ_7NSr32S9A9i",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800C/props/StringProp/UJO6OwPS04uu7aMx",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "**Info:**",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800D",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800D",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800C",
    "text": "OS: Ubuntu 26.04 LTS",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800E",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800E",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800F",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800G",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800H"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800C",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800E/props/StringProp/6eDFox6GzGjxBUmy",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800E/props/StringProp/nq-8BgR8VajdjqL7",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "text": "How did you run terminus-server: Using Docker directly",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800F",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800F",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800E",
    "text": "`terminusdb/terminusdb-server:v12` (Docker image)",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800G",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800G",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800E",
    "text": "`TerminusDB v12.0.7 (57f2093baeafd65e16004e84b7b58e0c5cf72858)`",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800H",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800H",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800E",
    "text": "`terminusdb-store v0.19.8`",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800J",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800J",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "**Additional context**\nTraced the actual root cause into `src/rust/terminusdb-community/src/doc/mod.rs`. For TerminusDB v12, a single-document `doc get` doesn't go through `json.pl`'s `get_document`/`get_document_` at all — it goes through the Rust foreign predicate `print_document_json`, which calls `DocumentContext::get_id_document`. That's where field-level `@unfold` is actually decided, via a `(parent_type_id, predicate_id)` pair (`unfold_pairs`) read off the *enclosing* stack frame:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800K",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800K",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "```rust\nlet field_level_unfold = match (parent_type_id, predicate_id) {\n    (Some(pt), Some(pr)) => self.unfold_pairs.contains(&(pt, pr)),\n    _ => false,\n};\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800M",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800M",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "`parent_type_id`/`predicate_id` come from `StackEntry::document_type_id()` / `current_predicate()`:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800N",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800N",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "```rust\nfn document_type_id(&self) -> Option<u64> {\n    match self {\n        Self::Document { type_id, .. } => *type_id,\n        _ => None,\n    }\n}\n\nfn current_predicate(&mut self) -> Option<u64> {\n    match self {\n        Self::Document { fields, .. } => fields.as_mut().and_then(|f| f.peek().map(|t| t.predicate)),\n        _ => None,\n    }\n}\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800P",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800P",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800Q",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800R",
      "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800S"
    ],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800P/props/StringProp/0n6wrwNOYRgvb1R0",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800P/props/StringProp/vRsLx_65TwunAyMl",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "text": "- `Set`/`Optional`/`Cardinality` values sit as direct triples on the document, so the enclosing frame is always `StackEntry::Document`, and both lookups succeed — this is why they work.\n- `List` and `Array` values instead sit behind an intermediate `StackEntry::List` / `StackEntry::Array` frame (the RDF Cons chain for `List`, the array's index triples for `Array`), pushed onto the traversal stack while the document's field is walked. Neither variant carries the parent document's type/predicate forward, so `document_type_id()`/`current_predicate()` fall through the wildcard arm above and return `None` for anything nested inside a `List` or `Array` — silently disabling field-level `@unfold` for their elements regardless of what the schema says.\n- Class-level `@unfoldable` keeps working regardless, because it's checked via `self.unfoldables.contains(&t.object)`, which only needs the target's own type, not the enclosing frame.",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800Q",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800Q",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800P",
    "text": "`Set`/`Optional`/`Cardinality` values sit as direct triples on the document, so the enclosing frame is always `StackEntry::Document`, and both lookups succeed — this is why they work.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800R",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800R",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800P",
    "text": "`List` and `Array` values instead sit behind an intermediate `StackEntry::List` / `StackEntry::Array` frame (the RDF Cons chain for `List`, the array's index triples for `Array`), pushed onto the traversal stack while the document's field is walked. Neither variant carries the parent document's type/predicate forward, so `document_type_id()`/`current_predicate()` fall through the wildcard arm above and return `None` for anything nested inside a `List` or `Array` — silently disabling field-level `@unfold` for their elements regardless of what the schema says.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800S",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800S",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800P",
    "text": "Class-level `@unfoldable` keeps working regardless, because it's checked via `self.unfoldables.contains(&t.object)`, which only needs the target's own type, not the enclosing frame.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800T",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800T",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "The PR's own test for `List` only asserts the schema round-trips the annotation, never that it actually unfolds at read time:",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800V",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800V",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "```javascript\nit('should accept @unfold on List property', async function () {\n  ...\n  const r2 = await document.get(agent, { query: { graph_type: 'schema', id: 'Sequence' } })\n  expect(r2.body.elements['@unfold']).to.equal(true)\n})\n```",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800W",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800W",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "That's why it passes CI regardless of whether unfolding actually happens at read time.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR800X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00C7BJZWR800X",
    "blockId": "00C7BJZWR800X",
    "children": [],
    "parent": "BlockNode/00C7BJZWR8003",
    "text": "**Fix**\nCapture the parent's `type_id`/`predicate_id` before pushing the `List`/`Array` frame and thread it through `StackEntry::List` and `ArrayStackEntry` so nested elements still match against `unfold_pairs`. Verified against a from-source build: the fix compiles, all existing Rust unit tests still pass, `cargo clippy` shows no new warnings, and new integration tests for `List` retrieval and a 2D `Array` case (added to `tests/test/field-level-unfold.js`, per the request in [the maintainer reply](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/2512#issuecomment-5463157961)) pass against a live server built from the fix.",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
